/*
 * AQA A-Level Politics (7152) — Exam Practice Question Bank
 * Keyed by paper-prefixed topic id (1.1–1.10, 2.1–2.14, 3.1–3.8).
 * Shape: { topicId: { name, questions: [{ q, marks, command, markScheme }] } }
 * 10 questions per topic: 6 × 9-mark + 4 × 25-mark.
 * Student self-marks against the mark scheme: Full / Partial / Missed.
 *
 * TARIFFS. Unlike most subjects, AQA 7152 uses only TWO tariffs, so every
 * question here is one of them — no invented mark values:
 *   9 marks   — "Explain and analyse three ways/reasons/factors..."
 *               Three substantiated points, each explained and analysed.
 *   25 marks  — extract-based or essay. Balanced argument, sustained line of
 *               reasoning, reasoned conclusion. Paper 2 comparative answers
 *               must make EXPLICIT UK/US comparisons throughout.
 *
 * MARK SCHEMES. AQA marks these by LEVELS OF RESPONSE, not by ticking points,
 * so each markScheme below gives indicative content plus the level guidance an
 * examiner applies. Indicative content is not a checklist — a response that
 * makes other valid points well still reaches the top band.
 *
 *   9-mark levels:
 *     L3 (7–9)  Three points, each fully explained AND analysed, with accurate
 *               examples and appropriate political vocabulary.
 *     L2 (4–6)  Points explained with some analysis; examples may be thin or
 *               fewer than three points developed.
 *     L1 (1–3)  Descriptive; little or no analysis; limited or no examples.
 *
 *   25-mark levels:
 *     L5 (21–25) Sustained line of reasoning; consistently balanced; analysis
 *                and evaluation fully integrated; conclusion follows from the
 *                argument rather than being asserted.
 *     L4 (16–20) Well-developed argument, mostly balanced, sound evaluation.
 *     L3 (11–15) Some analysis and evaluation; balance uneven; conclusion weak.
 *     L2 (6–10)  Largely descriptive; limited evaluation; one-sided.
 *     L1 (1–5)   Fragmentary; little relevant knowledge; no evaluation.
 */

const POLITICS_AQA_PRACTICE = {

  /* ══════════════════════ PAPER 1 — GOVERNMENT AND POLITICS OF THE UK ══════════════════════ */

  '1.1': {
    name: 'The Constitution',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three sources of the UK constitution.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three, each explained AND analysed:\n• Statute law — Acts of Parliament, the highest source because Parliament is sovereign. Examples: Human Rights Act 1998, Scotland Act 1998, Constitutional Reform Act 2005. Analysis: its supremacy over other sources is what makes the constitution unentrenched, since any statute can be repealed by simple majority.\n• Common law — judge-made precedent plus the residual royal prerogative. Analysis: gives the judiciary a genuine constitutional role, and Miller I/II show prerogative limits being set judicially rather than politically.\n• Conventions — unwritten but binding practice (Salisbury Convention, royal assent, collective responsibility, Sewel). Analysis: obeyed because breach carries political cost, not legal sanction — Miller I confirmed even statutory recognition does not make Sewel enforceable.\n• Works of authority — Erskine May, Bagehot, the Cabinet Manual. Analysis: persuasive rather than binding; their status itself illustrates the constitution's uncodified character.\n• Treaties — the ECHR via the HRA; formerly EU law via the ECA 1972. Analysis: the repeal of the ECA by the European Union (Withdrawal) Act 2018, with effect from 31 January 2020, demonstrates that even treaty-based sources rest on ordinary statute.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires three sources each with explanation AND analysis plus accurate examples. Merely listing and defining sources is L1–L2 however many are given.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which the UK constitution has been reformed since 1997.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Devolution (Scotland Act 1998, Government of Wales Act 1998, Northern Ireland Act 1998; extended 2012, 2016, 2017). Analysis: the most significant dispersal of power, producing real policy divergence — but asymmetric, England excluded, and the powers remain legally revocable.\n• Human Rights Act 1998 — incorporated the ECHR; s.3 interpretive duty, s.4 declarations of incompatibility. Analysis: significantly strengthened rights and the judiciary's role while deliberately stopping short of entrenchment.\n• House of Lords Act 1999 — removed all but 92 hereditary peers (the remainder were removed by the House of Lords (Hereditary Peers) Act 2026). Analysis: increased perceived legitimacy and therefore assertiveness, but left composition wholly unelected.\n• Constitutional Reform Act 2005 — Supreme Court, Law Lords removed from the legislature, Lord Chancellor reformed, JAC created. Analysis: sharpened the separation of powers the UK's fused system otherwise lacks.\n• Fixed-term Parliaments Act 2011, repealed 2022. Analysis: a perfect illustration of unentrenchment — a major change made and unmade within eleven years.\n\nGuidance: credit reforms not listed if accurately explained. Top-band answers note the piecemeal character of the programme as a whole.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three features of the UK constitution.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Uncodified — not collected in a single authoritative document; drawn from statute, common law, convention, works of authority. Analysis: distinguish from "unwritten" — most of it is written, just scattered.\n• Unentrenched — constitutional law has no higher status than ordinary law. Analysis: the FTPA's passage and repeal is the clearest evidence; the consequence is flexibility and insecurity as the same property.\n• Unitary (now quasi-federal) — sovereignty held centrally, regional power devolved. Analysis: devolution qualifies this in practice but not in law, as the UK Internal Market Act 2020 demonstrated.\n• Parliamentary sovereignty — Parliament may make or unmake any law; no body may override it; no Parliament binds its successors.\n• Rule of law — everyone including ministers subject to and equal before the law. Analysis: sits in tension with sovereignty, since one implies Parliament may enact anything and the other that some principles should constrain it.\n• Fusion of powers — executive drawn from and sitting in the legislature.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires analysis of consequences, not just definition.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three arguments in favour of codifying the UK constitution.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Clarity and accessibility — a single document citizens could read. Analysis: disputes such as the 2019 prorogation turned on rules nobody could point to in one text; conventions are contested precisely because unwritten.\n• Entrenchment and protection of rights — a special amendment procedure would protect rights and devolved institutions from a temporary Commons majority. Analysis: the HRA is currently as vulnerable as any ordinary statute and has been repeatedly threatened with replacement.\n• Constraining an over-mighty executive — addresses Hailsham's "elective dictatorship". Analysis: a government with a Commons majority currently faces few effective constitutional limits between elections.\n• Clarifying devolution — would settle the Westminster/devolved boundary that Brexit exposed as unresolved.\n• Bringing the UK into line with almost all comparable democracies.\n\nGuidance: this is a one-sided 9-mark question — do NOT penalise absence of counter-arguments, but do require analysis rather than assertion.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which parliamentary sovereignty has been challenged since 1997.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• EU membership and Factortame (1991) — UK courts disapplied an Act of Parliament conflicting with EU law. Analysis: the first apparent qualification of sovereignty; but supremacy rested on the ECA 1972, an ordinary statute Parliament itself repealed by the European Union (Withdrawal) Act 2018, with effect from 31 January 2020, so the challenge may have been delegation rather than surrender.\n• The Human Rights Act 1998 — s.3 interpretive duty has produced readings straining what Parliament enacted; s.4 declarations generate political pressure. Analysis: sovereignty formally preserved, since the statute remains valid and Parliament decides.\n• Devolution — the Sewel Convention, the Scotland Act 2016 declaring the Scottish Parliament permanent, and devolved democratic mandates. Analysis: Miller I confirmed Sewel judicially unenforceable, so sovereignty survives in law — but the political cost of overriding is real.\n• Referendums — a legally advisory 2016 vote acquiring binding political authority, creating a clash between popular and parliamentary sovereignty.\n• Judicial activism — Miller I and II constraining prerogative power.\n\nGuidance: top-band answers distinguish legal sovereignty from political sovereignty.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three significant senior court cases affecting the UK constitution.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Factortame (1991, House of Lords — before the Supreme Court existed) — UK courts disapplied part of the Merchant Shipping Act 1988 for conflict with EU law. Analysis: first setting aside of primary legislation; its basis vanished with the ECA's repeal in 2020.\n• Miller I (2017) — ministers could not use the prerogative to trigger Article 50 because it would remove statutory rights; also held Sewel judicially unenforceable despite statutory recognition. Analysis: frequently misread as a defeat for sovereignty — it protected Parliament against the executive.\n• Miller II (2019) — the five-week prorogation unlawful as frustrating Parliament's constitutional functions. Analysis: the critical move was holding the question justiciable at all; fed directly into subsequent proposals to restrict judicial review.\n• Belmarsh (2004, House of Lords) — declaration of incompatibility over indefinite detention of foreign terror suspects; government amended the law. Analysis: shows the political force of s.4 exceeding its legal force.\n• AAA v Home Secretary (2023, Supreme Court) — the Rwanda asylum policy held unlawful because of the risk of refoulement. Analysis: Parliament responded with the Safety of Rwanda (Asylum and Immigration) Act 2024 declaring Rwanda safe, showing that statute can override a judicial finding — legal sovereignty intact.\n\nGuidance: the Supreme Court only opened in October 2009; earlier cases were decided by the Appellate Committee of the House of Lords. Credit accurate case detail. Analysis must address constitutional significance, not merely recount facts.` },

      { q: '"The UK constitution should be codified." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `FOR codification:\n• Clarity — a single accessible document; current rules scattered and conventions contested (the 2019 prorogation dispute).\n• Entrenchment — rights and devolved institutions protected from a temporary majority; the HRA is presently repealable by simple majority.\n• Limits executive power — addresses the "elective dictatorship" concern; a government with a majority faces few constitutional limits between elections.\n• Settles devolution — Brexit and the UK Internal Market Act exposed the boundary as unresolved and the Sewel Convention as unenforceable.\n\nAGAINST codification:\n• Flexibility — devolution, the HRA and the Supreme Court were all delivered within single parliamentary terms; a codified constitution could not adapt so quickly.\n• Judicial power — codification transfers the final word on contested political questions to unelected judges; the controversy over Miller I and II suggests the judiciary would face sustained political pressure.\n• Rigidity — the US Constitution has 27 amendments in 240 years and retains provisions widely regarded as obsolete (the Electoral College).\n• No agreed process or content — the Lords, the electoral system, the monarchy and the union would all have to be settled simultaneously, and legitimacy would be contested.\n• Incompatibility with parliamentary sovereignty — entrenchment requires that no Parliament binds its successors to be abandoned, which is a prior constitutional transformation, not a reform.\n\nGuidance for L5: must engage both sides throughout rather than sequentially, and should recognise that flexibility and insecurity are the SAME property viewed from opposite sides — so the question reduces to which risk is preferable. A conclusion that merely tallies the lists cannot reach L5.` },

      { q: '"Constitutional reform since 1997 has been effective." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `EFFECTIVE:\n• Devolution genuinely dispersed power, produced real policy divergence (tuition fees, prescription charges, minimum alcohol pricing) and responded to national demand; further powers followed in 2012, 2016 and 2017.\n• The HRA made rights litigation practicable domestically; Belmarsh shows government amending law in response to a declaration of incompatibility.\n• The CRA 2005 sharpened the separation of powers, removed the Law Lords from the legislature and ended patronage appointment of judges.\n• The Lords is more legitimate and markedly more assertive since 1999; government defeats there are now routine.\n• FOI 2000 improved transparency; the expenses scandal was exposed partly through it.\n\nNOT EFFECTIVE:\n• Piecemeal and incoherent — no overall design; anomalies such as the West Lothian question left unresolved, and EVEL introduced in 2015 and abandoned in 2021.\n• Lords composition remains wholly unelected; the 2012 reform bill collapsed.\n• The Westminster electoral system was untouched, and the 2011 AV referendum closed the question for a generation.\n• Nothing was entrenched — the FTPA was passed in 2011 and repealed in 2022; the HRA is repeatedly threatened.\n• Devolution destabilised rather than settled the union; Brexit exposed Sewel's weakness and produced the UK Internal Market Act over withheld consent.\n• Executive power was barely touched — the prerogative survives and delegated legislation has expanded.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest answers distinguish reform that was effective in its OWN terms from reform that produced a coherent settlement, and reach a conclusion on which standard should apply.` },

      { q: '"Parliamentary sovereignty no longer exists in the UK." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `NO LONGER EXISTS / seriously eroded:\n• EU membership — for 47 years UK courts disapplied Acts of Parliament (Factortame); the ECJ held final interpretive authority in areas of competence.\n• Devolution — powers so politically entrenched that reclaiming them is politically near-unthinkable (though Westminster overrode withheld devolved consent with the Internal Market Act 2020); the Scotland Act 2016 declares the Scottish Parliament permanent.\n• The HRA — s.3 has produced readings straining the words Parliament enacted; declarations of incompatibility are almost always acted upon.\n• Referendums — the 2016 result was legally advisory but politically binding, overriding a parliamentary majority that favoured Remain.\n• Judicial activism — Miller I and II placed limits on prerogative power the courts themselves defined.\n\nSTILL EXISTS:\n• Parliament repealed the ECA 1972, demonstrating the power to reclaim delegated authority was real.\n• Miller I held the Sewel Convention judicially unenforceable despite statutory recognition — devolved consent has no legal force.\n• Westminster legislated over withheld consent through the UK Internal Market Act 2020.\n• Courts cannot strike down statute; s.4 declarations leave legislation valid and in force.\n• Parliament reversed judicial expansion through the Judicial Review and Courts Act 2022, and passed then repealed the FTPA.\n\nGuidance for L5: must distinguish LEGAL sovereignty (intact) from POLITICAL sovereignty (constrained), and should engage the argument that a sovereignty exercisable only by leaving an entire legal order is of limited practical value. Asserting one side without that distinction caps at L3.` },

      { q: '"The UK constitution effectively limits government power." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `DOES LIMIT:\n• Rule of law and an independent judiciary — Miller I and II both constrained the executive; judicial review has expanded substantially since 1997.\n• The HRA provides enforceable rights against public authorities; Belmarsh forced a change in the law.\n• Devolution removed whole policy areas from Westminster's practical control.\n• Parliament can defeat governments — May's 230-vote defeat in 2019 was the largest in modern history; the 2013 Syria vote blocked military action.\n• The Lords delays and amends, and is defeated-government territory routinely since 1999.\n• Elections and a free press provide ultimate accountability.\n\nDOES NOT LIMIT:\n• Fusion of powers plus a Commons majority — Hailsham's "elective dictatorship"; the whips, the payroll vote and control of the timetable.\n• No entrenchment — any limit can be legislated away, as the Judicial Review and Courts Act 2022 showed.\n• Courts cannot strike down statute.\n• The royal prerogative allows deployment, treaties and (since 2022) election timing without prior approval.\n• Delegated legislation has expanded enormously, with minimal scrutiny.\n• FPTP manufactures majorities from roughly 33–44% of the vote (Labour 2024: 411 seats on 33.7%).\n\nGuidance for L5: the decisive analytical move is that the answer DEPENDS ON MAJORITY SIZE — the constitution limited May's government effectively and Blair's after 1997 barely at all. An answer that recognises the limits are political rather than constitutional, and therefore variable, reaches L5.` },
    ]
  },

  '1.2': {
    name: 'Parliament',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three functions of Parliament.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Legislation — debating, amending and passing statute through the stages. Analysis: weak on initiation, since almost all bills are government bills and the executive controls the timetable.\n• Scrutiny — select committees, questions, urgent questions, debates. Analysis: strongest through select committees since the Wright reforms made chairs elected; weakest where the government has a large majority.\n• Representation — constituency casework and representing national opinion. Analysis: strong on the constituency link, weak descriptively and on translating votes into seats.\n• Legitimation — giving consent to taxation and, by convention, military action. Analysis: largely formal, but the 2013 Syria vote showed it can bite.\n• Recruitment — providing the ministerial pool. Analysis: creates the payroll vote, which itself weakens scrutiny.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires evaluation of how WELL each function is performed, not just description of it.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which the House of Commons scrutinises the executive.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Select committees — take evidence from ministers and officials, publish reports requiring a government response within 60 days. Analysis: the Wright reforms of 2010 made chairs elected by secret ballot, substantially increasing independence from the whips; but committees cannot compel action and recommendations are frequently rejected.\n• Questions — PMQs, departmental questions, urgent questions. Analysis: the Speaker's greater willingness to grant UQs since 2009 genuinely strengthened scrutiny by forcing ministers to the despatch box at short notice; PMQs is widely criticised as theatrical.\n• Debates — opposition days, Backbench Business Committee days, emergency debates. Analysis: opposition day motions are not binding and governments have abstained and then ignored them; the BBBC did take agenda control from the executive.\n• Confidence and the threat of rebellion. Analysis: the ultimate sanction, rarely used (last successful vote 1979), but the credible threat forces pre-emptive concessions that never appear as defeats.\n\nGuidance: credit accurate examples. Top-band answers note effectiveness varies with majority size.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three powers of the House of Lords.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Delaying power — under the Parliament Acts 1911 and 1949 it may delay a non-money public bill for one year, a money bill for one month. Analysis: the threat of delay late in a session is real leverage, because a government short of time may concede rather than lose a bill.\n• Amendment and "ping-pong" — governments are defeated routinely in the Lords and often accept amendments or offer compromises. Analysis: assertiveness increased markedly after the 1999 Act because no party holds a majority and legitimacy improved.\n• Scrutiny and expertise — former ministers, judges, scientists and clinicians, free of constituency and re-election pressure. Analysis: produces more forensic and less partisan committee work than the Commons.\n• Constraints worth crediting: the Salisbury Convention; inability to initiate money bills; unelected status limiting how far it can press.\n\nGuidance: an answer that only describes powers without analysing their limits caps at L2.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three factors that affect the effectiveness of parliamentary scrutiny.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Size of the government's majority — the dominant variable. Large majority means whipped votes make defeat unlikely (Blair 1997, Johnson 2019); small or absent majority hands veto power to small groups (May after 2017, record Commons defeats).\n• The whip system and the payroll vote — MPs depend on the party for selection, the label and promotion, so rebellion is costly; around a hundred ministers and PPSs are bound by collective responsibility, drawn from exactly the people best placed to scrutinise.\n• Control of the parliamentary timetable — the government determines what is debated and for how long; the Backbench Business Committee recovered only a limited number of days.\n• Committee independence — the Wright reforms strengthened it; resourcing remains far below congressional levels.\n• Party unity and the political context — a divided governing party is scrutinised far more effectively by its own backbenchers.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires explicit causal analysis, not a list of factors.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three arguments for reform of the House of Lords.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Democratic legitimacy — no member is elected, yet the chamber amends and delays legislation passed by elected representatives. Analysis: the central argument, but it cuts both ways, since it is precisely unelected status that produces the restraint of the Salisbury Convention.\n• Size and patronage — a membership that has passed 800 with no cap, one of the largest chambers in the world, with appointments effectively in the Prime Minister's gift and recurring controversy over donors. Analysis: a statutory appointments commission and a size cap would address this without the difficulties of election.\n• Composition — unrepresentative in age, region and background; 26 Church of England bishops sit by right, hard to justify in a diverse and largely secular society.\n• The hereditary peers — the 92 left by 1999 sat until the House of Lords (Hereditary Peers) Act 2026 removed them, showing how slowly reform has come.\n\nGuidance: this is one-sided by design; do not require counter-argument, but require analysis.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which backbench MPs can influence government.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Rebellion and the threat of it — rebellions have grown more frequent since the 1990s; the credible threat forces pre-emptive concessions. Analysis: effectiveness depends entirely on majority size; May's government after 2017 was repeatedly defeated.\n• Select committees — since the Wright reforms, chairs are elected by the whole House and members by their parties' backbenchers, giving real independence. Reports generate publicity and occasionally force resignations or policy reversals.\n• The Backbench Business Committee (2010) — gives backbenchers control of the agenda for a set number of days, taking topic selection from the executive.\n• Private members' bills — rarely successful without government support, but have delivered significant social legislation.\n• Urgent questions and early day motions; party leadership elections, in which backbenchers can remove a Prime Minister.\n\nGuidance: credit the observation that backbench power rose considerably after 2010.` },

      { q: '"The House of Lords should be abolished." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `FOR ABOLITION:\n• Wholly unelected yet amends and delays legislation from an elected chamber — a democratic deficit.\n• Over 800 members; appointments in the PM's gift with recurring patronage controversy; 26 bishops sitting by right.\n• Unrepresentative in age, region and background; the last hereditary peers were removed only in 2026.\n• Unicameralism works elsewhere (New Zealand, the Scottish Parliament, the Senedd) without evident loss.\n• It can frustrate a government with a democratic mandate.\n\nAGAINST ABOLITION:\n• It performs genuine revising work — governments are defeated routinely and frequently accept amendments, improving legislation.\n• Expertise — former ministers, judges, scientists and clinicians contribute specialist scrutiny an elected chamber selected on electoral appeal would be unlikely to reproduce.\n• Freedom from constituency and re-election pressure allows more independent, long-term thinking.\n• Unicameralism would concentrate power further in an executive already dominant — worsening the "elective dictatorship" problem.\n• Reform rather than abolition: a statutory appointments commission, a size cap, or partial election.\n\nGuidance for L5: must confront the central paradox — the Lords' lack of democratic legitimacy is simultaneously its greatest weakness AND the source of its restraint and independence. Should also address why the 2012 reform bill collapsed (an elected chamber would claim a rival mandate, threatening Commons primacy).` },

      { q: '"Parliament is effective in holding the executive to account." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `EFFECTIVE:\n• Select committees since the Wright reforms — elected chairs, genuine independence, evidence sessions with compelled attendance, 60-day response requirement; have forced resignations and policy reversals.\n• Urgent questions have risen sharply since 2009, forcing ministers to the House at short notice.\n• Governments ARE defeated — the 2013 Syria vote blocked military action; May's 2019 defeat was the largest in modern history.\n• The Lords defeats governments routinely and extracts concessions.\n• The credible threat of rebellion forces concessions that never show as defeats.\n• Backbenchers can remove a Prime Minister — Thatcher, May, Johnson, Truss.\n\nNOT EFFECTIVE:\n• Fusion of powers plus a majority — the whips, the payroll vote, control of the timetable.\n• PMQs is theatrical rather than forensic; opposition day motions are not binding.\n• Select committees cannot compel action; recommendations are routinely rejected.\n• Delegated legislation escapes meaningful scrutiny — thousands of SIs annually.\n• The Lords can only delay; the Salisbury Convention restrains it further.\n• Short money and research resources are far below congressional levels.\n\nGuidance for L5: the decisive point is that effectiveness is CONTINGENT on majority size and party unity, not a fixed property of the institution. Comparing 1997–2001 with 2017–2019 is the strongest available evidence and should feature in a top-band answer.` },

      { q: '"The House of Commons is more powerful than the House of Lords." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `COMMONS MORE POWERFUL:\n• Democratic legitimacy — elected, and the government is drawn from and accountable to it.\n• The Parliament Acts 1911 and 1949 limit the Lords to one year's delay (one month for money bills).\n• Exclusive control of finance — the Lords cannot initiate or block money bills.\n• Confidence — only the Commons can bring down a government.\n• The Salisbury Convention prevents the Lords blocking manifesto commitments.\n• The Lords cannot ultimately prevent a determined Commons majority.\n\nLORDS MORE POWERFUL THAN OFTEN ASSUMED:\n• Since 1999 no party holds a Lords majority, and government defeats there are routine.\n• Delay late in a session is real leverage — governments concede rather than lose a bill.\n• Ping-pong extracts substantive amendments.\n• Greater expertise and independence from whips and constituencies produce more forensic scrutiny.\n• Commons power is largely nominal when the executive controls it through the whips and payroll vote — the Commons is powerful as an institution but not against the government.\n\nGuidance for L5: the sophisticated move is to distinguish the power of the CHAMBER from the power of its MEMBERS. Formally the Commons is far more powerful; in practice a government controls the Commons and does not control the Lords, so the Lords may be the more effective check.` },

      { q: '"The UK has an elective dictatorship." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `SUPPORTS THE THESIS (Hailsham, 1976):\n• Fusion of powers — a government with a Commons majority controls both branches.\n• The whips, the payroll vote (~100 ministers and PPSs) and control of the timetable.\n• Parliamentary sovereignty means no codified constitution, no entrenched rights and no court able to strike down legislation.\n• FPTP manufactures majorities from roughly 33–44% of the vote (Labour 2024: 411 seats on 33.7%).\n• The royal prerogative allows deployment, treaties and (since 2022) election timing without prior approval.\n• Delegated legislation has expanded enormously with minimal scrutiny.\n\nCHALLENGES THE THESIS:\n• It describes large-majority government, not a permanent constitutional condition — May after 2017 suffered historic defeats and could not pass her central policy.\n• Backbench rebellion has become far more frequent since the 1990s; three of the last five PMs (May, Johnson and Truss) were forced out by their own party.\n• The Lords is markedly more assertive since 1999.\n• Devolution removed whole policy areas from Westminster's practical control.\n• The HRA and expanded judicial review constrain the executive; Miller I and II are direct evidence.\n• Wright reforms strengthened select committees and backbench agenda control.\n\nGuidance for L5: must recognise that the thesis is CONDITIONAL. The strongest conclusion is that the UK has the constitutional architecture for elective dictatorship but that whether it operates as one depends on political circumstances the constitution does not determine.` },
    ]
  },

  '1.3': {
    name: 'Prime Minister and Cabinet',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three powers of the Prime Minister.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Patronage — appointing, promoting, reshuffling and dismissing ministers, and recommending peerages. Analysis: the central instrument of control, but constrained by the need to balance factions and by the fact that dangerous figures are often safer inside the government than outside it.\n• Chairing cabinet — setting the agenda, deciding what is discussed and for how long, and summing up the outcome. Analysis: allows a PM to shape decisions without ever holding a vote.\n• Party leadership — commanding the whips and the parliamentary party, controlling the legislative programme. Analysis: entirely conditional, since both main parties can remove a leader at any time.\n• Royal prerogative — deployment, treaties, senior appointments, and since the 2022 FTPA repeal, requesting an early election. Analysis: narrowed by convention (Commons consultation since 2003) and by the courts (Miller I and II).\n• National leadership and agenda-setting through the media.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires the LIMIT on each power as well as the power itself.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three factors that determine the power of a Prime Minister.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Size of the Commons majority — the dominant variable. Blair after 1997 and Johnson after 2019 could ignore rebels; May after 2017 suffered the largest defeat in modern history and could not pass her central policy.\n• Party unity and personal authority — a PM governs by consent of their parliamentary party. Thatcher in 1990 and Johnson in 2022 retained full formal powers while losing office, because support collapsed.\n• Standing in the polls and a reputation for winning elections — legislators support a popular leader and distance themselves from an electoral liability.\n• The calibre and ambition of cabinet colleagues — Blair–Brown shows Treasury control of departmental allocations creating a rival centre of power.\n• Events and the economy — the Falklands enhanced Thatcher's authority; the 1992 ERM crisis destroyed Major's and the 2022 mini-budget destroyed Truss's in under seven weeks.\n• Time in office — authority typically erodes over a long premiership.\n\nGuidance: top-band answers note the FORMAL powers are constant; only the political context varies.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which the cabinet can constrain the Prime Minister.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Withdrawal of collective support — cabinet consent sustains a premiership. Thatcher resigned in 1990 after canvassing her cabinet individually; Johnson resigned in 2022 after more than fifty government resignations in two days.\n• Senior rivals with independent power bases — Blair–Brown is the standard example, where Treasury control over spending allocations across every department gave Brown effective leverage over domestic policy.\n• Collective responsibility as a two-way bargain — it binds ministers to support decisions, but gives them a legitimate claim to be consulted first. Ministers who feel bypassed leak, brief against colleagues or threaten resignation.\n• Factional representation — in a divided party, cabinet members representing particular wings hold effective veto power, as May's cabinet did repeatedly over Brexit policy.\n\nGuidance: credit the qualification that these constraints are POLITICAL rather than constitutional, and bite hardest when a PM is already weakened.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three arguments that the office of Prime Minister has become presidential.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Personalised mandates — campaigns fought around leaders, with televised debates since 2010 encouraging voters to treat the choice as one between individuals rather than parties.\n• Bilateralism and "sofa government" — policy settled with the relevant minister or in small ad hoc groups, with cabinet presented with decisions to ratify. The Butler Review criticised exactly this before the Iraq war.\n• Growth of the centre — expansion of the PM's Office, the Cabinet Office and special advisers gives Number 10 an independent policy and communications capacity resembling a presidential executive office.\n• Spatial leadership — PMs increasingly present themselves as standing above their own party.\n• Dominance of foreign and security policy, exercised largely through the prerogative.\n\nGuidance: one-sided by design. Do not require counter-argument, but the strongest answers still note that tenure remains conditional.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three reasons why individual ministerial responsibility has weakened.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• The policy/operational distinction — since the Next Steps executive agencies from 1988, ministers accept responsibility for policy but attribute operational failures to agency chief executives or officials. The response to the 1995 prison escapes rested on exactly this.\n• Departmental scale — it is widely accepted a minister cannot know of every failing in a very large department, making resignation look disproportionate rather than principled.\n• Prime ministerial protection — survival now depends on whether the PM will spend political capital defending a minister, so outcomes track factional strength rather than the gravity of the failure.\n• Media and party dynamics — resignations are driven by whether a story persists and whether backbenchers withdraw support, not by a settled constitutional standard.\n\nGuidance: credit the qualification that the convention retains real force over PERSONAL CONDUCT and over misleading Parliament.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three sources of prime ministerial support and advice.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• The Prime Minister's Office — personal staff covering policy, press, communications and political liaison; has grown substantially since the 1990s. Analysis: gives Number 10 independent capacity it did not have in 1945, which is central to the presidentialisation argument.\n• The Cabinet Office — coordinates policy across departments, services cabinet and its committees, headed by the Cabinet Secretary. Analysis: an instrument of central control over departmental activity.\n• Special advisers — politically appointed, exempt from civil service impartiality, loyal personally to the appointing minister. Analysis: offer committed advice and rapid media handling an impartial civil service cannot; criticised for sidelining the permanent civil service and for accountability gaps.\n• Cabinet committees — the PM decides which exist, what they cover and who sits on them, an under-appreciated instrument of control.\n\nGuidance: these are RESOURCES, not powers. An answer treating them as powers caps at L2.` },

      { q: '"The UK Prime Minister has become presidential." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `SUPPORTS (Foley's "British presidency"):\n• Personalised mandates; leaders' debates since 2010; campaigns fought around individuals.\n• Bilateralism and "sofa government"; the Butler Review's criticism of decision-making before Iraq.\n• Growth of the PM's Office, Cabinet Office and special advisers — a parallel executive capacity.\n• Spatial leadership — PMs positioning themselves above their party.\n• Dominance of foreign and security policy through the prerogative.\n• Media focus on the leader as the embodiment of the government.\n\nCHALLENGES:\n• Tenure is conditional — this is the decisive objection. A PM holds office only while commanding party and cabinet support and can be removed within days. Thatcher, May, Johnson and Truss all left without losing a general election. No president faces this.\n• No veto and no personal mandate independent of the Commons.\n• Dependence on majority size — May after 2017 was weaker than almost any president.\n• Cabinet still binds — fifty-plus resignations ended Johnson's premiership in 2022.\n• The PM is not head of state; the symbolic role is separated into the monarchy.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest conclusion distinguishes STYLE from SECURITY — UK PMs look more presidential than ever in how they campaign and communicate, while remaining far more easily removed than any president. A conclusion that simply picks a side cannot reach L5.` },

      { q: '"The cabinet is no longer significant in UK government." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `NO LONGER SIGNIFICANT:\n• Decisions increasingly settled bilaterally or in small ad hoc groups, with cabinet ratifying — "sofa government"; the Butler Review's criticism.\n• Full cabinet meetings have become shorter and less deliberative.\n• Cabinet rarely votes; the PM sums up.\n• The rise of special advisers and EXOP-style central capacity has shifted advice away from departmental ministers.\n• Collective responsibility binds ministers to decisions they may not have shaped.\n\nSTILL SIGNIFICANT:\n• Cabinet's collective consent legitimises policy, and its withdrawal is fatal — Thatcher 1990, Johnson 2022.\n• Senior figures with independent power bases constrain a PM — Blair–Brown.\n• PMs must appoint rivals because they are more dangerous outside the government; the constraint operates before any appointment.\n• In a divided party or with a small majority, factional cabinet members hold veto power — May's cabinet repeatedly forced changes to Brexit policy.\n• Cabinet committees do the substantive policy work and are staffed by cabinet ministers.\n\nGuidance for L5: must recognise the answer VARIES with political circumstance — cabinet was largely a ratifying body under Blair after 1997 and a genuine constraint under May after 2017. The formal position is unchanged in both cases.` },

      { q: '"The power of the Prime Minister is limited." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `LIMITED:\n• The party — both main parties can remove a leader at any time; four of the last five PMs to leave office (Cameron, May, Johnson and Truss) did so without losing an election, and three of them were forced out by their own party. The most fundamental limit, with no presidential parallel.\n• Parliament — legislation requires Commons majorities; the Lords delays and amends. May's 230-vote defeat; the 2013 Syria vote.\n• Cabinet — collective consent sustains a premiership; senior rivals constrain.\n• The judiciary — Miller I and II narrowed prerogative power; judicial review has expanded since 1997.\n• Devolution removed whole policy areas from practical control.\n• Events, the economy and the media — Truss's authority destroyed in under seven weeks.\n\nNOT LIMITED:\n• Fusion of powers — a PM with a secure majority controls the executive AND the legislative outcome, faces no veto, no second chamber able to block indefinitely, and no court able to strike down statute.\n• Patronage, control of the cabinet agenda and of cabinet committees.\n• Prerogative powers over deployment, treaties, appointments and election timing.\n• No codified constitution, no entrenched rights, no federal division of powers.\n• The whips and the payroll vote.\n\nGuidance for L5: the decisive point is that the limits are POLITICAL rather than constitutional, and therefore variable. The strongest conclusion explains why the same office produced Blair's dominance and Truss's collapse.` },

      { q: '"Collective ministerial responsibility remains an effective convention." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `REMAINS EFFECTIVE:\n• It still binds — ministers who cannot support a decision are expected to resign, and many do.\n• It gives the government a single public position and disciplines dissent.\n• The payroll vote it creates delivers around a hundred guaranteed government votes.\n• Its withdrawal remains fatal to a premiership, which shows the convention still carries real weight — fifty-plus resignations ended Johnson's premiership in 2022.\n• Cabinet confidentiality is still formally expected and breaches are treated as serious.\n\nWEAKENED:\n• Formal suspensions — the 1975 and 2016 EU referendums, when ministers campaigned on both sides.\n• Routine leaking and briefing against colleagues has eroded the unity the convention is meant to project.\n• Ministers have publicly dissented without resigning, and PMs with weak authority cannot enforce it.\n• "Sofa government" undermines the bargain — ministers bound by decisions they were not consulted on have less reason to feel bound.\n• Enforcement depends entirely on the PM's political strength, so it is strongest when least needed.\n\nGuidance for L5: the sharpest analysis notes that the convention is a BARGAIN — support in exchange for consultation — and that the collapse of the second half explains the erosion of the first.` },
    ]
  },

  '1.4': {
    name: 'The Judiciary',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which judicial independence is maintained in the UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Security of tenure — senior judges hold office during good behaviour and can be removed only by the monarch following an address from both Houses, a procedure used only once, in 1830, against the Irish judge Sir Jonah Barrington. Analysis: removes the most direct instrument of pressure.\n• Guaranteed salaries — charged on the Consolidated Fund, so not subject to annual parliamentary approval or ministerial manipulation.\n• Independent appointment — the Judicial Appointments Commission, created by the CRA 2005, selects on merit through open competition, removing patronage appointment by a serving politician. Analysis: contrast with the openly political US confirmation process.\n• Separation from the legislature — the CRA 2005 removed the Law Lords from Parliament and stripped the Lord Chancellor of headship of the judiciary.\n• The sub judice rule and contempt of court restrain parliamentary and press pressure.\n\nGuidance: top-band answers distinguish FORMAL protections (robust) from the INFORMAL position (weaker — ministerial and press attacks after Miller I and II).` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three reasons why judicial review has become more significant since 1997.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• The Human Rights Act 1998 — created an entirely new set of grounds and allowed rights claims domestically rather than in Strasbourg, making litigation far cheaper and more practicable, and generating a substantial body of case law.\n• Devolution — created questions about the limits of devolved competence that only the courts could resolve.\n• Growth of executive and agency decision-making under broad delegated powers — simply created more decisions capable of challenge.\n• A more rights-conscious legal culture, with campaigning groups and firms willing to bring test cases.\n• The CRA 2005 gave the senior judiciary a clearer institutional identity separate from the legislature.\n\nAnalysis expected: the government response — restrictions on legal aid, tighter standing and time limits, and the Judicial Review and Courts Act 2022 — is itself evidence that review had become a genuine constraint, and evidence that Parliament retains the final word.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three limits on the power of the UK Supreme Court.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Parliamentary sovereignty — no court may override an Act of Parliament, and there is no higher constitutional law against which statute can be measured. The Court can only interpret, or declare incompatibility.\n• Declarations of incompatibility leave the statute valid and in force — only Parliament may amend it.\n• Parliament can reverse any ruling by ordinary statute — the Judicial Review and Courts Act 2022 reversed the Cart (2011) ruling and introduced suspended quashing orders.\n• The Court is reactive — it cannot initiate cases and can only decide disputes litigants bring.\n• It depends on the executive to give effect to its rulings.\n\nGuidance: the strongest analysis compares with the US Supreme Court, whose constitutional rulings can realistically only be reversed by the Court itself — which is why the "too powerful" charge is far harder to sustain in the UK.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three arguments that the UK Supreme Court has become too powerful.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Miller I and II placed the Court at the centre of the most contested political question of a generation. Analysis: the charge is that unelected judges settled matters belonging to elected politicians; Miller II in particular applied a standard with limited precedent.\n• The HRA s.3 interpretive duty has produced readings some regard as straining the words Parliament actually enacted.\n• Judicial review has expanded substantially in grounds and frequency, giving courts routine oversight of executive decisions and imposing caution on administration.\n• The senior judiciary is unrepresentative — disproportionately male, white, privately and Oxbridge educated — raising questions about whose assumptions inform interpretation (Griffith's critique).\n\nGuidance: one-sided by design, but a top-band answer may still note the rebuttals — the Court did not choose these cases, it ruled on executive power under existing principle, and Parliament reversed part of the expansion in 2022.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse the significance of three landmark UK court cases.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Factortame (1991) — UK courts disapplied part of the Merchant Shipping Act 1988 for conflict with EU law. Significance: the first setting aside of primary legislation; basis removed with the ECA's repeal.\n• Miller I (2017) — prerogative could not be used to remove statutory rights; only Parliament could authorise Article 50. Also held Sewel judicially unenforceable. Significance: protected Parliament against the executive, so it strengthened rather than weakened sovereignty.\n• Miller II (2019) — prorogation unlawful as frustrating Parliament's constitutional functions. Significance: held a prerogative power justiciable that the government argued was purely political.\n• Belmarsh (2004) — declaration of incompatibility over indefinite detention; government amended the law. Significance: shows s.4's political force exceeding its legal force.\n\nGuidance: analysis must address CONSTITUTIONAL significance, not recount facts. L1–L2 for narrative accounts.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three factors that may threaten judicial neutrality.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Unrepresentative composition — the senior judiciary remains disproportionately male, white, privately educated and Oxbridge educated, reflecting the narrow recruitment base of the senior bar. Analysis: a homogeneous bench may share unexamined assumptions where interpretation involves discretion.\n• Griffith's critique — the argument that judges share an establishment outlook producing a consistent tilt on public order, property and state power, whatever their conscious intentions.\n• Political and media pressure — ministerial and press attacks on judges after Miller I and II, and government reluctance to defend them promptly.\n• The expansion of judicial review into politically contested territory makes rulings look political whether or not they are.\n\nCounter-arguments worth crediting: merit-based selection through the JAC; improving diversity at lower levels feeding upwards; and the fact that courts rule against government frequently, which is hard to square with systematic establishment bias.` },

      { q: '"The UK Supreme Court is too powerful." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `TOO POWERFUL:\n• Miller I and II placed the Court at the heart of the most divisive political question of a generation; Miller II applied a novel standard.\n• HRA s.3 has produced interpretations straining what Parliament enacted.\n• Judicial review has expanded substantially since 1997, giving courts routine oversight of administration.\n• Judges are unelected, unaccountable and unrepresentative.\n• Rulings have overturned government decisions taken on a democratic mandate.\n\nNOT TOO POWERFUL:\n• It CANNOT strike down an Act of Parliament — the decisive structural point.\n• Declarations of incompatibility leave statute valid; Parliament decides.\n• Parliament can reverse any ruling by ordinary legislation, and did so in the Judicial Review and Courts Act 2022.\n• The Court did not choose these cases; they were brought to it, and it ruled on the limits of EXECUTIVE power under existing principle, not on the merits of Brexit.\n• It is reactive and has no enforcement machinery.\n• Compare the US Supreme Court, whose constitutional rulings are effectively irreversible.\n\nGuidance for L5: the comparison with the USA is the strongest available evidence and should appear. A conclusion that recognises the Court's power runs against the EXECUTIVE rather than the LEGISLATURE reaches L5.` },

      { q: '"Judicial independence is well protected in the UK." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `WELL PROTECTED:\n• Security of tenure — removal only by address from both Houses; used only once, in 1830 (against the Irish judge Sir Jonah Barrington).\n• Salaries charged on the Consolidated Fund, beyond ministerial manipulation.\n• The JAC since 2005 removed patronage appointment by a serving politician — a sharp contrast with the US confirmation process.\n• The CRA 2005 removed the Law Lords from the legislature and reformed the Lord Chancellor.\n• The sub judice rule and contempt of court restrain parliamentary and press pressure.\n• Judges rule against government regularly, which is direct evidence of independence in practice.\n\nLESS WELL PROTECTED:\n• Ministerial and press attacks on judges after Miller I and II, with government slow to defend them.\n• The Lord Chancellor's duty to defend judicial independence has been inconsistently discharged; the post no longer requires legal qualification.\n• Parliament can legislate to curb judicial power, as in 2022 — independence exists at Parliament's pleasure.\n• Legal aid restrictions limit access, affecting the courts' practical reach.\n• Unrepresentative composition raises neutrality rather than independence concerns, but the two are often conflated.\n\nGuidance for L5: must distinguish FORMAL protections (robust, and largely strengthened since 2005) from the INFORMAL conventions of restraint (eroding). The best conclusions note that independence is legally secure but politically more exposed than a decade ago.` },

      { q: '"The Human Rights Act 1998 has been effective in protecting rights in the UK." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `EFFECTIVE:\n• Incorporation made rights litigation practicable domestically rather than requiring a journey to Strasbourg.\n• s.3 imposes a strong interpretive duty, substantially shaping statutory meaning.\n• s.6 makes it unlawful for public authorities to act incompatibly — this is where the Act actually bites, and it reaches most government activity.\n• s.4 declarations of incompatibility have almost always produced legislative amendment — Belmarsh is the standard example.\n• It generated a substantial body of rights case law and a more rights-conscious legal culture.\n\nNOT EFFECTIVE:\n• Not entrenched — repealable by simple Commons majority, and repeatedly threatened with replacement by a British Bill of Rights.\n• Declarations leave the offending statute valid and in force; Parliament may simply decline to act.\n• Rights have been curtailed by counter-terrorism legislation permitting extended detention and expanded surveillance, and by restrictions on protest.\n• Legal aid restrictions limit practical access to the remedies it provides.\n• Critics argue it transferred too much interpretive power to unelected judges; others that it did not transfer enough.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest analysis notes that the s.4 mechanism was a DELIBERATE compromise preserving parliamentary sovereignty, so judging the Act by whether it entrenches rights misunderstands what it was designed to do. Should also weigh theoretical fragility against demonstrated practical stability.` },

      { q: '"The UK judiciary has become too political." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `TOO POLITICAL:\n• Miller I and II ruled on the most contested political question of a generation; Miller II held a prerogative power justiciable that had long been assumed political.\n• Expanded judicial review gives courts routine oversight of politically contested administrative decisions.\n• HRA s.3 requires courts to make choices about statutory meaning that are unavoidably evaluative.\n• Pressure groups now use litigation as a deliberate political strategy, drawing courts into policy disputes.\n• Griffith's argument that judges share an establishment outlook.\n\nNOT TOO POLITICAL:\n• The Court does not select its cases and cannot initiate them.\n• In Miller I and II it ruled on the LIMITS OF EXECUTIVE POWER under existing constitutional principle, not on the merits of Brexit — it constrained the government on Parliament's behalf.\n• Appointment is by an independent commission on merit, not by politicians — unlike the USA, nobody claims UK judges were appointed to produce particular outcomes.\n• Judges are barred from political activity and bound by the sub judice rule.\n• Courts cannot strike down statute, so their rulings are always reversible by Parliament.\n\nGuidance for L5: the comparative dimension is decisive. The charge has far more force in the USA, where appointments are openly partisan and precedent has been reversed following changes in composition. In the UK the criticism concerns rulings on executive power, not the process that produced the judges. An answer making this distinction reaches L5.` },
    ]
  },

  '1.5': {
    name: 'Devolution',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three features of devolution in the UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Asymmetry — the nations hold different powers and use different structures, and England has no devolved legislature at all. Analysis: because each settlement was negotiated separately, there is no principled basis for resolving the resulting anomalies.\n• Legal revocability — powers are delegated by a sovereign Parliament, not constitutionally divided. Analysis: this is what distinguishes devolution from federalism; the UK Internal Market Act 2020 demonstrated it in practice.\n• Proportional electoral systems — AMS in Scotland (and in Wales until 2026), STV in Northern Ireland. Analysis: produced multi-party systems, coalition and minority government, in sharp contrast to Westminster.\n• Power-sharing in Northern Ireland — mandatory inclusion of unionist and nationalist parties with linked First and deputy First Minister offices. Analysis: essential to the peace settlement but structurally fragile.\n• Policy divergence — tuition fees, prescription charges, social care, minimum alcohol pricing, COVID rules.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires analysis of consequences, not description of institutions.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which devolution has produced policy divergence in the UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Higher education funding — Scotland abolished tuition fees for Scottish students at Scottish universities; England moved to fees of up to £9,250, raised to £9,535 from 2025/26; Wales operates its own system of grants and fees.\n• Health and social care — free personal care for the elderly in Scotland; prescription charges abolished in Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland but retained in England; Scotland and Wales declined the English internal-market NHS reforms.\n• Public health — minimum unit pricing of alcohol in Scotland and Wales.\n• Education — different curricula and assessment regimes, including the abolition of SATs-style testing in Wales.\n• COVID-19 — four different sets of rules and timings across the UK, which made divergence unusually visible to the public.\n\nAnalysis expected: divergence proves devolution transferred REAL power rather than administrative discretion; it also creates pressure, since English voters observe policies unavailable to them.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which devolution has affected the UK constitution.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Dispersal of power from Westminster — legislatures with primary law-making powers over major policy areas, leading many to describe the UK as quasi-federal. Analysis: the description is imprecise, since Westminster legally retains the power to amend or abolish these bodies.\n• Pressure on parliamentary sovereignty — the Sewel Convention, the Scotland Act 2016 declaring the Scottish Parliament permanent, and devolved democratic mandates. Analysis: Miller I resolved the legal question by holding Sewel unenforceable, confirming sovereignty in strict law.\n• New electoral and party dynamics — proportional systems produced multi-party politics and gave nationalist parties a platform and, decisively, a record of government.\n• The West Lothian question and the English anomaly — EVEL was introduced in 2015 and abandoned as unworkable in 2021.\n\nGuidance: credit the observation that devolution disperses power without dividing sovereignty.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three reasons why the devolution settlement has proved unstable.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Asymmetric design with no agreed endpoint — settlements negotiated separately and renegotiated in 2012, 2016 and 2017, with England excluded. Analysis: continual transfer without a settled endpoint invites further demands rather than closing the question.\n• Absence of legal entrenchment — devolved powers rest on ordinary statute and on a convention Miller I confirmed the courts will not enforce, so disputes escalate into constitutional crises rather than being resolved judicially.\n• Brexit and competing mandates — Scotland and Northern Ireland voted Remain while the UK voted Leave; returning EU competences in devolved fields raised whether they should pass to the devolved bodies or be held centrally. The UK Internal Market Act 2020 was passed over withheld consent.\n• Northern Ireland's power-sharing structure allows either community's largest party to collapse the institutions — suspensions in 2017–2020 and 2022–2024.\n• The Barnett formula's population basis remains contested from several directions.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires causal analysis of WHY instability follows.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three arguments that the UK should become a federal state.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Entrenchment would protect devolved powers legally rather than politically — Miller I showed Sewel is unenforceable, and the UK Internal Market Act showed Westminster will legislate over withheld consent.\n• It would resolve the English anomaly by creating an English tier, answering the West Lothian question that EVEL failed to settle.\n• It would provide a constitutional court and clear rules for resolving centre–region disputes, which the current arrangement lacks entirely.\n• It would replace the ad hoc, repeatedly renegotiated settlement with a coherent design.\n• It might stabilise the union by giving the nations genuinely secure self-government within it.\n\nCounter-arguments worth crediting: federalism requires codification, which is incompatible with parliamentary sovereignty as traditionally understood; England's size (around 85% of the population) makes a balanced federation very difficult.\n\nGuidance: one-sided by design; require analysis rather than assertion.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which Westminster retains power over the devolved nations.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Legal sovereignty — devolved bodies are creatures of statute and could be amended or abolished by ordinary Act. Westminster suspended the Northern Ireland Assembly on several occasions.\n• Reserved matters — defence, foreign affairs, immigration, the constitution, most macroeconomic policy, and equality law all remain at Westminster.\n• The Sewel Convention's weakness — Miller I held it judicially unenforceable despite statutory recognition, and the word "normally" has been used to legislate without consent (EU Withdrawal legislation; UK Internal Market Act 2020).\n• Funding — the Barnett formula is set by the Treasury; the devolved nations depend on block grants they do not control.\n• The UK Internal Market Act 2020 constrains devolved regulatory divergence.\n\nGuidance: top-band answers note the gap between LEGAL power (extensive) and POLITICAL capacity to use it (limited).` },

      { q: '"Devolution has strengthened the United Kingdom." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `STRENGTHENED:\n• Gave the nations meaningful self-government WITHIN the UK, removing the argument that only independence could deliver distinctive policy.\n• The 2014 Scottish referendum produced a No vote on ~85% turnout.\n• Proportional systems produce coalition and compromise rather than automatic nationalist dominance.\n• Responded to demonstrated demand — the 1997 referendums produced Yes votes in Scotland and Wales.\n• Allowed policy tailored to each nation, increasing satisfaction with governance.\n• The Good Friday Agreement's power-sharing was essential to the Northern Ireland peace settlement.\n\nWEAKENED:\n• Created national institutions, parties and media around which distinct political identities consolidated.\n• Gave nationalist parties a platform AND a record of government, transforming the SNP from protest vehicle to party of government.\n• Continual renegotiation (2012, 2016, 2017) suggests an unstable process rather than a settled endpoint.\n• Brexit created competing mandates — Scotland and NI voted Remain.\n• The UK Internal Market Act 2020, passed over withheld consent, did lasting damage to intergovernmental relations.\n• Asymmetry and the unresolved English question generate resentment.\n\nGuidance for L5: must engage the counterfactual — would nationalism have grown anyway without devolution? The strongest conclusions note that devolution changed the FORM of the constitutional question rather than answering it.` },

      { q: '"The devolution settlement is in need of reform." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `NEEDS REFORM:\n• Asymmetry — different powers, different structures, and England with no devolved legislature despite ~85% of the population.\n• The West Lothian question remains unanswered; EVEL was introduced in 2015 and abandoned in 2021.\n• No legal entrenchment — Sewel is unenforceable and Westminster has legislated over withheld consent.\n• The Barnett formula allocates by population rather than assessed need and was intended as temporary in the 1970s.\n• Northern Ireland's institutions are structurally fragile — two prolonged suspensions since 2017.\n• Brexit created unresolved disputes over returning competences.\n• No mechanism exists for resolving centre–devolved disputes other than politics or litigation.\n\nDOES NOT NEED REFORM / reform is impractical:\n• The settlement has delivered stable devolved government and genuine policy divergence for over two decades.\n• Flexibility has allowed incremental adaptation to demand — three further transfers since 2012.\n• Federalism would require codification, incompatible with parliamentary sovereignty as traditionally understood.\n• England's size makes a balanced federation very difficult; English regional devolution was rejected in the 2004 North East referendum.\n• Reform risks reopening questions currently dormant and could accelerate rather than contain nationalism.\n\nGuidance for L5: should distinguish reforms that are DESIRABLE from those that are FEASIBLE, and reach a conclusion about which anomaly matters most.` },

      { q: '"Devolution has created a quasi-federal United Kingdom." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `QUASI-FEDERAL:\n• Devolved legislatures hold primary law-making powers over major policy areas, producing substantial real divergence.\n• Scotland has significant tax and welfare powers under the Scotland Act 2016.\n• The Scotland Act 2016 and Wales Act 2017 declare the devolved bodies permanent and put Sewel in statute.\n• Reclaiming devolved powers would be politically near-impossible, whatever the legal position.\n• Devolved governments have their own mandates, civil services and international representation.\n\nNOT FEDERAL:\n• Powers are DELEGATED, not constitutionally divided — Parliament remains legally sovereign and could amend or abolish the devolved bodies by ordinary Act.\n• Miller I held Sewel judicially unenforceable despite statutory recognition — the devolved nations have no legal remedy.\n• Westminster legislated over withheld consent through the UK Internal Market Act 2020; no US state could be overridden this way.\n• England has no devolved tier at all, so there is no symmetrical federal structure.\n• There is no codified constitution, no constitutional court and no entrenched division of powers.\n• Northern Ireland's Assembly has been suspended by Westminster.\n\nGuidance for L5: the term "quasi-federal" captures a POLITICAL reality and a LEGAL fiction simultaneously. A top-band answer explains that the gap between the two is precisely what Brexit exposed, and reaches a conclusion about whether political entrenchment can substitute for legal entrenchment.` },

      { q: '"Further devolution would be beneficial for the UK." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `BENEFICIAL:\n• Brings decisions closer to those affected, allowing policy tailored to local circumstances.\n• Responds to demonstrated demand — every further transfer since 1998 followed political pressure.\n• May contain nationalism by delivering self-government within the union.\n• English devolution through metro mayors has produced genuine local innovation and could be extended.\n• Would address the English anomaly and the West Lothian question.\n• Allows policy experimentation from which other nations can learn.\n\nNOT BENEFICIAL:\n• Risks fragmenting the UK internal market and creating regulatory divergence that burdens business — the rationale for the UK Internal Market Act.\n• Postcode-lottery concerns: entitlements varying by residence within one state.\n• May accelerate rather than contain nationalism by strengthening national institutions and identities.\n• Funding complexity — the Barnett formula is already contested and further divergence would strain it.\n• Duplication and administrative cost.\n• English regional devolution was rejected in the 2004 North East referendum, suggesting limited appetite.\n\nGuidance for L5: should distinguish further devolution TO THE NATIONS from devolution WITHIN England, since the arguments differ substantially. A conclusion that treats them as one question caps at L3.` },
    ]
  },

  '1.6': {
    name: 'Democracy and Participation',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three features of representative democracy in the UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Free and fair elections under universal adult suffrage with a secret ballot, regulated by the Electoral Commission. Analysis: legitimacy is qualified by FPTP disproportionality — governments take office on roughly 33–44% of the vote (Labour 2024: 411 seats on 33.7%).\n• The constituency link — each voter has one identifiable MP accountable for their area, providing a clear channel for casework and redress. Analysis: the strongest representative feature of the system; list systems dilute it.\n• Party competition and a functioning opposition, funded through Short Money. Analysis: gives voters a programmatic choice and an alternative government.\n• Accountability — MPs and governments face removal at elections, and Parliament scrutinises between them.\n• Pluralism — pressure groups and a free press provide continuous representation between elections.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires evaluation of how well each feature works, not description alone.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three arguments that the UK is suffering a participation crisis.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Declining turnout — fell from ~84% in 1950 to 59.4% in 2001, the lowest since 1918, and has not returned to post-war levels. Turnout is far lower in local, devolved and PCC elections.\n• Collapse in party membership — from millions in the 1950s to a small fraction. Analysis: parties recruit candidates, debate policy and connect citizens to government, so this weakens the whole chain of representation.\n• The age gap — turnout among 18–24s is consistently far below the over-65s, skewing whose preferences politicians attend to.\n• Declining trust in politicians, accelerated by the 2009 expenses scandal.\n• The growth of safe seats under FPTP, which makes votes feel worthless in most constituencies.\n\nGuidance: one-sided by design; require analysis of CONSEQUENCES, not just statistics.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which participation in UK politics could be increased.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Compulsory voting — as in Australia and Belgium. Analysis: would raise turnout immediately and correct the age and class skew, but manufactures turnout without engagement and may produce uninformed voting.\n• Votes at 16 — Scotland and Wales already do this for devolved elections, and the 2014 referendum showed strong engagement; the Representation of the People Bill (introduced February 2026, not yet law by September 2026) would extend it to all UK elections. Analysis: voting is habit-forming, but 18–24s already have the lowest turnout, so it may enlarge the abstaining group.\n• Electoral reform — addresses the strongest explanation for low turnout, since turnout is higher in marginals and under proportional systems. Analysis: rejected decisively in 2011, and the parties able to legislate are those FPTP advantages.\n• Digital or e-voting — convenience, though security concerns and the likelihood that convenience is not the binding constraint.\n• More referendums and greater use of e-petitions.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires the trade-off for each proposal.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three milestones in the extension of the UK franchise.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Great Reform Act 1832 — extended the vote to some middle-class men and began the redistribution of seats. Analysis: established the principle that the franchise could be extended, opening the way to further reform.\n• Representation of the People Act 1918 — all men over 21, women over 30 with a property qualification. Analysis: the single largest extension; the gendered age limit shows how partial it remained.\n• Equal Franchise Act 1928 — women on the same terms as men, at 21.\n• Representation of the People Act 1969 — voting age lowered to 18.\n• Scotland (2014 onwards) and Wales — votes at 16 for devolved and local elections.\n\nAnalysis expected: credit discussion of the suffragist/suffragette debate over which methods were decisive, and of the role of the First World War.\n\nGuidance: narrative accounts without analysis cap at L2.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which rights are protected in the UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• The Human Rights Act 1998 — incorporates the ECHR; s.3 interpretive duty, s.4 declarations, s.6 making it unlawful for public authorities to act incompatibly. Analysis: s.6 is where the Act actually bites, reaching most government activity.\n• The Equality Act 2010 — consolidated anti-discrimination law around nine protected characteristics, and reaches PRIVATE actors in employment and services, which constitutional rulings cannot.\n• Common law and judicial review — natural justice, and courts quashing unlawful public-body decisions.\n• Continuing ECHR membership — cases may reach Strasbourg independently of the HRA.\n• The Freedom of Information Act 2000 — transparency underpinning accountability.\n\nAnalysis expected: the central weakness is that NONE of this is entrenched — all are repealable by simple majority.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three arguments for lowering the voting age to 16.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Consistency with other responsibilities — at 16 a person may work and pay income tax and national insurance, consent to medical treatment, and join the armed forces with parental consent. No taxation without representation.\n• Habit formation — voting is widely understood to be habit-forming, so earlier enfranchisement while young people are still in education, and reachable through citizenship teaching, may raise lifelong participation.\n• Precedent and evidence — 16- and 17-year-olds voted in the 2014 Scottish independence referendum with high engagement, and Scotland and Wales have since extended the franchise for devolved and local elections without difficulty.\n• Long-term stakes — decisions on tuition fees, climate and Brexit fall disproportionately on the young.\n\nCounter-arguments worth crediting: maturity and susceptibility to influence; 18–24s already have the lowest turnout.\n\nGuidance: one-sided by design.` },

      { q: '"The UK is suffering a participation crisis." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `YES:\n• Turnout fell to 59.4% in 2001 and has not returned to post-war levels — after recovering to 67–69% in 2017–19 it fell back to about 60% in 2024, the second-lowest since 1918; local, devolved and PCC turnout is frequently below 50%.\n• Party membership collapsed from millions in the 1950s.\n• Turnout among 18–24s is far below the over-65s, skewing policy responsiveness.\n• Trust in politicians fell sharply after the 2009 expenses scandal.\n• Growth of safe seats makes votes feel worthless in most constituencies.\n\nNO:\n• Participation has SHIFTED, not declined — pressure group membership (the National Trust, the RSPB) far exceeds that of all parties combined; e-petitions attract hundreds of thousands of signatures.\n• Turnout responds to stakes — ~85% in the 2014 Scottish referendum, ~72% in 2016.\n• Party membership surged after 2015; Labour's rose above half a million and the SNP grew dramatically after 2014.\n• Turnout recovered to 67–69% in 2017 and 2019 after the 2001 low, although it fell back to about 60% in 2024.\n• UK turnout is not unusually poor among established democracies.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest answers REFRAME the question — the evidence is better explained as a crisis of LEGITIMACY than of participation, since citizens engage readily when they judge a vote will decide something and abstain when they judge it will not. That reframing points at the electoral system rather than at apathy, and changes which reforms count as solutions. An answer that only tallies turnout statistics caps at L3.` },

      { q: '"Rights are effectively protected in the UK." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `EFFECTIVELY PROTECTED:\n• The HRA makes Convention rights enforceable domestically; s.6 reaches most government activity; s.4 declarations have almost always produced amendment (Belmarsh).\n• The Equality Act 2010 reaches private discrimination in employment, services and housing — something constitutional rights alone cannot.\n• Judicial review has expanded substantially since 1997; courts rule against government regularly.\n• An independent judiciary and the rule of law.\n• Continuing ECHR membership provides an external backstop independent of the HRA.\n• In practice no government has repealed either statute despite decades of proposals.\n\nNOT EFFECTIVELY PROTECTED:\n• Nothing is entrenched — both statutes are repealable by simple Commons majority, and replacement of the HRA has been repeatedly proposed.\n• Rights curtailed by counter-terrorism legislation permitting extended detention and expanded surveillance, and by restrictions on protest.\n• Declarations of incompatibility leave the offending statute valid and in force.\n• Legal aid restrictions limit practical access to remedies.\n• Rights are residual in character rather than positively guaranteed.\n\nGuidance for L5: must weigh THEORETICAL fragility against DEMONSTRATED stability, and should note that a British Bill of Rights would be as vulnerable as the HRA unless entrenched — which is incompatible with parliamentary sovereignty. That point is what separates L5 from L4.` },

      { q: '"Referendums should be used more widely in the UK." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `MORE WIDELY:\n• Direct legitimacy for constitutional change — in an uncodified constitution nothing else prevents a government altering the framework by ordinary legislation.\n• High engagement — ~85% turnout in 2014 and ~72% in 2016, far above contemporaneous general elections.\n• Resolves cross-cutting issues that no general election can settle, since Europe divided both main parties for decades.\n• Educative — generates extensive public debate.\n• Entrenches change politically where legal entrenchment is unavailable.\n\nNOT MORE WIDELY:\n• Binary framing — the 2016 vote asked whether to leave without specifying what leaving meant, producing a majority for a direction but not an outcome, and years of parliamentary deadlock.\n• Clash with parliamentary sovereignty — a legally advisory vote acquiring binding political authority against a parliamentary majority, with no constitutional mechanism to resolve the conflict.\n• No threshold — a narrow majority on one day can deliver irreversible change.\n• Vulnerable to campaign funding, media framing and differential turnout.\n• They can entrench rather than settle division — neither 2014 nor 2016 closed the question it asked.\n• Often called to manage internal party division rather than because the constitutional moment demanded it — 1975 and 2016 both arose substantially from governing-party splits.\n\nGuidance for L5: should distinguish CONSTITUTIONAL questions (a strong case) from ordinary policy (a weak one).` },

      { q: '"Democracy in the UK is in need of reform." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `NEEDS REFORM:\n• FPTP disproportionality — governments on roughly 33–44% of the vote (Labour 2024: 411 seats on 33.7%); UKIP's 3.9m votes and one seat in 2015; safe seats and wasted votes.\n• The wholly unelected House of Lords amends and delays legislation.\n• Turnout and party membership declines; the age and class skew in participation.\n• The royal prerogative allows significant executive action without prior approval.\n• Executive dominance of a sovereign Parliament — the "elective dictatorship" concern.\n• Party funding dependence on a small number of large donors.\n\nDOES NOT NEED REFORM:\n• Free and fair elections, universal suffrage, the rule of law and an independent judiciary are all secure.\n• FPTP delivers decisive, accountable single-party government and a clear constituency link.\n• Electoral reform was decisively rejected in 2011 on a 68% No vote.\n• Reform since 1997 has already been extensive — devolution, the HRA, the Supreme Court, the Wright reforms.\n• Participation has shifted to pressure groups and single-issue campaigning rather than disappearing.\n• The Lords performs genuine revising work and is more assertive since 1999.\n\nGuidance for L5: must PRIORITISE — which defect matters most, and would the proposed remedy actually address it? A conclusion that recommends reform without engaging the 2011 referendum result, or that opposes it without engaging the 2015 disproportionality figures, cannot reach L5.` },
    ]
  },

  '1.7': {
    name: 'Elections and Referendums',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three advantages of the First Past the Post electoral system.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Strong, decisive single-party government — by amplifying the lead of the largest party into a seat bonus, FPTP usually manufactures a majority, allowing a manifesto to be implemented without post-election bargaining. Analysis: voters know at the moment of voting which programme they are choosing.\n• Clear accountability — a single governing party can be held responsible and removed at the next election, unlike a coalition whose programme no voter endorsed.\n• Simplicity — one X, quickly counted and easily understood, supporting confidence in the result.\n• The constituency link — one identifiable MP accountable for each area, providing a clear channel for casework.\n• Excludes extremist parties with dispersed support.\n\nGuidance: one-sided by design; require analysis of WHY each is an advantage.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three disadvantages of the First Past the Post electoral system.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Disproportionality — seat shares diverge sharply from vote shares. In 2015 UKIP won ~3.9m votes and one seat while the SNP won ~1.5m votes and 56 seats, because FPTP rewards concentrated support and penalises dispersed support. Governments routinely take office on roughly 33–44% of the vote (Labour 2024: 411 seats on 33.7%).\n• Wasted votes — votes for losing candidates and the winner's surplus have no effect on the outcome, so most votes cast do not influence who is elected.\n• Safe seats — the result is effectively predetermined in most constituencies, depressing turnout and concentrating campaigning and policy attention on a small number of marginals.\n• Tactical voting — voters are pushed away from expressing a genuine first preference.\n• A high barrier to new parties, entrenching the two main parties.\n\nGuidance: the 2015 comparison is the single strongest piece of evidence and should feature in a top-band answer.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three electoral systems used in the UK other than First Past the Post.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Additional Member System (Scottish Parliament; the Senedd until its move to closed-list PR in 2026) — two votes, one for a constituency member elected by FPTP and one for a regional list, with list seats allocated correctively. Analysis: retains a single constituency representative while producing broadly proportional results; has produced coalition and minority government.\n• Single Transferable Vote (Northern Ireland Assembly and local elections, Scottish local elections) — multi-member constituencies with ranked preferences and a quota. Analysis: highly proportional with few wasted votes; chosen for Northern Ireland precisely to guarantee both communities representation; dilutes the link to a single representative.\n• Supplementary Vote — first and second preferences, with all but the top two eliminated. Used for mayors and PCCs; changed to FPTP by the Elections Act 2022, then restored by the English Devolution and Community Empowerment Act 2026. Analysis: the winner has broader support than a bare plurality but the system is not proportional.\n• Closed-list PR (the Senedd from 2026) — 96 members elected in 16 six-member constituencies, seats allocated by D'Hondt, and voters choose a party rather than a candidate. Analysis: highly proportional, but removes any choice between a party's candidates and weakens the individual constituency link.\n\nGuidance: credit the analytical point that the same electorate produces different party systems under different rules.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three arguments for reforming the Westminster electoral system.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Proportionality — governments take office on roughly 33–44% of the vote (Labour 2024: 411 seats on 33.7%), so the seat distribution does not reflect how the country voted; 2015 produced UKIP's 3.9m votes and one seat.\n• Wasted votes and safe seats — most votes have no effect, which depresses turnout and is the strongest explanation for the participation problem.\n• Devolved evidence — AMS (in Scotland, and in Wales until 2026) and STV (in Northern Ireland) have produced workable government, undermining the claim that proportionality means instability.\n• Comparative evidence associates proportional systems with higher turnout.\n• Representation for parties with substantial but dispersed support.\n\nCounter-arguments worth crediting: the 2011 AV referendum's 68% No vote; coalition bargaining after the election rather than voter choice before it.\n\nGuidance: one-sided by design.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three arguments against the wider use of referendums in the UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Binary framing of complex questions — the 2016 referendum asked whether to leave without specifying what leaving meant, so it produced a majority for a direction but not for any outcome, and Parliament spent years unable to agree on implementation.\n• Clash with parliamentary sovereignty — UK referendums are legally advisory but politically binding, creating a conflict between popular and parliamentary authority that the constitution provides no mechanism to resolve.\n• No threshold — a narrow majority on a single day can deliver an irreversible constitutional change.\n• Vulnerability to campaign funding, media framing and differential turnout.\n• They can entrench rather than settle division — neither 2014 nor 2016 closed the question.\n• Motive — often called to manage internal party division rather than because the constitutional moment required it.\n\nGuidance: one-sided by design.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which the choice of electoral system affects a party system.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Plurality systems sustain two-party dominance (Duverger's law) — only the largest party in each district wins anything, so dispersed support wins nothing and voters, donors and candidates converge on the two viable parties.\n• Proportional systems produce multi-party systems, coalition and minority government — AMS in Scotland (and in Wales until 2026) has given Greens and others genuine representation; STV in Northern Ireland guarantees both communities.\n• Geography of support interacts with the system — under FPTP, concentrated support succeeds (SNP 56 seats on 1.5m votes) while dispersed support fails (UKIP one seat on 3.9m).\n• Voter behaviour changes with the rules — the same UK electorate splits tickets and supports smaller parties where the system rewards it.\n\nGuidance: this is strong evidence that electoral systems SHAPE party systems rather than merely reflecting them; L3 requires that analytical point.` },

      { q: '"First Past the Post should be replaced for UK general elections." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `SHOULD BE REPLACED:\n• Disproportionality — governments on roughly 33–44% of the vote (Labour 2024: 411 seats on 33.7%); UKIP 3.9m votes/1 seat vs SNP 1.5m votes/56 seats in 2015.\n• Wasted votes and safe seats depress turnout and concentrate campaigning on a handful of marginals.\n• Tactical voting distorts genuine preferences.\n• Devolved experience shows proportional systems producing workable government.\n• Comparative evidence links PR with higher turnout.\n• A high barrier to new entrants entrenches a two-party duopoly that no longer reflects how people vote.\n\nSHOULD NOT BE REPLACED:\n• Delivers decisive single-party government able to implement a manifesto voters endorsed.\n• Under PR the governing programme is negotiated AFTER the election between parties, which no voter directly chose.\n• Small parties can gain leverage disproportionate to their support in coalition bargaining.\n• The single-member constituency link would be weakened or lost.\n• Simplicity and rapid, clear results support confidence.\n• The 2011 AV referendum rejected change by ~68% on ~42% turnout.\n\nGuidance for L5: must engage WHY reform has stalled — the parties able to legislate are precisely those FPTP advantages, which is a rational rather than a philosophical explanation. Should also note AV is not proportional, so the 2011 result is weaker evidence against PR than it appears.` },

      { q: '"Referendums have been good for UK democracy." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `GOOD:\n• Direct legitimacy for major constitutional change — devolution, AV, Scottish independence, EU membership.\n• Exceptional engagement — ~85% turnout in 2014 including 16- and 17-year-olds; ~72% in 2016, both far above general elections.\n• Settled questions no general election could, since Europe cut across both main parties for decades.\n• Educative — generated sustained public debate on constitutional questions.\n• The 1998 Good Friday Agreement referendum was essential to the Northern Ireland peace settlement.\n\nNOT GOOD:\n• 2016's binary question produced a mandate for a direction but not an outcome, and years of parliamentary deadlock, prorogation, litigation and an early general election followed.\n• Created an unresolvable clash between popular and parliamentary sovereignty.\n• No threshold — an irreversible change on a narrow majority.\n• Intensified rather than settled division; neither 2014 nor 2016 closed its question.\n• Often called for party management reasons — 1975 and 2016 both arose from governing-party splits.\n• Campaign spending and misinformation concerns.\n\nGuidance for L5: should distinguish referendums that WORKED (1998 Good Friday; 1997 devolution — implementing a clear, worked-out proposal) from those that did not (2016 — a direction without a specification). That distinction is the strongest available analytical move.` },

      { q: '"Elections in the UK are democratic." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `DEMOCRATIC:\n• Universal adult suffrage, secret ballot, free and fair contests regulated by the Electoral Commission.\n• Regular elections with a maximum five-year term.\n• Genuine party competition and peaceful transfers of power.\n• Statutory spending limits and a ban on paid political TV advertising constrain the role of money far more than in the USA.\n• The constituency link gives every voter an identifiable representative.\n• Proportional systems operate at devolved level.\n\nNOT FULLY DEMOCRATIC:\n• FPTP disproportionality — governments on a minority of the vote; millions of wasted votes.\n• Safe seats mean the outcome is decided by relatively few voters in marginals.\n• The House of Lords is wholly unelected.\n• Voter ID requirements introduced by the Elections Act 2022 raise access concerns.\n• Party funding depends on a small number of large donors.\n• Turnout and the age and class skew in participation.\n• Boundary review and constituency size disparities.\n\nGuidance for L5: must distinguish PROCEDURAL democracy (secure) from the DEMOCRATIC QUALITY of outcomes (contested). The strongest conclusions specify which standard is being applied.` },

      { q: '"Proportional representation would improve UK democracy." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `WOULD IMPROVE:\n• Seat shares would reflect vote shares, ending the 2015-style distortion.\n• Far fewer wasted votes; the end of safe seats would raise turnout and spread campaigning across the country.\n• Representation for parties with dispersed support currently excluded.\n• Devolved experience shows PR producing stable, workable government in the UK specifically.\n• Coalition governments may produce more consensual and durable policy.\n• Would end tactical voting.\n\nWOULD NOT IMPROVE:\n• The governing programme would be negotiated after the election, so voters would not choose it.\n• Small parties can hold leverage disproportionate to their support — kingmaker problems.\n• Weakened or lost constituency link, particularly under list or large multi-member systems.\n• Coalition can blur accountability — voters cannot easily identify whom to punish.\n• Potential for instability and for extremist parties to win seats.\n• The 2011 referendum rejected change decisively.\n\nGuidance for L5: must specify WHICH proportional system, since the trade-offs differ sharply between AMS, STV and list systems. An answer treating "PR" as a single option caps at L3. The devolved evidence is the strongest UK-specific material available and should be used.` },
    ]
  },

  '1.8': {
    name: 'Political Parties',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three functions of political parties in the UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Representation — aggregating and articulating the interests of sections of society. Analysis: weakened by dealignment and falling membership, so parties represent a narrower activist base than they once did.\n• Participation and recruitment — providing a route into political activity and selecting candidates for office. Analysis: the ministerial pool comes almost entirely from parties.\n• Policy formulation — developing programmes presented in a manifesto, which underpins the doctrine of the mandate and the Salisbury Convention.\n• Providing government and opposition — the governing team and a credible alternative, with the opposition funded through Short Money.\n• Political education — informing and structuring public debate.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires evaluation of how well each function is now performed.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which the Labour Party changed under Tony Blair.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Revision of Clause IV in 1995 — replaced the commitment to common ownership with an endorsement of a dynamic market economy. Analysis: symbolically decisive, signalling abandonment of the traditional socialist objective.\n• Acceptance of the market and of Conservative settlements — retained privatisation and trade union legislation, accepted Conservative spending limits for two years, and rejected rises in income tax rates, pursuing redistribution instead through tax credits and the minimum wage.\n• The Third Way — drawing on Giddens, presenting a position beyond free-market capitalism and state socialism, stressing opportunity and responsibility rather than equality of outcome.\n• Organisational centralisation — tighter control of candidate selection, conference and communications, reducing union and conference influence.\n\nAnalysis expected: supporters credit three consecutive election victories; critics argue it hollowed out the party's distinctiveness and its relationship with its base.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three differences between One Nation conservatism and the New Right.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Role of the state — One Nation accepts intervention and the welfare state out of paternalistic obligation and to preserve stability; the New Right regards a large state as damaging to enterprise and self-reliance and would roll it back economically.\n• Economics — One Nation accepts the mixed economy and managed capitalism; the neo-liberal strand of the New Right pursues free markets, privatisation, deregulation and lower direct taxation.\n• Philosophical basis — One Nation rests on organic society, hierarchy carrying obligation and Burkean pragmatism; the New Right's neo-liberal strand rests on abstract rational individualism (Rand, Nozick), which Burke and Oakeshott would regard as precisely the error they warned against.\n• Attitude to change — One Nation is cautious and evolutionary; Thatcherism was radical and disruptive of established institutions and communities.\n\nGuidance: credit the analytical point that the New Right's conservative credentials are genuinely contested.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three arguments in favour of the state funding of political parties.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Reduces dependence on large donors — both main parties rely on a small number of very large contributions, creating a persistent perception, reinforced by recurring honours controversies, that access and influence can be purchased. Public funding paired with a donation cap would break that link.\n• Fairer competition — parties currently campaign with very unequal resources, which advantages the best-funded rather than the most popular. Allocation by votes received would match resources to demonstrated support and help smaller parties.\n• Parties perform genuine public functions — candidate recruitment, policy development, political education — which arguably merit public support, as Short Money already partly recognises.\n• Would free parties to develop policy rather than fundraise.\n\nCounter-arguments worth crediting: taxpayers funding parties they oppose; entrenching existing parties; removing the incentive to build a mass membership.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three factors affecting the electoral success of UK political parties.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Perceived economic competence — voters reward parties they trust to manage the economy. The 1992 ERM crisis damaged Conservative reputation for a decade; the 2022 mini-budget did so again. New Labour's pre-1997 discipline on tax and spending was designed to neutralise this vulnerability.\n• Leadership and party unity — visible division is strongly associated with defeat. Labour's divisions in the early 1980s and Conservative splits over Europe in the 1990s both preceded heavy defeats; the disciplined parties of 1997 and 2019 won large majorities.\n• The electoral system and the geography of support — FPTP means WHERE votes are matters as much as how many; parties target a limited number of marginal seats, which shapes policy offers.\n• Dealignment and valence — declining class-based loyalty means competence and leadership now predict vote choice more than ideological position.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires causal analysis with examples.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which minor parties influence UK politics.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Agenda-setting without seats — UKIP won ~3.9m votes and one seat in 2015, but its electoral pressure was central to the calling of the EU referendum. Analysis: influence can be exerted through the threat to a major party's vote rather than through representation.\n• Winning seats where support is concentrated — the SNP converted ~1.5m votes into 56 seats in 2015 and dominated Scottish representation until 2024, when it fell to 9 seats as Labour won 37 of 57.\n• Representation at devolved level — proportional systems give Greens and others a genuine platform, from which policy influence follows.\n• Holding the balance of power — the Liberal Democrats in the 2010 coalition; the DUP's confidence-and-supply arrangement after 2017.\n• Policy absorption — major parties adopt minor-party positions to capture their support.\n\nGuidance: credit the contrast between concentrated and dispersed support under FPTP.` },

      { q: '"UK political parties have converged ideologically." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `HAVE CONVERGED:\n• New Labour accepted privatisation, market mechanisms, trade union legislation and Conservative spending limits; Clause IV was revised in 1995.\n• Cameron's Conservatives accepted NHS spending commitments and social liberalism, including same-sex marriage.\n• Both competing for the median voter under FPTP — the "catch-all party" thesis.\n• Both accept the market economy, NATO membership and the broad shape of the welfare state.\n• Valence politics means competition is increasingly about competence rather than ideology.\n\nHAVE NOT CONVERGED:\n• The period after 2015 saw sharp divergence — Corbyn proposed extensive renationalisation and higher taxation while the Conservatives pursued a hard Brexit.\n• Substantial ongoing disagreement on taxation, public ownership, welfare and the size of the state.\n• Brexit cut ACROSS parties rather than along them, fracturing both internally rather than bringing them together.\n• Different electoral coalitions produce genuinely different priorities.\n\nGuidance for L5: the defensible conclusion is that convergence was a PHASE (roughly 1994–2015) rather than a permanent condition, and that what voters perceive as lack of choice may be a problem of TRUST rather than of ideological distance. An answer that treats convergence as a fixed feature caps at L3.` },

      { q: '"The UK has a two-party system." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `YES, BY SEATS AT WESTMINSTER:\n• Conservative and Labour have alternated in government throughout the post-war period and between them take the large majority of Commons seats.\n• FPTP penalises parties with dispersed support and raises a high barrier to new entrants.\n• Only these two parties are realistic candidates for government.\n• The Official Opposition structure institutionalises the binary.\n\nNO, BY VOTES AND BY LEVEL:\n• The two main parties' combined vote share has fallen well below post-war levels; Liberal Democrats, Greens, Reform and nationalist parties command a substantial combined share.\n• 2024 — Reform UK won 14.3% of the vote, the Liberal Democrats 72 seats and the Greens 4, and the Conservative and Labour combined vote share fell to about 57%; the SNP dominated Scottish representation from 2015 but fell to 9 seats in 2024.\n• Devolved nations have genuinely multi-party systems under proportional rules — coalition and minority government are normal.\n• Northern Ireland has an entirely separate party system.\n• Coalition (2010) and confidence-and-supply (2017) show single-party majority government is not guaranteed.\n• Some periods look DOMINANT-PARTY rather than two-party — 1979–97 and 2010–24.\n\nGuidance for L5: the answer depends on WHAT IS MEASURED (votes or seats) and WHERE (Westminster or devolved). A top-band answer says so explicitly and reaches a reasoned conclusion rather than asserting one label.` },

      { q: '"Party funding in the UK needs reform." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `NEEDS REFORM:\n• Both main parties depend on a small number of very large donations — wealthy individuals and companies for the Conservatives, affiliated unions for Labour.\n• Recurring controversies over honours and access create a persistent perception that influence can be purchased.\n• Very unequal resources advantage the best-funded rather than the most popular party.\n• Declining membership has increased dependence on large donors.\n• Regulation is weak — the Lobbying Act 2014 is widely judged ineffective.\n\nDOES NOT NEED REFORM / reform is problematic:\n• Statutory spending limits and the ban on paid political TV advertising already constrain money far more than in the USA.\n• All donations above a threshold must be declared to the Electoral Commission — transparency already exists.\n• State funding would require taxpayers to fund parties they oppose.\n• It could entrench existing parties against new entrants and remove the incentive to build a mass membership.\n• No formula would command cross-party agreement, which is why repeated reform attempts have failed.\n• Donation is itself a form of political participation.\n\nGuidance for L5: should engage the practical obstacle — each party resists reform that would disadvantage its own funding base, which explains the repeated failure of cross-party talks. That rational explanation is worth more than listing arguments.` },

      { q: '"Political parties are still essential to UK democracy." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `STILL ESSENTIAL:\n• Only parties can contest elections, form governments and legislate — pressure groups cannot.\n• They recruit and train the political class and provide the entire ministerial pool.\n• They aggregate diverse interests into coherent programmes, giving voters a meaningful choice.\n• The manifesto and mandate underpin the Salisbury Convention and the legitimacy of the legislative programme.\n• They structure Parliament, making government and opposition possible.\n• Membership surged after 2015, showing continued capacity to mobilise.\n\nLESS ESSENTIAL:\n• Membership collapsed from millions in the 1950s to a small fraction, weakening the representative chain.\n• Pressure groups, think tanks, social media and single-issue campaigns now perform much of the agenda-setting and mobilisation parties once monopolised — National Trust and RSPB membership exceeds that of all parties combined.\n• Trust in parties and politicians fell sharply after 2009.\n• Dealignment has weakened habitual loyalty; valence voting focuses on leaders rather than parties.\n• Referendums bypass parties entirely on the biggest questions.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest conclusion distinguishes a loss of MONOPOLY from a loss of FUNCTION — parties have lost their exclusive hold on political activity but retain the one function nothing else can perform. An answer that conflates the two caps at L3.` },
    ]
  },

  '1.9': {
    name: 'Pressure Groups',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three methods used by pressure groups to influence government.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Lobbying ministers and civil servants, and submitting evidence to select committees and consultations. Analysis: most effective at the drafting stage before positions harden; the executive controls the agenda, so this is the highest-value target in the UK.\n• Media campaigning to shift public opinion and create political costs for inaction — Marcus Rashford's free school meals campaign forced a reversal in 2020.\n• Petitions, demonstrations and direct action — Extinction Rebellion shifted the agenda and contributed to the declaration of a climate emergency, though tactics inconveniencing the public risk alienating the audience.\n• Legal action — judicial review and HRA challenges, a route that bypasses an unsympathetic political majority.\n• Supplying research and specialist expertise departments lack.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires evaluation of when each method works, not description.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three factors that determine the success of a pressure group.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Alignment with the government's agenda — the most reliable predictor. A group pushing where government already wants to go succeeds with modest effort; one opposing settled policy may fail regardless of resources. Trade unions had substantial influence under 1970s Labour governments and almost none under Thatcher, with membership largely unchanged.\n• Resources — funding buys professional lobbying, research, legal action and sustained campaigning, which is why business groups exert influence disproportionate to their numbers.\n• Insider status and expertise — groups whose specialist knowledge government needs (the BMA, the NFU) become embedded in the policy process.\n• Public support and salience — the Snowdrop campaign secured handgun legislation after Dunblane by making inaction politically costly.\n\nGuidance: penalise the claim that MEMBERSHIP SIZE determines success — the Countryside Alliance mobilised huge marches and lost on hunting. Top-band answers make this point explicitly.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which pressure groups enhance democracy.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Continuous representation between elections — elections are infrequent and force voters to choose a whole package at once, whereas groups allow specific preferences to be expressed continuously.\n• Representing intense minorities and the voteless — an electoral system cannot register how strongly a view is held, and groups represent children, future generations and non-citizens.\n• Improving policy quality — groups supply specialist expertise departments lack, exposing unintended consequences before legislation passes.\n• Dispersing power — pluralist competition between many groups prevents any single interest dominating, with government arbitrating.\n• Participation and political education — mobilising citizens and informing debate.\n• Scrutiny — monitoring implementation and using judicial review to hold government to the law.\n\nGuidance: these benefits ASSUME rough parity of access; the strongest answers note that elitists deny it.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which pressure groups may undermine democracy.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Unequal influence — access and influence track resources, organisation and proximity to decision-makers rather than the number of people affected. Business enjoys structural leverage because governments depend on investment and employment decisions, so a well-funded minority can defeat a diffuse majority, inverting the democratic principle.\n• Lack of accountability — groups are not elected, need not be internally democratic, leaders may not represent members, funding is often opaque (particularly for think tanks), and successful groups bear no responsibility for consequences.\n• Illegal direct action — imposes costs on uninvolved members of the public and can be seen as attempting to override democratic decisions.\n• Intense minority lobbying can produce policy serving the organised few at the expense of the unorganised many.\n\nGuidance: one-sided by design; require analysis of MECHANISM, not assertion.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three differences between insider and outsider pressure groups.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Access — insiders are consulted routinely by departments and sit on advisory committees (BMA, NFU); outsiders have no such access, either because government is unsympathetic or by choice.\n• Methods — insiders negotiate quietly with ministers and officials; outsiders rely on demonstrations, direct action and media campaigns (Extinction Rebellion, Just Stop Oil).\n• The trade-off — insider status normally requires moderating demands and accepting confidentiality, which limits the group's ability to campaign publicly or mobilise supporters. Outsiders retain freedom of action but lack the drafting-stage influence that matters most.\n• Status is not fixed — it changes with the government of the day, as the trade unions' shift from insider to outsider after 1979 shows.\n• Access does not guarantee outcomes — a government may consult a group and ignore it.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires the trade-off, not just the definitions.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which think tanks and corporations influence UK policy.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Think tanks set the intellectual agenda — the IFS, IEA and Adam Smith Institute produce policy analysis; much of the Thatcher government's programme was developed in think tanks before becoming policy. They also supply governments with ideas and personnel. Criticism: opacity of funding makes it hard to know whose interests a report serves.\n• Corporations exercise STRUCTURAL power — because governments depend on investment, employment and tax revenue, the anticipated reaction of business constrains policy even without active lobbying. This is the strongest evidence for the elitist critique.\n• Professional lobbying and the revolving door — firms employ former officials and ministers, gaining privileged access and inside knowledge. The Lobbying Act 2014 created a register covering only CONSULTANT lobbyists, excluding the in-house lobbyists who do most of the work.\n• Technical expertise supplied during consultation, which departments increasingly depend on.\n\nGuidance: the structural power point is what separates L3 from L2.` },

      { q: '"Pressure groups enhance democracy in the UK." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `ENHANCE:\n• Continuous representation between elections; specific preferences expressed rather than a single bundled choice.\n• Give intense minorities a voice a majoritarian electoral system cannot register, and represent those who cannot vote.\n• Supply expertise that improves the technical quality of legislation.\n• Disperse power — pluralist competition prevents any single interest dominating; devolution and judicial review have multiplied access points.\n• Increase participation and political education at a time of falling party membership.\n• Scrutinise implementation and hold government to the law.\n\nUNDERMINE:\n• Influence tracks resources, not numbers — a well-funded minority can defeat a diffuse majority.\n• Business enjoys structural leverage no membership campaign can match.\n• Groups are unelected, often not internally democratic, and frequently opaque about funding.\n• Illegal direct action imposes costs on the uninvolved and can override democratic decisions.\n• The Lobbying Act 2014 fails to regulate in-house lobbying or the revolving door.\n\nGuidance for L5: must engage the PLURALIST/ELITIST framework explicitly. The strongest conclusion is that the UK is pluralist in FORM but elitist in EFFECT — competition is genuine, but pluralism assumes rough parity of access and that assumption does not hold. Answers that list benefits and harms without that framework cap at L3.` },

      { q: '"Pressure groups are more powerful than political parties in the UK." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `GROUPS MORE POWERFUL:\n• Membership of major pressure groups far exceeds that of all political parties combined.\n• Groups have gained where parties have weakened — as loyalty and membership declined, single-issue campaigning grew.\n• Groups can shift policy directly — the Snowdrop campaign, Marcus Rashford on free school meals.\n• Judicial review and the HRA give groups a route that bypasses political majorities.\n• Devolution multiplied the access points available to groups.\n• Business exercises structural power over policy that no party can ignore.\n\nPARTIES MORE POWERFUL:\n• Only parties contest elections, form governments and legislate — the decisive point.\n• Parties set the legislative agenda and control the parliamentary timetable.\n• A group's success depends overwhelmingly on whether its aims align with the governing party's agenda.\n• Parties are accountable to voters; groups pursue narrow interests without responsibility.\n• Group victories are usually on single issues; parties determine the whole programme of government.\n• Party membership surged after 2015, so decline is not linear.\n\nGuidance for L5: influence has SHIFTED rather than TRANSFERRED. A top-band answer distinguishes agenda-setting power (increasingly with groups) from decision-making power (still with parties) and concludes on which matters more.` },

      { q: '"UK pressure group politics is elitist rather than pluralist." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `ELITIST:\n• Influence tracks wealth, organisation and access rather than the number of people affected.\n• Business enjoys structural power — governments depend on investment and employment decisions, so the anticipated reaction of business constrains policy without any lobbying at all.\n• Insider status is granted selectively by government, so access is controlled by the powerful.\n• The revolving door entrenches corporate access; the Lobbying Act 2014 excludes in-house lobbyists.\n• Think tank funding is frequently opaque.\n• The Countryside Alliance's mass marches lost on hunting while small professional bodies routinely win — numbers do not determine outcomes.\n\nPLURALIST:\n• Competition between groups is genuine and outcomes often reflect compromise.\n• Multiple access points — Westminster, Whitehall, devolved bodies, local government, the courts, the media — so defeat in one arena is not final.\n• Judicial review and the HRA allow poorly resourced groups to defeat government.\n• Successful campaigns by low-resource groups exist — Snowdrop, Rashford.\n• Devolution has genuinely increased dispersal since 1999.\n\nGuidance for L5: the defensible conclusion is that both descriptions capture part of the truth — the system is pluralist in form and elitist in effect, because pluralism's assumption of rough parity of access fails. An answer reaching that synthesis, rather than choosing a side, reaches L5.` },

      { q: '"Insider pressure groups are more successful than outsider groups." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `INSIDERS MORE SUCCESSFUL:\n• Access at the drafting stage, before positions harden and become public — the highest-value point of influence in a system where the executive controls the agenda.\n• Routine consultation and seats on advisory bodies give continuous rather than episodic influence — the BMA on health, the NFU on agriculture.\n• Government depends on their technical expertise, creating mutual dependence.\n• They can influence quietly without the political cost of public confrontation.\n• Their aims are usually already aligned with the governing agenda, which is the strongest single predictor of success.\n\nOUTSIDERS CAN BE MORE SUCCESSFUL:\n• Public campaigning can change the political weather in a way quiet lobbying cannot — the Snowdrop campaign secured handgun legislation after Dunblane against an organised opposing lobby.\n• Extinction Rebellion contributed materially to the declaration of a climate emergency.\n• Marcus Rashford forced a policy reversal on free school meals through media campaigning alone.\n• Litigation offers outsiders a route that bypasses an unsympathetic political majority.\n• Insider status carries a real cost — moderating demands and accepting confidentiality limits what a group can publicly campaign for.\n• Access does not guarantee outcomes: a government may consult a group attentively and ignore it entirely.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest answers note that the categories are not fixed — status changes with the government of the day, as the trade unions' shift after 1979 shows — and that ALIGNMENT WITH THE GOVERNING AGENDA predicts success better than insider status does. Many effective groups deliberately combine both approaches.` },
    ]
  },

  '1.10': {
    name: 'The European Union',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three aims of the European Union.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Preventing war — the founding purpose after 1945 was to bind European states, particularly France and Germany, so closely that war between them became impractical. The European Coal and Steel Community pooled precisely the industries needed to wage war.\n• Economic integration — a single market with the four freedoms (goods, services, capital, people) plus a customs union with a common external tariff. Analysis: the aspect the UK participated in most fully.\n• "Ever closer union" — progressive deepening of political integration, including monetary union and common policies on agriculture, fisheries, competition and the environment. Analysis: this is what the UK consistently resisted, taking opt-outs from the euro and Schengen.\n• Regional development and cohesion funding to reduce disparities between member states.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires analysis of the UK's differentiated relationship with each aim.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three institutions of the European Union.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• European Commission — the executive: proposes legislation, enforces the treaties, manages the budget. Members are appointed, not elected. Analysis: central to the democratic deficit critique, since citizens cannot remove the body that proposes law.\n• European Parliament — directly elected MEPs; scrutinises and, with the Council, approves legislation and the budget. Analysis: powers were strengthened by Lisbon, but turnout in European elections is low.\n• Council of the EU — ministers from member state governments, sharing legislative power. Analysis: qualified majority voting means a state can be outvoted, which was a persistent UK concern.\n• European Council — heads of government setting overall direction.\n• European Court of Justice — interprets EU law and ensures uniform application. Analysis: distinct from the European Court of Human Rights, which the UK remains subject to.\n\nGuidance: penalise confusion of the ECJ with the ECtHR.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which EU membership affected the UK constitution.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Qualification of parliamentary sovereignty — the ECA 1972 gave EU law domestic effect, and Factortame (1991) established that UK courts would disapply an Act of Parliament conflicting with it. Analysis: the competing reading is that supremacy rested on an ordinary statute Parliament itself passed and later repealed, so sovereignty was delegated rather than surrendered.\n• Strengthening the courts — membership required UK judges to interpret and apply EU law, refer questions to the ECJ, and disapply primary legislation. Analysis: materially expanded the judiciary's constitutional role, and the confidence developed arguably shaped later rulings.\n• Transfer of competence — trade, agriculture, fisheries, competition, much environmental and employment law passed to EU institutions.\n• New democratic authority — European Parliament elections under a proportional list system gave UKIP a platform and MEPs that FPTP would have denied it, generating the pressure that led to the referendum.\n\nGuidance: credit the point that membership's constitutional legacy includes the forces that ended it.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three reasons why the UK voted to leave the European Union in 2016.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Sovereignty and "take back control" — membership transferred law-making power to unelected EU institutions, and successive treaties deepened integration far beyond the common market endorsed in 1975 without a further popular vote. Analysis: its force lay in fusing a technical constitutional objection to an intuitive claim about who decides.\n• Immigration and free movement — accession of central and eastern European states from 2004 without UK transitional controls produced substantial migration on which no government could act. Analysis: probably the most electorally potent factor, because it made the sovereignty argument concrete.\n• Long-term economic and political discontent — Leave support correlated with lower incomes, fewer qualifications, older age and post-industrial regions. Analysis: the referendum offered a rare decisive vote against the status quo, which explains why establishment economic warnings were discounted rather than persuasive.\n• Campaign factors — Leave's clearer message; Conservative division; UKIP's pressure.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires causal analysis, not listing.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three constitutional consequences of Brexit for the UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Restoration of formal legal supremacy — the EU (Withdrawal) Act 2018 repealed the ECA 1972, ending EU law supremacy and ECJ jurisdiction while converting existing EU law into retained domestic law (renamed assimilated law by the REUL Act 2023). UK courts can no longer disapply an Act of Parliament on EU law grounds.\n• Strain on devolution — returning competences in devolved fields raised whether they should pass to the devolved bodies or be held centrally; the UK Internal Market Act 2020 was passed over the objections of both the Scottish Parliament and the Senedd, exposing Sewel's lack of legal force.\n• The Northern Ireland Protocol — created trade friction within the UK, strongly opposed by unionists, contributing to the collapse of power-sharing from 2022 until February 2024, resolved after the Windsor Framework (2023).\n• Executive power and scrutiny — extensive delegated powers to amend retained EU law by statutory instrument, with minimal parliamentary debate.\n• Popular vs parliamentary sovereignty — a legally advisory vote acquiring binding political authority.\n\nGuidance: credit any three developed with analysis.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three reasons why EU membership was politically contentious in the UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Sovereignty — the supremacy of EU law and ECJ jurisdiction sat uneasily with a constitution founded on parliamentary sovereignty, and integration deepened incrementally through successive treaties without further popular consent.\n• Free movement and immigration — substantial migration on which membership meant no government could act, fusing the immigration question to the sovereignty question.\n• The democratic deficit — key powers resting with the appointed Commission and the Council rather than the directly elected Parliament, with low turnout in European elections.\n• Party management — Europe cut ACROSS rather than along party lines, splitting the Conservatives between single-market supporters and sovereignty-focused sceptics from Maastricht onwards, and creating a problem successive leaders could not contain.\n• Electoral pressure from UKIP, amplified by proportional European elections.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires causal analysis of WHY each generated conflict.` },

      { q: '"EU membership seriously undermined UK parliamentary sovereignty." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `SERIOUSLY UNDERMINED:\n• Factortame (1991) — UK courts disapplied part of an Act of Parliament, the first such instance; for 47 years courts could refuse to apply primary legislation in areas of EU competence.\n• The ECJ held final interpretive authority in those areas.\n• Competence transferred over trade, agriculture, fisheries, competition, and much environmental and employment law.\n• Qualified majority voting meant the UK could be outvoted and bound by law it opposed.\n• Integration deepened incrementally through Maastricht and Lisbon without further popular consent.\n\nDID NOT SERIOUSLY UNDERMINE:\n• Supremacy rested on the ECA 1972 — an ordinary statute Parliament itself passed and, in 2018, repealed.\n• Sovereignty was therefore DELEGATED, not surrendered, and the power to reclaim it proved entirely real.\n• The UK retained vetoes in significant areas and helped shape the rules it was bound by.\n• The UK secured opt-outs from the euro and Schengen, so integration was not uniform.\n• Parliament remained free to legislate on everything outside EU competence.\n\nGuidance for L5: the decisive analytical move is to distinguish sovereignty as a LEGAL CAPACITY (retained, and demonstrably so in 2018–20) from sovereignty as a PRACTICAL POLITICAL POWER (constrained, since the only remedy was leaving the entire legal order). A conclusion that engages that distinction reaches L5; one that asserts either side without it caps at L3.` },

      { q: '"Brexit has had a damaging effect on the UK constitution." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `DAMAGING:\n• Created an unresolvable clash between popular and parliamentary sovereignty — a legally advisory vote acquiring binding political authority against a parliamentary majority that favoured Remain.\n• Produced years of deadlock, an unlawful prorogation, two landmark Supreme Court cases and an early general election.\n• Exposed and then damaged the devolution settlement — the UK Internal Market Act 2020 passed over withheld consent from Scotland and Wales; Sewel shown to be legally worthless.\n• The Northern Ireland Protocol created trade friction within the UK and contributed to a two-year Assembly suspension.\n• Massive delegated powers to amend retained EU law with minimal scrutiny.\n• Drew the judiciary into political controversy and prompted restrictions on judicial review.\n\nNOT DAMAGING / beneficial:\n• Restored formal legal supremacy to Parliament; courts can no longer disapply statute on EU law grounds.\n• Demonstrated that the constitution can absorb an extraordinary shock without breakdown — the institutions functioned.\n• Miller I and II showed the courts capable of constraining executive overreach.\n• Resolved a question that had destabilised both main parties for decades.\n• Returned significant policy competence to elected UK institutions.\n\nGuidance for L5: should distinguish damage to the constitutional SETTLEMENT (substantial, particularly devolution) from damage to constitutional FUNCTIONING (the institutions worked). The strongest conclusions note that Brexit did not create these tensions but exposed ones that were already unresolved.` },

      { q: '"The European Union suffered from a democratic deficit." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `YES:\n• The Commission — which proposes all legislation and enforces the treaties — is appointed, not elected, and cannot be removed by voters.\n• Turnout in European Parliament elections is consistently low, weakening the Parliament's democratic claim.\n• Qualified majority voting means a member state can be bound by law its government and electorate opposed.\n• Complexity and remoteness — few citizens understood the institutions or could identify their MEP.\n• Integration deepened through treaties negotiated by governments, frequently without referendums.\n• The ECJ, an appointed body, held final interpretive authority.\n\nNO:\n• The European Parliament IS directly elected and its powers were substantially strengthened by Lisbon; it must approve legislation and the budget.\n• The Council consists of ministers from democratically elected national governments, so a second chain of accountability exists.\n• Treaties required ratification by every member state according to its own constitutional procedures.\n• National parliaments scrutinise their governments' EU positions.\n• Subsidiarity requires decisions at the lowest effective level.\n• Comparable complexity and remoteness exist in any large federal or multi-level system.\n\nGuidance for L5: should note that the critique assumes a national model of democracy may not translate to a supranational body, and that the deficit was a genuine grievance AND a politically useful one for Eurosceptics regardless of its merits. That double point is the mark of L5.` },

      { q: '"The UK was always an awkward partner in the European Union." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `AWKWARD:\n• Joined late (1973), having declined to join at the founding, and held a renegotiation referendum within two years.\n• Secured opt-outs from the euro, Schengen and initially the Social Chapter — differentiated integration throughout.\n• Consistently resisted "ever closer union" and political integration while embracing the single market.\n• Europe split the governing party repeatedly — Maastricht rebels, and division from the 1990s onwards.\n• Persistent budget rebate disputes.\n• The only member state to leave.\n\nNOT SIMPLY AWKWARD:\n• The UK was a leading architect of the single market — the Single European Act 1986 reflected British priorities.\n• It supported enlargement to central and eastern Europe, shaping the EU's development substantially.\n• It complied with EU law at rates comparable to or better than many member states.\n• Other member states also secured opt-outs and resisted integration (Denmark, Ireland).\n• Public opinion was not consistently hostile — the 1975 referendum produced a ~67% Yes vote, and polling fluctuated considerably.\n• "Awkwardness" reflects a genuine constitutional difference — parliamentary sovereignty — rather than mere obstinacy.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest analysis explains awkwardness STRUCTURALLY — an uncodified constitution founded on parliamentary sovereignty fits poorly with a supranational legal order — rather than treating it as a national character trait.` },
    ]
  },

  /* ══════════════════════ PAPER 2 — USA AND COMPARATIVE POLITICS ══════════════════════ */

  '2.1': {
    name: 'The US Constitution and Federalism',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three principles of the US Constitution.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Separation of powers — legislative, executive and judicial functions vested in separate institutions with separate personnel; a member of Congress cannot simultaneously serve in the executive. Analysis: the opposite of UK fusion, and the reason a President has no guaranteed legislative majority.\n• Checks and balances — the branches deliberately overlap, each holding specific powers over the others (veto and override, confirmation, ratification, judicial review, impeachment). Analysis: makes gridlock the intended consequence rather than a malfunction.\n• Federalism — power constitutionally divided between national and state government, with the Tenth Amendment reserving undelegated powers to the states.\n• Entrenchment — a double supermajority for amendment, protecting the whole structure from temporary majorities.\n• Limited government and the Bill of Rights placing liberties beyond ordinary legislation.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires analysis of CONSEQUENCES.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which federal power has expanded in the USA.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Broad interpretation of the commerce clause — from the New Deal the Court accepted Congress could regulate almost any activity substantially affecting interstate commerce, underpinning federal regulation of wages, agriculture, civil rights in public accommodation and the environment. Analysis: achieved entirely through interpretation, not amendment; partially reversed in United States v Lopez (1995).\n• Fiscal federalism — the Sixteenth Amendment gave Washington revenues the states could not match, and conditional grants allow it to direct state policy where it cannot legislate directly (the national minimum drinking age via highway funding). Analysis: circumvents the constitutional division entirely, since states remain formally sovereign but financially unable to refuse.\n• Crisis and war — the Depression, the Second World War and the Cold War each concentrated authority in Washington.\n• Incorporation of the Bill of Rights against the states through the Fourteenth Amendment.\n\nGuidance: the fiscal point is what separates L3 from L2.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three checks that Congress holds over the President.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Overriding a veto by a two-thirds vote in both chambers. Analysis: rarely achieved, which makes the veto a formidable presidential power.\n• The power of the purse — all federal spending requires appropriation, so Congress can refuse funds, attach conditions or force a shutdown. Analysis: the most concrete lever, since it requires no executive cooperation.\n• Senate confirmation of appointments (simple majority) and ratification of treaties (two-thirds). Analysis: the two-thirds threshold has defeated major agreements, which is why presidents increasingly use executive agreements instead.\n• Impeachment by the House and conviction by a two-thirds Senate majority. Analysis: the ultimate sanction, but no President has ever been convicted.\n• Oversight through committee investigations and subpoenas.\n\nGuidance: credit the analytical point that these checks operate powerfully under divided government and are largely unused under unified government.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three strengths of the US Constitution.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Effective dispersal of power — separation of powers, checks and balances and federalism together ensure no institution governs unchecked; presidents have been constrained by impeachment, adverse rulings, congressional refusal and midterm losses.\n• Entrenched rights — the Bill of Rights and Reconstruction amendments place liberties beyond ordinary majorities and make them judicially enforceable against both federal and state government.\n• Stability with adaptability — it has survived civil war, depression and profound social change, with judicial interpretation supplying flexibility the amendment process cannot.\n• Federalism accommodates diversity across a continental state and permits policy experimentation.\n\nGuidance: one-sided by design, but top-band answers may note that each strength is the direct source of a corresponding weakness.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three weaknesses of the US Constitution.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Gridlock — the separation of powers, divided government and the filibuster mean major legislation frequently fails even when a President has just won a national mandate. Analysis: this is the intended consequence of the design, so whether it is a weakness depends on whether one fears government doing too much or too little.\n• Rigidity — the double supermajority means only 27 amendments in 240 years, so provisions widely regarded as obsolete persist. The Electoral College has twice since 2000 elected a President who lost the popular vote, with majority public support for reform.\n• Transfer of constitutional authorship to the Court — because amendment is near-impossible, change happens through judicial interpretation, so nine unelected justices effectively amend.\n• Undemocratic features — Senate malapportionment gives Wyoming and California equal representation.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires analysis of why each matters.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three types of power in the US federal system.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Enumerated (delegated) powers — expressly granted to the federal government: declaring war, coining money, regulating interstate commerce, raising armies.\n• Implied powers — inferred from the enumerated powers via the necessary and proper (elastic) clause. Analysis: the principal engine of federal expansion, since it allows Congress to legislate on matters not expressly listed.\n• Reserved powers — under the Tenth Amendment, all powers not delegated federally nor prohibited to the states: education, policing, the conduct of elections, and since Dobbs, abortion regulation. Analysis: these are why rights vary substantially by state.\n• Concurrent powers — held by both levels, such as taxation and law enforcement.\n\nAlso relevant: the supremacy clause establishing that federal law prevails over conflicting state law.\n\nGuidance: credit accurate examples for each category.` },

      { q: '"The US Constitution is no longer fit for purpose." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `NOT FIT FOR PURPOSE:\n• Near-unamendable — 27 amendments in 240 years; the Equal Rights Amendment passed Congress in 1972 and never ratified. Obsolete provisions persist despite majority opposition.\n• The Electoral College has twice since 2000 elected a President who lost the popular vote; Senate malapportionment gives equal representation to vastly unequal states.\n• Gridlock — polarisation plus the filibuster means major legislation frequently fails even under unified government.\n• Transfers constitutional authorship to nine unelected justices, making appointments the central battleground of American politics.\n• Says nothing about political parties, primaries, campaign finance or the administrative state, all of which now dominate.\n\nFIT FOR PURPOSE:\n• Has survived civil war, depression, world wars and profound social change — remarkable durability.\n• Entrenched rights have protected minorities against hostile majorities — Brown, Obergefell.\n• Effective dispersal of power; presidents genuinely constrained.\n• Judicial interpretation supplies adaptability the amendment process lacks.\n• Federalism accommodates enormous diversity and permits state-level policy experimentation.\n• Gridlock is the intended consequence of a design meant to force broad consensus.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest answers recognise that each strength is the DIRECT SOURCE of the corresponding weakness — entrenchment protects rights AND makes obsolete provisions unreformable. A conclusion that tallies lists caps at L3.` },

      { q: '"Federalism in the USA is in decline." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `IN DECLINE:\n• Broad commerce clause interpretation from the New Deal transformed a government of limited enumerated powers into one with near-general legislative competence.\n• Fiscal federalism — conditional grants direct state policy in areas where Washington cannot legislate directly; states are formally sovereign but financially unable to refuse.\n• Incorporation of the Bill of Rights against the states via the Fourteenth Amendment subjects state action to federal constitutional standards.\n• Crisis and war repeatedly concentrated authority in Washington.\n• Federal mandates and regulation have expanded enormously.\n\nNOT IN DECLINE:\n• United States v Lopez (1995) revived commerce clause limits, showing the expansion is reversible.\n• New Federalism from Nixon and Reagan replaced categorical with block grants, restoring state discretion.\n• Recent decades show sharply increased state divergence on cannabis, healthcare expansion, voting procedures and — since Dobbs — abortion.\n• Federal gridlock has made states the only functioning arena for policy change, increasing their practical importance.\n• States retain control of elections, education and policing.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest conclusion notes that federalism has not declined so much as CHANGED CHARACTER — from dual to cooperative and now to a polarised divergence driven by federal paralysis. Should also note the balance tracks the Supreme Court's composition.` },

      { q: '"The separation of powers in the USA produces effective government." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `EFFECTIVE:\n• Prevents concentrated power — presidents have been constrained by impeachment, adverse rulings, congressional refusal and midterm losses.\n• Forces broad consensus, so legislation that does pass commands wide support and is more durable.\n• Congress is genuinely independent — it writes its own bills and sets its own agenda, unlike the UK Parliament.\n• Multiple access points allow citizens and groups to influence policy at several stages.\n• Judicial review protects constitutional limits and rights.\n\nNOT EFFECTIVE:\n• Gridlock — major legislation frequently fails even under unified government; the filibuster's effective 60-vote threshold compounds a design already requiring broad agreement.\n• Divided government has been the norm for much of the post-war period.\n• Policy-making has been displaced to the executive (executive orders) and the courts, transferring authority from the most representative branch to the two least accountable.\n• Executive orders produce oscillation rather than settled policy, since successors reverse them.\n• Accountability is blurred — voters cannot easily identify who is responsible for failure.\n• Government shutdowns and debt-ceiling brinkmanship.\n\nGuidance for L5: must engage the point that gridlock is the INTENDED consequence, not a malfunction — so the question is really whether the framers' priorities remain appropriate. That reframing is what reaches L5.` },

      { q: '"The US Constitution effectively protects rights." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `EFFECTIVELY PROTECTS:\n• Rights are entrenched, beyond ordinary legislative repeal, and judicially enforceable against both federal and state government.\n• The Court has used them to deliver change elected branches would not — Brown (1954), Obergefell (2015), Bostock (2020).\n• The Fourteenth Amendment's equal protection clause provides a standard against which any law can be measured.\n• Incorporation extended federal constitutional standards to state action.\n• Strong First Amendment protection of speech, assembly and religion.\n\nDOES NOT EFFECTIVELY PROTECT:\n• Rights depend on INTERPRETATION, so they can be withdrawn — Dobbs (2022) reversed a fifty-year precedent after three appointments, with no change to the text.\n• Shelby County (2013) disabled the Voting Rights Act's most effective mechanism, and Congress has not restored it.\n• Federalism means rights vary substantially by state — abortion, voting procedures, policing standards.\n• Formal equality has not delivered substantive equality in wealth, incarceration or policing outcomes.\n• The Reconstruction amendments lay dormant for nearly a century — text alone achieves nothing without institutional will.\n• Rights restricted by counter-terrorism legislation and surveillance powers.\n\nGuidance for L5: the decisive point is that entrenchment RELOCATES rather than removes vulnerability — US rights cannot be repealed by legislation but can be reinterpreted away by a changed Court. The comparison with the UK's repealable-but-stable statutes strengthens this considerably.` },
    ]
  },

  '2.2': {
    name: 'Congress',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three functions of the US Congress.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Legislation — both chambers must pass an identical text; committees conduct detailed scrutiny and can kill a bill by declining to report it. Analysis: genuinely independent of the executive, unlike the UK Parliament, but polarisation means less is passed.\n• Representation — of a district (House) or state (Senate), with extensive constituency casework. Analysis: weak discipline allows members to vote on constituency grounds, making Congress highly responsive locally but poor at pursuing diffuse national interests.\n• Oversight — committee hearings, investigations and subpoenas. Analysis: vigour tracks partisan advantage, not constitutional duty.\n• The power of the purse — all federal spending requires appropriation.\n• Senate confirmation of appointments and ratification of treaties; impeachment.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires evaluation of how well each is performed.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three exclusive powers of the US Senate.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Confirming presidential appointments — cabinet secretaries, ambassadors and federal judges including Supreme Court justices, by simple majority; since 2017 a filibuster can no longer block a Supreme Court nomination, because cloture on nominations now also needs only a simple majority. Analysis: gives the Senate direct influence over the judiciary for decades, which is why Garland and Barrett became such flashpoints.\n• Ratifying treaties by a two-thirds majority. Analysis: a threshold that has defeated major agreements including the Treaty of Versailles, which is why presidents increasingly use executive agreements instead.\n• Trying impeachments, with conviction requiring two-thirds. Analysis: no President has ever been convicted, which shows the threshold's practical effect.\n• Electing the Vice President if no Electoral College majority.\n\nGuidance: penalise confusion with House exclusive powers (initiating revenue bills, bringing impeachment charges).` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which Congress oversees the executive branch.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Committee investigations and hearings — compelled testimony under oath and subpoenas for documents. Watergate and the January 6th committee are the landmark examples; the requirement to justify policy publicly disciplines administrations in advance.\n• The power of the purse — refusing funds, attaching conditions, or declining to fund government at all. Analysis: the most concrete lever because it needs no executive cooperation; used to cut off funding for Indochina operations and in the Boland Amendments.\n• Senate confirmation hearings — scrutiny of nominees and of the policies they will implement.\n• Impeachment — Clinton and Trump (twice) impeached by the House; none convicted.\n\nAnalysis expected: the decisive point is that oversight vigour tracks PARTISAN ADVANTAGE, not constitutional duty. Under unified government committees have little incentive to investigate their own administration.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three reasons why congressional incumbents are usually re-elected.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Structural advantages of office — name recognition, franked mail, staff resources, and a record of casework and federal funding secured for the district.\n• Fundraising — donors prefer likely winners and want continued access, so challengers are typically heavily outspent.\n• Safe districts — drawn deliberately through gerrymandering or produced by voters sorting geographically, so the general election is effectively decided in advance.\n\nAnalysis expected: House re-election rates routinely exceed 90%. The crucial consequence is that where the general election is a formality, the ONLY real threat is a primary challenge from the ideological flank — so members are rewarded for purity and punished for compromise, which is the principal institutional driver of polarisation. Senate incumbents are less secure, since statewide electorates cannot be gerrymandered.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which the filibuster affects the work of the Senate.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• It creates a de facto supermajority requirement — cloture normally needs 60 of 100 senators, so most legislation requires bipartisan support that polarisation has made unavailable.\n• It pushes legislating into budget reconciliation, which allows budget-related measures by simple majority but sharply restricts what can be included — the ACA's 2010 amendments, the 2017 tax cuts, the 2021 American Rescue Plan and the 2022 Inflation Reduction Act all used this route, although the ACA itself needed 60 votes for cloture.\n• It empowers individual senators and small blocs, since a single member can force delay, giving leverage no representative possesses.\n• Its removal for nominations (lower courts 2013, Supreme Court 2017) means appointments now pass on party lines, which transformed confirmation politics.\n\nAnalysis expected: defenders argue it forces consensus and protects minority interests; critics that it is an extra-constitutional veto allowing senators representing a small share of the population to block majority-supported legislation.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three factors that affect the power of Congress relative to the President.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Party control — the dominant variable. Under divided government Congress investigates aggressively, blocks appointments and refuses legislation; under unified government the same powers go largely unused because members will not damage their own administration.\n• The policy area — congressional checks operate far more effectively over domestic policy than foreign affairs and national security, where presidents have conducted sustained military action without declarations of war and Congress has been politically unwilling to cut funding for deployed troops.\n• Internal fragmentation — Congress is 535 individuals with separate electoral bases, rewarded for constituency service rather than for defending congressional prerogatives. It has repeatedly delegated authority over tariffs, emergencies and regulation because delegation lets members avoid responsibility.\n• Presidential approval ratings and the electoral cycle — honeymoon, midterm losses, lame duck.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires causal analysis.` },

      { q: '"Congress is an effective check on the President." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `EFFECTIVE:\n• Formidable formal powers — appropriations, confirmations, treaty ratification, oversight, impeachment, veto override.\n• Presidents routinely fail to pass central priorities — Clinton on health reform, Trump on ACA repeal in 2017 despite unified government.\n• Committee investigations have exposed wrongdoing — Watergate (leading to Nixon's resignation in 1974) and the January 6th committee (public hearings and criminal referrals of Trump in 2022).\n• Refusal to act is itself an exercise of power.\n• The Senate blocked the Garland nomination outright.\n• Government shutdowns show appropriations leverage in practice.\n\nNOT EFFECTIVE:\n• Checks operate powerfully under divided government and are largely unused under unified government, so effectiveness tracks partisan advantage rather than constitutional duty.\n• Far weaker over foreign policy and national security — no declaration of war since 1942; the War Powers Resolution is largely unenforced because Congress will not cut funding for deployed troops.\n• Congress has delegated extensive authority over tariffs, emergencies and regulation, letting members avoid responsibility.\n• No President has ever been convicted on impeachment — the two-thirds threshold makes the ultimate sanction near-unusable.\n• Presidents bypass Congress through executive orders and agreements.\n\nGuidance for L5: must recognise the check is CONTINGENT (on party control) and DOMAIN-SPECIFIC (strong domestically, weak on national security). An answer asserting one side without those qualifications caps at L3.` },

      { q: '"The Senate is more powerful than the House of Representatives." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `SENATE MORE POWERFUL:\n• Exclusive powers — confirming appointments and ratifying treaties, giving it direct influence over the executive branch and the judiciary for decades ahead.\n• Individual influence — 100 members rather than 435, and Senate rules protecting extended debate mean a single senator can delay; the filibuster lets 41 senators block most legislation.\n• Six-year terms insulate senators from immediate electoral pressure and allow a longer view.\n• Statewide constituencies give a broader mandate and national visibility; the Senate is the main presidential recruiting ground.\n• It tries impeachments.\n\nHOUSE MORE POWERFUL:\n• Exclusive power to initiate revenue bills — the power of the purse begins there.\n• Exclusive power to bring impeachment charges.\n• The Rules Committee gives leadership far tighter control of the floor agenda, so a disciplined House majority can act with a speed the Senate cannot.\n• More directly representative — districts apportioned by population, whereas Senate malapportionment gives Wyoming and California equal weight.\n• Two-year terms make it more responsive to public opinion.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest analysis notes that the Senate's greater power is largely a power to OBSTRUCT rather than to act, and that the comparison therefore depends on whether one values blocking or legislating.` },

      { q: '"The filibuster should be abolished." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `SHOULD BE ABOLISHED:\n• It appears nowhere in the Constitution — an extra-constitutional veto that the framers did not design.\n• 41 senators representing a small share of the national population can block legislation supported by a clear majority, compounding Senate malapportionment.\n• In a polarised era it produces paralysis rather than compromise, because the bipartisan majorities it assumes no longer exist.\n• It has already been abolished for nominations without disaster, showing removal is feasible.\n• It forces legislating through reconciliation, which distorts policy by restricting what can be included.\n\nSHOULD NOT BE ABOLISHED:\n• It forces consensus and protects minority interests in a chamber designed for deliberation rather than majority rule.\n• It prevents rapid reversal of policy with each change of majority, providing stability.\n• Its removal for nominations arguably WORSENED confirmation politics by removing any incentive to seek cross-party support — a cautionary precedent.\n• Whichever party abolishes it will face its absence when next in the minority.\n• The Senate was deliberately designed as a brake on the popularly elected House.\n\nGuidance for L5: should engage the nominations precedent as evidence about what abolition would actually produce — that empirical move is stronger than restating principle.` },

      { q: '"Congress represents the American people effectively." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `EFFECTIVE:\n• Direct accountability to identifiable constituencies, with extensive casework.\n• Weak party discipline allows members to vote on constituency grounds against their party.\n• Two-year House terms make it highly responsive to public opinion.\n• Primaries give voters control over candidate selection, unlike systems where parties choose.\n• Congress has become substantially more diverse in recent decades.\n• The Senate gives smaller states a voice in a continental federation.\n\nNOT EFFECTIVE:\n• Senate malapportionment — Wyoming and California have equal representation despite vastly different populations, so a minority of the population can command a Senate majority.\n• Partisan gerrymandering produces House seat shares diverging from statewide vote shares; Rucho (2019) held such claims non-justiciable federally.\n• Incumbency advantage exceeds 90% in the House, weakening electoral accountability.\n• Safe districts mean the real contest is a low-turnout primary dominated by ideological activists.\n• Campaign finance dependence gives donors disproportionate access.\n• Poor at pursuing diffuse national interests, since members are rewarded for local benefits.\n\nGuidance for L5: should distinguish representation of PLACES (strong) from representation of PEOPLE (distorted by malapportionment and gerrymandering) — that distinction is the mark of L5.` },
    ]
  },

  '2.3': {
    name: 'The Presidency',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three formal powers of the US President.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• The veto — refusing assent to legislation, overridable only by a two-thirds vote in both chambers. Analysis: rarely overridden, making it formidable — but it is a NEGATIVE power, allowing a President to prevent outcomes rather than produce them.\n• Commander-in-Chief — directing the armed forces, though only Congress may declare war. Analysis: presidents have conducted sustained military action without declarations since 1942.\n• Appointments — nominating cabinet secretaries, ambassadors and federal judges including Supreme Court justices, subject to Senate confirmation. Analysis: judicial appointments extend presidential influence decades beyond the term.\n• Treaties — negotiating, subject to two-thirds Senate ratification.\n• The pardon power for federal offences.\n\nGuidance: Neustadt's point that the formal powers are largely negative is what separates L3 from L2.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three informal powers of the US President.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Executive orders — directives to the federal executive branch with the force of law, requiring no congressional approval. Analysis: attractive under gridlock, but reversible by a successor and strikeable by the courts, so they produce oscillation rather than settled policy.\n• Executive agreements — international agreements concluded without Senate ratification, avoiding the two-thirds threshold. Analysis: the cost is impermanence, as the abandonment of the Iran nuclear agreement and the Paris accord showed.\n• The bully pulpit — using the visibility of the office to shape public opinion and pressure Congress. Analysis: Neustadt regarded persuasion as the essence of presidential power, but polarisation has reduced the number of persuadable legislators, so appeals now mobilise the base and harden the opposition.\n• Signing statements and direction of agency enforcement priorities.\n• Party leadership and the electoral mandate.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires the trade-off for each.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three limits on the power of the US President.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Congress — cannot legislate, cannot appropriate money, and requires Senate confirmation and treaty ratification. Analysis: has defeated major initiatives of almost every modern presidency, but operates inconsistently, powerfully under divided government and weakly under unified.\n• The courts — can strike down executive orders and enforcement policies as exceeding authority. Youngstown (1952) invalidated Truman's steel seizure; Biden v Nebraska (2023) struck down student loan forgiveness and DHS v Regents (2020) blocked the rescission of DACA — though the Supreme Court upheld the final travel ban in Trump v Hawaii (2018). Analysis: significance has grown precisely because presidents shifted from legislation to executive action, which is more legally vulnerable.\n• Federalism and the bureaucracy — many powers rest with states, which can decline to cooperate, and the federal bureaucracy is large, permanent and resistant to direction.\n• The two-term limit and the electoral cycle; impeachment.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires analysis of when each bites.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three reasons why presidents increasingly rely on executive orders.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Congressional gridlock — polarisation, the filibuster and frequent divided government mean major legislation often cannot pass, so executive action is the only route to delivering campaign commitments. Obama's use of DACA after the DREAM Act failed is the standard example.\n• Speed and unilateral control — an order takes effect immediately, requires no negotiation or concession, and can be drafted entirely within the executive branch.\n• Broad statutory delegations — Congress has granted extensive discretion in immigration, environmental and trade policy, which presidents exploit.\n• Political demonstration — presidents issue orders at the start of a term to show delivery and to reverse a predecessor's.\n\nAnalysis expected: the weakness is impermanence. Because orders rest on presidential authority rather than statute, a successor can revoke them immediately and the courts can strike them down — producing policy that oscillates rather than settles.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three factors that affect the power of a US President.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Party control of Congress — the dominant variable; unified government enables legislation, divided government largely prevents it.\n• The electoral cycle — the honeymoon period brings maximum authority, so major achievements are heavily front-loaded; the President's party almost always loses seats at the midterms, usually ending the legislative programme; lame duck status drains authority further.\n• Approval ratings — legislators support a popular President whose coattails help them and distance themselves from an unpopular one.\n• Events — crisis can enhance authority (9/11) or destroy it.\n• Personal skill in persuasion and bargaining — Neustadt's central argument.\n\nAnalysis expected: the FORMAL powers are constant throughout; what varies is political capital. That is why the same office produces very different presidencies.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which the President conducts foreign policy.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• As Commander-in-Chief — directing military deployments. Analysis: no declaration of war since 1942, and the War Powers Resolution 1973 has proved largely ineffective because presidents dispute its constitutionality and Congress will not cut funding for deployed troops.\n• Treaty negotiation — subject to two-thirds Senate ratification, a threshold that has defeated major agreements.\n• Executive agreements — concluded without Senate ratification, now the dominant instrument. Analysis: quicker and easier, but a successor can withdraw unilaterally.\n• Recognition of foreign governments, appointment of ambassadors, and direction of the State Department and NSC.\n• The National Security Council within EXOP centralises foreign policy in the White House rather than the State Department.\n\nAnalysis expected: presidential dominance is greatest here, because secrecy, speed and congressional deference all favour the executive.` },

      { q: '"The US presidency has become imperial." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `IMPERIAL (Schlesinger, 1973):\n• Sustained military action without declarations of war since 1942; the War Powers Resolution largely unenforced.\n• Expanding use of executive orders, executive agreements and signing statements.\n• Claims of executive privilege and extensive surveillance powers.\n• Growth of EXOP as an unaccountable parallel executive, with staff requiring no Senate confirmation.\n• Congressional delegation of authority over tariffs, emergencies and regulation.\n• Trump v United States (2024) — immunity for official acts, qualifying the principle of US v Nixon that the President is not above the law.\n\nIMPERILLED:\n• Presidents routinely fail to pass central priorities — Clinton on health reform, Trump on ACA repeal under unified government.\n• The President's party almost always loses the midterms, usually ending the legislative programme.\n• Executive orders are reversed by successors and struck down by courts — Youngstown; DHS v Regents (2020) on DACA; Biden v Nebraska (2023) on student loan forgiveness; Learning Resources v Trump (2026) on IEEPA tariffs.\n• Three presidents impeached; United States v Nixon rejected an executive privilege claim.\n• The two-term limit; federalism; an unresponsive bureaucracy.\n• Neustadt: the formal powers are largely negative, so presidents must persuade rather than command.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest conclusion is DOMAIN-SPECIFIC — the thesis fits foreign policy and national security, where secrecy and congressional deference give wide latitude, and fits domestic policy poorly, where appropriations, confirmations and the filibuster impose real constraint. "It depends which policy area" reaches L5; picking a side does not.` },

      { q: '"The President is more powerful than Congress." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `PRESIDENT MORE POWERFUL:\n• A single national mandate and fixed tenure — cannot be removed for unpopularity or legislative defeat.\n• The veto, requiring a two-thirds override rarely achieved.\n• Executive orders and agreements allow unilateral action; broad statutory delegations give wide discretion.\n• Commander-in-Chief of the world's largest military, with Congress unwilling to use its funding leverage.\n• Judicial appointments extend influence for decades.\n• Agenda-setting through the bully pulpit and national visibility.\n\nCONGRESS MORE POWERFUL:\n• The President cannot legislate or appropriate a single dollar.\n• Senate confirmation and two-thirds treaty ratification.\n• Congress has defeated major presidential priorities repeatedly.\n• Oversight, subpoenas and impeachment.\n• Government shutdowns demonstrate appropriations leverage.\n• The Constitution places Congress in Article I, before the executive.\n\nGuidance for L5: the answer is CONTINGENT on party control and DOMAIN-SPECIFIC. The strongest conclusion notes that Congress is more powerful in what it can PREVENT and the President in what he can DO unilaterally — and that the balance has shifted towards the executive precisely because congressional gridlock closed the legislative route.` },

      { q: '"Executive orders are an effective tool of presidential power." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `EFFECTIVE:\n• Unilateral — no negotiation, no concessions, immediate effect.\n• The only available route under gridlock or divided government; DACA followed the DREAM Act's failure in Congress.\n• Broad statutory delegations give wide discretion in immigration, environmental and trade policy.\n• Demonstrate delivery on campaign commitments and allow rapid reversal of a predecessor's agenda.\n• They have produced genuinely major policy — desegregation of the armed forces, DACA, environmental standards.\n\nNOT EFFECTIVE:\n• Impermanent — a successor revokes them with a stroke of the pen, so policy oscillates rather than settles.\n• Courts can strike them down for exceeding delegated authority — student loan forgiveness was struck down in Biden v Nebraska (2023), though the Supreme Court upheld the final travel ban in Trump v Hawaii (2018).\n• They cannot appropriate money, which only Congress can.\n• Congress can legislate to override, subject to the veto.\n• Reliance on them signals weakness — a President resorting to executive action has usually failed to build a legislative coalition.\n• They invite legal challenge and administrative uncertainty.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest analysis notes the PARADOX — executive orders are evidence of presidential power and of presidential weakness simultaneously, since their use expands precisely where the ability to legislate has collapsed.` },

      { q: '"The US cabinet is insignificant." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `INSIGNIFICANT:\n• Secretaries are individually appointed advisers to a President in whom ALL executive power is constitutionally vested — not a collective decision-making body.\n• They cannot outvote or remove the President; no US cabinet has ever ended a presidency.\n• They do not sit in Congress and have no independent political base, depending entirely on presidential favour.\n• Power has shifted markedly to EXOP — the National Security Adviser may matter more than the Secretary of State, and EXOP staff require no Senate confirmation.\n• Full cabinet meetings are infrequent and largely ceremonial.\n\nSIGNIFICANT:\n• Secretaries run vast departments with enormous budgets and implement policy on the ground.\n• Senate confirmation gives them independent standing and a public record.\n• They provide expertise and constituency links the President lacks.\n• High-profile resignations damage a President politically.\n• Some secretaries have been genuinely influential in shaping policy in their field.\n• They are the public face of the administration's policy before Congress.\n\nGuidance for L5: the comparison with the UK cabinet is the strongest available material — the UK cabinet is a collective body whose withdrawal of support ends a premiership (Thatcher 1990, Johnson 2022), which has no US parallel. That comparison explains WHY the US cabinet is weaker rather than merely asserting it.` },
    ]
  },

  '2.4': {
    name: 'The Supreme Court and the Judicial Branch',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three features of the US Supreme Court.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Nine justices appointed for life during good behaviour. Analysis: insulates them from political pressure, but means one appointment can shape constitutional interpretation for thirty years or more, long outlasting the appointing President.\n• Judicial review — the power to declare Acts of Congress, executive actions and state laws unconstitutional and void. Analysis: NOT granted by the Constitution; the Court asserted it in Marbury v Madison (1803).\n• Control of its own docket through the writ of certiorari, granting review in only a small fraction of petitions. Analysis: significant agenda-setting power.\n• Appointment by the President with Senate confirmation by simple majority; since 2017 a filibuster can no longer block a Supreme Court nomination, because cloture on nominations now also needs only a simple majority.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires analysis of consequences, not description.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three reasons why Supreme Court appointments are politically contentious.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Life tenure and stakes — one appointment can determine policy on abortion, guns, campaign finance and voting for decades, and vacancies are rare and unpredictable.\n• The Court's policy significance — because formal amendment is near-impossible, the Court effectively decides the most contested questions, so appointments are the most reliable route to lasting policy change. Interest groups therefore mobilise heavily.\n• Eroded procedural norms — the Senate refused to consider the Garland nomination in 2016 on election-year grounds, then rapidly confirmed Barrett weeks before the 2020 election. Removal of the filibuster for Supreme Court nominations in 2017 eliminated any need for cross-party support.\n• Consequences — Dobbs reversed a fifty-year precedent shortly after three appointments, reinforcing the perception that constitutional meaning tracks appointments rather than law.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires the rational-incentive analysis, not just description of the process.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three limits on the power of the US Supreme Court.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• No enforcement power — it depends on the executive and the states to implement rulings. The decade of resistance to desegregation after Brown required federal troops at Little Rock.\n• Reactive — it cannot initiate cases and can only decide disputes litigants bring to it.\n• Political checks — justices are nominated by the President and confirmed by the Senate, so composition is determined by elected officials; Congress controls appellate jurisdiction and the number of justices.\n• Reversal — statutory rulings can be reversed by ordinary legislation; constitutional rulings by amendment, as the Sixteenth Amendment did.\n• Legitimacy — its authority rests entirely on public acceptance, imposing a real practical limit on departing from settled opinion.\n\nAnalysis expected: several of these are largely theoretical, since amendment is near-impossible and court-packing politically explosive, so the EFFECTIVE constraints are narrower than the formal list.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse the significance of three landmark Supreme Court cases.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Marbury v Madison (1803) — established judicial review, which the Constitution does not grant. Significance: made the Court the authoritative interpreter of the Constitution.\n• Brown v Board of Education (1954) — segregated schooling unconstitutional, overturning Plessy. Significance: a court delivering nationwide change no legislature of the period would; but implementation required a decade of further litigation.\n• Citizens United v FEC (2010) — independent political spending is protected First Amendment speech; enabled Super PACs. Significance: the Court reshaping the rules of political competition itself.\n• Shelby County v Holder (2013) — struck down the VRA pre-clearance coverage formula.\n• Dobbs v Jackson (2022) — overturned Roe, returning abortion to the states.\n\nGuidance: analysis must address CONSTITUTIONAL significance. Narrative recounting caps at L2.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three differences between originalism and the living Constitution approach.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Source of meaning — originalism holds that the Constitution means what its text meant when adopted; living constitutionalism that it should be applied in light of contemporary conditions and values.\n• Engine of change — originalism leaves social change to legislatures and the amendment process; living constitutionalism allows constitutional protection to develop judicially, which matters because the amendment thresholds make formal change practically unattainable.\n• Judicial role — originalism claims to constrain judges by an objective historical standard; living constitutionalism accepts a more creative interpretive role.\n• Associated rulings — Dobbs and Second Amendment cases (originalist); Brown, Roe and Obergefell (living).\n\nAnalysis expected: NEITHER removes discretion as fully as it claims. Originalism requires contested judgments about historical meaning and about the level of generality intended; living constitutionalism requires judgments about which contemporary values count. Justices' conclusions correlate strikingly with their political outlook whatever method they profess.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which the Supreme Court influences US politics.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Determining the scope of rights — extending them (Brown, Obergefell, Bostock) and withdrawing them (Dobbs). Analysis: a constitutional ruling applies nationwide, needs no legislative majority, and is reversible only by the Court or an unattainable amendment.\n• Policing federal–state boundaries — through the commerce clause, the Tenth Amendment and the supremacy clause, the Court effectively determines how much the federal government may do. Analysis: the balance therefore tracks the Court's composition.\n• Shaping the rules of political competition — Citizens United on campaign finance, Shelby County on voting, Rucho on districting. Analysis: this is influence of a distinctive kind, because it affects who wins future elections and therefore who makes future appointments, creating a feedback loop.\n• Constraining the executive — United States v Nixon; Youngstown.\n\nGuidance: the feedback-loop point separates L3 from L2.` },

      { q: '"The US Supreme Court is too powerful." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `TOO POWERFUL:\n• Nine unelected justices with life tenure can strike down legislation passed by elected majorities.\n• It can reverse long-standing precedent — Dobbs overturned a fifty-year ruling after three appointments, with no change to the constitutional text.\n• Because amendment is near-impossible, its constitutional rulings are effectively final, so it is the real amender.\n• It settles contested moral questions, removing them from ordinary democratic revision.\n• It shapes the rules of political competition itself — Citizens United, Shelby County, Rucho.\n\nNOT TOO POWERFUL:\n• No enforcement machinery — it depends on others to implement, as the decade of resistance after Brown showed.\n• Reactive — it cannot choose which disputes arise.\n• Its composition is determined by elected officials, which is the framers' intended democratic input.\n• Congress can reverse statutory rulings by ordinary legislation and controls the Court's appellate jurisdiction and size.\n• Most cases are decided unanimously or with cross-ideological majorities; the divided cases are unrepresentative.\n• Its authority rests entirely on public acceptance of its legitimacy.\n\nGuidance for L5: the most sophisticated point is STRUCTURAL — an entrenched constitution that is nearly impossible to amend NECESSARILY transfers political authorship to whoever interprets it, so the Court's power is a consequence of the amendment thresholds rather than of judicial ambition. Blaming the justices misidentifies the cause.` },

      { q: '"The Supreme Court appointment process is unsatisfactory." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `UNSATISFACTORY:\n• Nakedly partisan — nominees are selected for judicial philosophy and confirmation votes are now near-party-line.\n• Norms have collapsed — the same election-year argument was applied to Garland in 2016 and abandoned for Barrett in 2020.\n• Removal of the filibuster in 2017 eliminated any incentive to seek cross-party support.\n• Life tenure means the timing of vacancies, including strategically timed retirements, determines constitutional outcomes for decades.\n• Presidents nominate deliberately young candidates to maximise influence.\n• The perception that outcomes follow appointments rather than law damages the Court's legitimacy — reinforced by Dobbs.\n\nSATISFACTORY / defensible:\n• The framers DELIBERATELY gave appointment to elected officials so the Court would reflect democratic politics over time — partisanship is the design working, not failing.\n• Senate confirmation provides genuine scrutiny of nominees' records and views.\n• Nominees are almost always highly qualified jurists.\n• Justices have repeatedly ruled against the expectations of their appointing President.\n• Alternatives — election, or an independent commission as in the UK — carry their own problems.\n\nGuidance for L5: the UK comparison is the strongest available evidence, and should explain WHY the processes differ: because a UK justice cannot strike down statute, the return on capturing an appointment is too low to justify investment. That is a rational-choice explanation, not a cultural one.` },

      { q: '"Judicial review has made the Supreme Court a political institution." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `POLITICAL:\n• Judicial review allows nine unelected justices to override elected majorities on the most contested questions.\n• Decisions correlate strongly with the appointing President's party on abortion, guns, campaign finance and voting rights.\n• Dobbs reversed a fifty-year precedent shortly after a shift in composition, with no change to the text.\n• Rulings on campaign finance and voting shape who wins elections and therefore who makes future appointments.\n• The appointment process is openly partisan, and interest groups campaign for and against nominees.\n\nNOT POLITICAL:\n• The Court cannot initiate cases and does not choose the disputes brought to it.\n• It applies legal reasoning and precedent; most decisions are unanimous or cross-ideological.\n• Justices have frequently disappointed their appointing President.\n• Judicial review protects constitutional limits and minority rights — a legal function, not a policy preference.\n• Where an entrenched Bill of Rights exists, SOMEONE must interpret it; the alternative is that rights mean whatever the legislature says.\n• The Court has no enforcement power and depends on accepted legitimacy.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest conclusion notes that the Court's political character is STRUCTURALLY INEVITABLE given entrenchment plus near-unamendability — the deeper problem is constitutional design, not judicial misbehaviour.` },

      { q: '"Judicial activism is a threat to US democracy." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `A THREAT:\n• Unelected justices with life tenure overturn the decisions of elected majorities.\n• Overturning precedent removes settled expectations — Dobbs affected rights relied upon for fifty years.\n• Constitutional rulings are effectively irreversible, removing questions from democratic revision permanently.\n• Activism has operated in both directions — Brown and Roe from the left, Dobbs, Citizens United and Second Amendment rulings from the right — so no political side can consistently defend it.\n• It politicises appointments, damaging the Court's legitimacy and the Senate's norms.\n\nNOT A THREAT:\n• The label is polemical — each side calls the other activist; it describes a disposition towards intervention, not a political direction.\n• Some activism has protected minorities against hostile majorities, which is precisely what a constitutional court is for — Brown could not have come from a legislature of the period.\n• Restraint has its own costs: deference to elected branches meant Plessy stood for nearly sixty years.\n• The Court cannot choose its cases and has no enforcement power.\n• Congress and the amendment process provide formal reversal routes.\n\nGuidance for L5: must engage the definitional problem — "activism" usually means "rulings I dislike". The strongest answers assess the underlying question of whether courts SHOULD correct democratic majorities, and reach a reasoned position on it.` },
    ]
  },

  '2.5': {
    name: 'Elections and Direct Democracy',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three features of the Electoral College.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Allocation — each state receives electors equal to its House members plus two senators, with DC receiving three, giving 538 in total and 270 needed to win. Analysis: the two-senator element over-represents small states, so a vote in Wyoming carries several times the weight of one in California.\n• Winner-take-all — almost all states award all their electors to the statewide winner; Maine and Nebraska allocate partly by district. Analysis: this is what allows a candidate to win the presidency while losing the popular vote, as in 2000 and 2016.\n• Contingent election — if no candidate reaches 270 the House chooses, with each state delegation casting one vote.\n• Faithless electors — theoretically possible, never decisive, and many states now bind their electors.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires consequences, particularly the swing-state distortion.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three criticisms of the Electoral College.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• It can elect a President who lost the popular vote — five times in total, twice since 2000 (2000 and 2016). Analysis: violates the principle that each vote should carry equal weight.\n• Over-representation of small states — every state receives two electors regardless of population, so a Wyoming vote carries several times the weight of a Californian one.\n• Distorted campaigning — because most states are reliably partisan under winner-take-all, candidates concentrate almost all spending, visits and policy attention on a handful of swing states, while voters elsewhere are effectively ignored, depressing turnout.\n• The contingent election procedure gives each state delegation one vote regardless of population.\n\nGuidance: one-sided by design; require analysis of MECHANISM.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three features of the US presidential nomination process.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Primaries — state-run secret-ballot elections in which registered voters select the party's candidate, which may be closed, open or semi-closed. Analysis: transferred selection from party elites to voters, so parties cannot control who runs under their label.\n• Caucuses — party-run meetings where supporters gather and publicly declare preference. Analysis: demand far more time and commitment, so turnout is low and participants are ideologically committed activists.\n• The invisible primary — the pre-voting period of fundraising, endorsements and name recognition, which effectively screens out those who cannot raise money. Analysis: advantages establishment candidates, though Trump in 2016 showed media attention can substitute.\n• Iowa and New Hampshire's calendar position generating momentum disproportionate to their delegate numbers (they still open the Republican calendar; in 2024 the Democrats made South Carolina their first official primary).\n• The National Convention formally confirming the nominee and adopting the platform.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires the polarisation consequence.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three features of campaign finance in the USA.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• PACs — raise money and donate directly to candidates, subject to strict statutory limits on both receipts and donations.\n• Super PACs — may raise and spend UNLIMITED sums on independent advocacy but may not donate to or coordinate with a campaign. Created after Citizens United (2010) and the SpeechNow ruling.\n• Dark money — 501(c)(4) "social welfare" organisations may spend on political advocacy without disclosing donors, making it impossible to trace who funds political messaging.\n• The constitutional framework — Buckley v Valeo (1976) held spending limits restrict protected speech; Citizens United extended this to corporations, unions and associations.\n\nAnalysis expected: the crucial point is that reform is CONSTITUTIONALLY FORECLOSED — Congress cannot simply legislate limits, unlike the UK which caps campaign spending by ordinary statute.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three forms of direct democracy used in US states.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Initiative — citizens propose a law or state constitutional amendment by collecting signatures, which is then put to a popular vote. Analysis: genuine citizen agenda-setting power the federal system entirely lacks; has delivered change on cannabis, minimum wage and healthcare expansion where legislatures were blocked.\n• Referendum — a measure passed by the legislature referred to voters for approval or rejection.\n• Recall — voters petition to remove an elected official before their term ends, then vote on it; California recalls in 2003 and 2021.\n\nAnalysis expected: criticisms include that qualifying an initiative requires expensive professional signature-gathering, so the mechanism can amplify organised money rather than popular will; and that complex issues are reduced to binary choices without scrutiny or amendment — California's Proposition 13 constrained property tax revenue for decades while other initiatives mandated spending.\n\nNote: there is NO national referendum in the USA.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three reasons why the President\'s party usually loses seats at midterm elections.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Lower turnout favours the more motivated opposition — voters energised against an administration turn out more reliably than those satisfied with it.\n• Midterms function as a referendum on an incumbent administration whose popularity has usually declined from its inauguration peak.\n• Loss of coattails — congressional candidates who benefited from a popular presidential candidate two years earlier no longer have that boost.\n• Regression from an unusually strong presidential-year result in marginal districts.\n\nAnalysis expected: the consequence is structurally important — losing a chamber usually ends the legislative programme and exposes the President to hostile oversight, which is why major legislative achievements are heavily front-loaded into the first two years and why presidents increasingly turn to executive orders thereafter.` },

      { q: '"The Electoral College should be abolished." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `SHOULD BE ABOLISHED:\n• It has twice since 2000 elected a President who lost the popular vote, violating equal weight of votes.\n• Small states are over-represented by the two-senator element.\n• Winner-take-all confines meaningful campaigning to a handful of swing states while millions of votes elsewhere are irrelevant, depressing turnout.\n• Polling consistently shows majority support for a national popular vote.\n• Faithless electors and the contingent election procedure are additional anomalies.\n\nSHOULD NOT BE ABOLISHED:\n• It preserves the FEDERAL character of the union — the USA is a union of states, and the presidential election is a set of state contests.\n• It requires geographically broad coalitions rather than concentration in dense urban areas, protecting smaller states' interests.\n• It produces clear, decisive outcomes and localises disputes — a recount concerns one state (Florida 2000) rather than 150 million ballots nationwide.\n• It reinforces the two-party system, discouraging fragmentation.\n• Abolition requires an amendment ratified by three-quarters of states, including many the system advantages — a practical impossibility.\n\nGuidance for L5: the PRACTICAL obstacle is as important as the principled argument. The National Popular Vote Interstate Compact is a workaround but remains short of 270 and would face immediate legal challenge. An answer that ignores feasibility caps at L4.` },

      { q: '"Money determines the outcome of US elections." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `MONEY DETERMINES:\n• Competitive campaigns cost billions; the invisible primary screens out those who cannot raise funds before a vote is cast.\n• Citizens United enabled unlimited independent expenditure, and outside spending has risen dramatically.\n• Dark money through 501(c)(4) groups conceals who is funding political messaging.\n• Incumbents heavily outspend challengers, contributing to re-election rates above 90% in the House.\n• Donors gain access, and access shapes which issues receive attention.\n\nMONEY DOES NOT DETERMINE:\n• Heavily outspent candidates win — Trump's 2016 campaign was outspent while attracting vast free media coverage, suggesting ATTENTION rather than expenditure is the underlying resource.\n• Spending is often a CONSEQUENCE of viability rather than its cause — donors back candidates who already look likely to win.\n• Partisanship now predicts vote choice more reliably than any campaign variable, so most voters are unpersuadable at any price.\n• Incumbency advantages include name recognition and casework, not only money.\n• Well-funded ballot initiative campaigns frequently lose.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest analysis distinguishes money as a NECESSARY condition for competitiveness from a SUFFICIENT condition for victory, and notes it buys access far more reliably than outcomes.` },

      { q: '"The US primary system is democratic." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `DEMOCRATIC:\n• Transferred candidate selection from party elites in smoke-filled rooms to ordinary voters — a major democratic advance after 1968.\n• Voters, not party machines, determine who appears on the ballot.\n• Outsider candidates can defeat establishment favourites, showing genuine openness — Trump in 2016, Obama in 2008.\n• Open primaries allow independents to participate.\n• Extensive scrutiny of candidates over a long contest.\n\nNOT DEMOCRATIC:\n• Turnout is low, so a small and unrepresentative electorate of committed activists selects nominees.\n• Iowa and New Hampshire's calendar position gives demographically unrepresentative states disproportionate influence.\n• The invisible primary screens out candidates who cannot raise money before a single vote is cast.\n• Closed primaries exclude independents, who are a large share of the electorate.\n• Caucuses demand time and commitment, excluding shift workers and carers.\n• In safe districts the primary IS the election, so a tiny ideological electorate effectively chooses the representative.\n\nGuidance for L5: should note the paradox — primaries are more democratic in FORM than party selection but can produce LESS representative outcomes, and are a principal driver of polarisation. That double-edge is what reaches L5.` },

      { q: '"Direct democracy at state level improves US democracy." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `IMPROVES:\n• Gives citizens genuine agenda-setting power that the federal system entirely lacks — there is no national referendum.\n• Delivers change where legislatures are blocked or captured — cannabis legalisation, minimum wage rises, Medicaid expansion in states whose legislatures refused it.\n• Increases engagement and political education.\n• Recall provides accountability between elections.\n• Allows policy experimentation from which other states learn.\n\nDOES NOT IMPROVE:\n• Qualifying an initiative requires expensive professional signature-gathering, so the mechanism can amplify organised money rather than popular will.\n• Complex issues reduced to binary choices with no committee scrutiny, no amendment and no weighing against other commitments.\n• Voters can approve spending commitments and tax limits simultaneously — California's Proposition 13 constrained property tax revenue for decades while other initiatives mandated spending, contributing to recurring fiscal crises.\n• Majority decisions can override minority rights without deliberative protection — measures on same-sex marriage later struck down by courts.\n• Low-turnout ballots mean measures pass with support from a small fraction of the electorate.\n\nGuidance for L5: should distinguish the DEMOCRATIC THEORY case (strong) from the POLICY-QUALITY case (weak), and reach a conclusion on which matters more.` },
    ]
  },

  '2.6': {
    name: 'US Political Parties',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three differences between the Democratic and Republican parties.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• The role of the federal government in the economy — Democrats support progressive taxation, regulation and social provision (the Affordable Care Act); Republicans favour lower taxes, deregulation and devolution to states and markets. Analysis: the oldest dividing line, tracing to the New Deal realignment, but less stable than it appears given Republican deficits and the recent turn to protectionism.\n• Social and cultural questions — abortion, LGBT rights, gun regulation, immigration and race. Analysis: these have displaced economics as the main predictor of vote choice, and are LESS amenable to compromise than distributive questions, since a budget can be split and a constitutional right cannot.\n• Electoral coalitions — Democrats strong among urban, minority, younger and college-educated voters; Republicans among rural, evangelical, non-college white and older voters.\n• Federalism — but positions here are tactical, tracking where each party currently holds power.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires analysis, not a list of policies.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three reasons why the USA has a two-party system.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Winner-take-all elections — single-member plurality districts and the winner-take-all Electoral College mean dispersed support wins nothing. Duverger's law. Ross Perot won ~19% of the popular vote in 1992 and ZERO electoral votes.\n• Strategic incentives — voters, donors and ambitious candidates converge on the two viable parties, because supporting a third risks helping the least preferred outcome (the spoiler effect, as with Nader in Florida in 2000).\n• Institutional barriers — state-by-state ballot access rules with substantial signature thresholds; presidential debate polling thresholds; campaign finance and primary structures built around the two parties.\n• Absorption — when a third party shows an issue has traction, a major party adopts its position, capturing the support. Populist and Progressive causes were absorbed this way, as more recently were Tea Party concerns.\n• Primaries allow dissenters to contest control of an existing party rather than founding a new one.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires the mechanism, not just the label.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three causes of polarisation in US politics.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Ideological sorting — until the 1960s both parties contained conservative southern Democrats and liberal northern Republicans, making cross-party coalitions routine. Democratic support for civil rights triggered the South's realignment, and each party became internally coherent. Analysis: polarisation is therefore partly the result of parties becoming CONSISTENT, not of anyone becoming more extreme.\n• Safe districts and primary dominance — where the general election is a formality, the only threat is a primary challenge from the flank, so legislators are rewarded for purity and punished for compromise.\n• Media fragmentation — partisan cable news and social media allow citizens to consume information confirming existing views.\n• Ideological donors and groups funding primary challenges; the nationalisation of politics eroding local factors.\n\nGuidance: the sorting point is what separates L3 from L2.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three consequences of polarisation for US government.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Legislative gridlock — the filibuster effectively requires 60 votes, and bipartisan coalitions have become rare, so major legislation frequently fails even under unified government. Analysis: this compounds a constitutional design already built to require broad consensus, raising the effective threshold above what the framers intended.\n• Displacement to the executive and the courts — where Congress cannot legislate, presidents govern by executive order and contested questions are settled by litigation. Analysis: transfers authority from the most representative branch to the two least accountable, and produces oscillation since orders are reversed by successors.\n• Erosion of institutional norms — the treatment of judicial nominations, debt-ceiling brinkmanship and shutdowns reflect willingness to use procedural powers maximally. Analysis: the American system depends on forbearance because its formal rules permit far more obstruction than was historically practised.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires causal analysis.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three arguments that US parties have been renewed rather than have declined.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Party-line voting in Congress is at historic highs, and leadership controls the agenda far more tightly than in the 1970s; the cross-party coalitions that once characterised Congress have largely disappeared.\n• Voter behaviour — partisan identification predicts vote choice more reliably than at any point in decades, split-ticket voting has collapsed, and voters increasingly align positions across issues with their party.\n• Fundraising and infrastructure — national committees raise enormous sums and provide data, analytics and coordinated campaign strategy on a scale not previously possible.\n\nCounter-argument worth crediting: parties remain weak in the respect that matters most — they CANNOT control who runs under their label, since primaries allow voters to nominate candidates the leadership opposes, and candidates fund and organise independently.\n\nAnalysis expected: parties have become stronger as IDEOLOGICAL IDENTITIES and weaker as ORGANISATIONS, so decline and renewal describe different dimensions.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three reasons why third parties struggle in US national elections.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Winner-take-all rules — single-member plurality districts and the Electoral College mean substantial but dispersed support produces nothing. Perot won ~19% of the popular vote in 1992 and no electoral votes at all.\n• The wasted vote and spoiler perception — supporting a third party risks helping the least preferred major candidate, as Nader's candidacy in Florida in 2000 is widely argued to have done, making third-party voting appear counterproductive rather than merely futile.\n• Institutional barriers — differing state ballot access requirements imposing significant signature and legal costs; debate polling thresholds excluding third-party candidates from the largest audiences of the campaign.\n• Absorption of issues — when a third party demonstrates traction, a major party adopts a version of its position, capturing the support and removing the rationale for the insurgent party.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires mechanism and example.` },

      { q: '"US political parties are in decline." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `IN DECLINE:\n• Parties cannot control who runs under their label — primaries allow voters to nominate candidates the leadership actively opposes, as Trump's 2016 nomination demonstrated.\n• Candidate-centred campaigning — candidates build their own organisations, raise their own money and owe little to the party apparatus.\n• No disciplinary equivalent to the UK whip; no power of deselection.\n• Powerful factions such as the Freedom Caucus can hold their own leadership hostage in a narrowly divided chamber.\n• Super PACs and outside groups can support candidates regardless of leadership approval.\n\nRENEWED / NOT IN DECLINE:\n• Party-line voting in Congress is at historic highs, and leadership controls the floor agenda more tightly than in the 1970s.\n• Partisan identification predicts vote choice more reliably than in decades; split-ticket voting has collapsed.\n• National committees raise and coordinate unprecedented sums with sophisticated data operations.\n• Parties are stronger as ideological brands than at any recent point.\n• Straight-ticket voting means congressional results now track presidential ones closely.\n\nGuidance for L5: the resolution is that parties have become STRONGER AS IDEOLOGICAL IDENTITIES and WEAKER AS ORGANISATIONS — decline and renewal describe different dimensions rather than contradicting each other. A conclusion asserting one side caps at L3.` },

      { q: '"Polarisation is the greatest problem facing US politics." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `YES:\n• Produces legislative gridlock, so major problems go unaddressed even under unified government.\n• Displaces policy-making to the executive and the courts — the least accountable branches — and produces oscillation as orders are reversed.\n• Erodes institutional norms: judicial nomination brinkmanship, debt-ceiling standoffs, shutdowns. The system depends on forbearance its formal rules do not require.\n• Makes compromise electorally dangerous, since the real threat is a primary challenge from the flank.\n• Undermines acceptance of election results and of the legitimacy of the other side.\n\nNO — other problems are greater or polarisation is overstated:\n• Money in politics post-Citizens United distorts access and agenda regardless of polarisation.\n• Structural defects are arguably deeper — the Electoral College, Senate malapportionment and near-unamendability would produce dysfunction even without polarisation.\n• Voting rights restrictions after Shelby County affect who participates at all.\n• Polarisation among ELITES exceeds polarisation among voters; many Americans hold mixed views.\n• Some argue clearer party differences improve democratic choice and accountability.\n• Sorting means parties are more coherent, which is arguably a democratic gain.\n\nGuidance for L5: must PRIORITISE and justify, and should consider whether polarisation is a cause or a SYMPTOM of institutional design — the strongest answers argue that winner-take-all rules, primaries and safe districts produce it structurally.` },

      { q: '"Third parties are irrelevant in US politics." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `IRRELEVANT:\n• Winner-take-all rules mean they win virtually nothing — Perot's ~19% in 1992 produced zero electoral votes.\n• No third-party candidate has won the presidency since the party system stabilised; none holds significant congressional representation.\n• Ballot access rules and debate thresholds exclude them from the largest audiences.\n• Voters avoid them for fear of the spoiler effect.\n• Major parties absorb their issues, removing their rationale.\n\nNOT IRRELEVANT:\n• They can be DECISIVE by splitting a major party's vote — Nader in Florida in 2000 is widely argued to have changed the outcome of a presidential election.\n• They raise issues the major parties are avoiding, and absorption is itself evidence of influence — Populist and Progressive causes entered the mainstream this way.\n• Perot's deficit focus shaped the 1990s fiscal debate despite winning nothing.\n• They serve as a protest outlet and an indicator of dissatisfaction that major parties must address.\n• At state and local level they occasionally win office.\n\nGuidance for L5: must distinguish ELECTORAL irrelevance (largely true) from AGENDA-SETTING relevance (demonstrably false). The absorption mechanism is the strongest evidence that influence and representation are different things.` },

      { q: '"The two-party system serves US democracy well." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `SERVES WELL:\n• Produces clear governing alternatives and simple, comprehensible choices for voters.\n• Broad coalition parties must moderate to assemble national majorities, historically pulling both towards the centre.\n• Delivers stable government and clear accountability — voters know whom to reward or punish.\n• Avoids the fragmentation and coalition instability of multi-party systems.\n• Primaries provide an internal outlet for dissent without fragmenting the system.\n\nDOES NOT SERVE WELL:\n• Millions of voters have no party representing their views; independents are the largest self-identified group.\n• Dispersed support wins nothing, so entire constituencies go unrepresented.\n• Polarisation has destroyed the moderating incentive — safe districts and primaries now reward ideological purity rather than centrism.\n• Absence of alternatives means dissatisfaction has no electoral outlet, feeding anti-system sentiment.\n• The spoiler effect actively punishes voters for expressing genuine preferences.\n• Reform is structurally blocked by the very parties the system advantages.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest analysis notes that the classic defence of two-party systems — that they force moderation — has been FALSIFIED by polarisation, since primaries and safe districts now reward the opposite. That empirical move reaches L5.` },
    ]
  },

  '2.7': {
    name: 'US Pressure Groups',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three methods used by US pressure groups to influence policy.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Lobbying — members of Congress, congressional staff and federal agencies, often supplying draft legislative language. Analysis: the most consistently effective method, because it operates at the drafting stage and because Congress's own research capacity has declined relative to what it regulates — a dependence that is itself the concern.\n• Electioneering — PAC donations, unlimited Super PAC expenditure, scorecards mobilising single-issue voters, and primary funding. Analysis: distinctively powerful because primaries determine outcomes in most safe districts, so a group able to fund a challenger holds leverage over a legislator's career.\n• Litigation — test cases and amicus curiae briefs. Analysis: can deliver permanent nationwide change beyond legislative reversal, as the NAACP's strategy culminating in Brown showed.\n• Grassroots mobilisation and direct action, including astroturfing.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires evaluation of when each works.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three reasons why pressure groups are powerful in the USA.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Multiple access points — separation of powers means a bill must pass the House, pass the Senate, survive the filibuster, be signed and survive judicial review; federalism adds fifty state governments. A group needs to succeed at only ONE veto point to block.\n• Weak party discipline — legislators are nominated in primaries and fund their own campaigns, so they vote independently. Persuading or funding one member can genuinely change an outcome.\n• Candidate-centred, expensive elections make legislators structurally dependent on continuous fundraising.\n• Entrenched, judicially enforceable rights make litigation a route to permanent change.\n• First Amendment protection of speech, assembly and petition gives group activity explicit constitutional standing.\n\nAnalysis expected: the crucial qualification is that many access points are also many VETO points, so US groups are better at blocking than achieving.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which pressure groups influence Congress.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Direct lobbying of members and staff — supplying research, technical expertise and frequently draft legislative language, at the stage before positions become public and entrenched.\n• Campaign funding through PACs and independent expenditure through Super PACs, creating dependence given the cost of competitive campaigns.\n• Scorecards and primary threats — rating legislators' voting records to mobilise single-issue voters. The NRA's effectiveness rested less on total spending than on a membership that would vote on that issue alone in primaries, making opposition dangerous.\n• Iron triangles — stable relationships between a committee, an agency and a group, insulating a policy area from wider scrutiny.\n• Grassroots mobilisation generating constituent contact.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires the primaries mechanism, which is what makes US group leverage distinctive.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three reasons why US pressure groups use litigation.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• It can deliver permanent nationwide change — because the Constitution is entrenched and judicially enforceable, a ruling that a law is unconstitutional applies across the country and can realistically be reversed only by the Court itself or by an unattainable amendment. No legislative victory offers comparable security.\n• It bypasses hostile majorities — litigation requires only a plaintiff with standing and a strong legal argument rather than electoral or congressional majorities. The NAACP's strategy culminating in Brown achieved what a Congress dependent on southern Democratic votes would not deliver.\n• Methods — carefully selected test cases with sympathetic facts, funding litigation, and amicus curiae briefs in cases brought by others.\n\nLimitations worth crediting: rulings depend on the Court's composition and can be reversed (Dobbs); judgments require executive and state cooperation, as the slow desegregation after Brown showed; litigation is expensive, favouring well-resourced groups.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three criticisms of the influence of US pressure groups.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Influence tracks resources rather than numbers — post-Citizens United unlimited independent expenditure means wealthy individuals, corporations and unions dominate political communication, inverting the democratic principle that a diffuse majority should prevail over a well-funded minority.\n• Dark money — 501(c)(4) organisations conceal donors, preventing voters from discounting messages by their source.\n• Iron triangles and the revolving door — stable committee–agency–group relationships, reinforced by officials moving into industry roles, place whole policy areas beyond effective public scrutiny.\n• Groups are unelected, need not be internally democratic, and bear no responsibility for consequences.\n\nAnalysis expected: the most sophisticated criticism is that the FRAGMENTATION of the American system makes it impossible for voters to identify who is responsible for an outcome — so the accountability failure lies in institutional design.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which iron triangles operate in the USA.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — the three participants and the exchange between them:\n• The interest group supplies campaign funding, technical expertise and political support.\n• The congressional committee or subcommittee supplies favourable legislation, appropriations and sympathetic oversight.\n• The federal agency supplies implementation favourable to the group's interests.\n\nAnalysis expected:\n• The arrangement is mutually beneficial and therefore STABLE — all three participants benefit from the status quo, which makes change difficult.\n• It insulates a policy area from wider political scrutiny, since no participant has an incentive to expose it. Defence procurement and agriculture are the standard examples.\n• The accountability problem: decisions are made by participants who all benefit and none of whom faces the public directly for the outcome.\n• Qualification worth crediting: the model fits some areas far better than others — ISSUE NETWORKS in contested fields such as healthcare and the environment are far more open and competitive.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires the qualification as well as the model.` },

      { q: '"US pressure groups strengthen democracy." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `STRENGTHEN:\n• Continuous representation between elections; intense minorities get a voice a majoritarian system cannot register.\n• Supply legislators with expertise Congress increasingly lacks.\n• Multiple access points mean defeat in one arena is not final, so a wide range of interests can be heard.\n• Mobilise and educate citizens; the ACA repeal defeat in 2017 showed grassroots mobilisation beating a better-funded effort.\n• Litigation allows minorities to secure rights hostile majorities would deny — the NAACP and Brown.\n• First Amendment protection gives group activity constitutional standing.\n\nWEAKEN:\n• Influence tracks resources, not numbers — post-Citizens United spending inverts the democratic principle.\n• Dark money conceals who funds political messaging.\n• Iron triangles and the revolving door insulate policy areas from scrutiny.\n• Groups are unelected, unaccountable and bear no responsibility for consequences.\n• Well-organised minorities can defeat diffuse majorities — the NRA on gun regulation despite majority support for specific measures.\n\nGuidance for L5: must use the PLURALIST/ELITIST framework. The strongest conclusion is that the system is pluralist in form and elitist in effect, and that the deeper accountability failure lies in the institutional fragmentation that makes group influence so effective in the first place.` },

      { q: '"Pressure groups are more powerful than parties in the USA." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `GROUPS MORE POWERFUL:\n• Weak party discipline means legislators answer to donors and primary electorates rather than to party leadership.\n• Super PACs can outspend party committees and support candidates regardless of leadership approval.\n• Groups can threaten a legislator's renomination through primary funding — a lever the party itself lacks, since parties cannot deselect.\n• Litigation allows groups to achieve permanent change parties could not legislate.\n• Iron triangles operate independently of party control.\n• Groups such as the NRA and AARP have shaped policy across administrations of both parties.\n\nPARTIES MORE POWERFUL:\n• Only parties contest elections and organise government; group influence is exercised THROUGH party officeholders.\n• Party-line voting in Congress is at historic highs, so partisanship predicts votes better than group pressure.\n• Party leadership controls the floor agenda and committee assignments.\n• Partisan identification predicts vote choice more reliably than any group endorsement.\n• Groups align with parties rather than transcending them — polarisation has made most major groups effectively partisan.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest analysis notes the two have MERGED — polarisation has made most major groups reliably partisan, so the question of which is more powerful is less meaningful than it was when cross-party coalitions existed.` },

      { q: '"Citizens United has damaged US democracy." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `DAMAGED:\n• Enabled unlimited independent expenditure by corporations, unions and associations, and outside spending rose dramatically.\n• Shifted money away from candidate control towards Super PACs and dark-money groups, so voters cannot identify who is funding messaging.\n• Amplifies the influence of the wealthy relative to ordinary citizens, inverting the democratic principle.\n• Reform is now CONSTITUTIONALLY FORECLOSED — Congress cannot legislate limits unless the Court reverses itself or an amendment passes.\n• Increases legislators' dependence on outside money and the groups that supply it.\n\nNOT DAMAGED / defensible:\n• Restricting political spending IS restricting political speech — the First Amendment principle is serious, not a pretext.\n• The ruling applies equally to unions, advocacy groups and non-profits, not only corporations, so it is not straightforwardly pro-business.\n• Spending does not determine outcomes — heavily outspent candidates win, and Trump was outspent in 2016.\n• Independent expenditure is not coordinated with campaigns, so the corruption risk is lower than direct donation.\n• Money was already central to US elections before 2010.\n\nGuidance for L5: the constitutional point is the strongest material — the problem cannot be legislated away, unlike in the UK where campaign spending is capped by ordinary statute. That comparison reaches L5.` },

      { q: '"US pressure group politics is pluralist." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `PLURALIST (Dahl):\n• Any interest that organises can enter the competition; group numbers and diversity are enormous.\n• Separation of powers and federalism create many access points, so no single group dominates every arena.\n• Government arbitrates between competing demands, and outcomes frequently reflect compromise.\n• Poorly resourced groups do win — the 2017 ACA repeal was defeated substantially by grassroots mobilisation.\n• Litigation gives minorities a route to victory without majorities.\n\nELITIST (Mills):\n• Influence follows money, organisation and access rather than numbers.\n• Business enjoys STRUCTURAL leverage — governments depend on investment and employment decisions, so the anticipated reaction of business constrains policy without any lobbying at all.\n• Post-Citizens United spending and dark money entrench the advantage of the wealthy.\n• Iron triangles and the revolving door concentrate access among insiders.\n• Diffuse public interests are consistently defeated by concentrated private ones.\n\nGuidance for L5: the defensible conclusion is that the system is PLURALIST IN FORM and ELITIST IN EFFECT — group competition is genuine, but pluralism assumes rough parity of access and that assumption fails. Structural power is the decisive concept and should be named.` },
    ]
  },

  '2.8': {
    name: 'Civil Rights',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three constitutional protections of civil rights in the USA.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• The Fourteenth Amendment (1868) — citizenship by birth, due process and equal protection of the laws, binding on the STATES. Analysis: the basis of most modern civil rights litigation, including Brown and Obergefell.\n• The Thirteenth Amendment (1865) — abolished slavery and involuntary servitude.\n• The Fifteenth Amendment (1870) — no denial of the vote on grounds of race, colour or previous servitude.\n• Later amendments — the Nineteenth (1920, women's suffrage), Twenty-Fourth (1964, poll taxes) and Twenty-Sixth (1971, voting at 18).\n• The Bill of Rights, incorporated against the states through the Fourteenth Amendment.\n\nAnalysis expected: the Reconstruction amendments lay SUBSTANTIALLY DORMANT for nearly a century — Plessy (1896) upheld segregation under the very amendment Brown (1954) later used to strike it down. Text alone achieves nothing without institutional will.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which civil rights have been advanced in the USA.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Constitutional amendment — the Thirteenth, Fourteenth and Fifteenth. Analysis: the most secure route, beyond legislative reversal and judicial reinterpretation, but practically unattainable today.\n• Judicial interpretation — Brown (1954), Loving, Obergefell (2015), Bostock (2020). Analysis: the most productive route, requiring no legislative majority and delivering immediate nationwide effect — invaluable when elected branches were hostile. But Dobbs showed what one Court grants another can withdraw.\n• Legislation and mass mobilisation — the Civil Rights Act 1964 and Voting Rights Act 1965, produced by sustained non-violent direct action. Analysis: reaches PRIVATE discrimination in employment, housing and public accommodation, which constitutional rulings cannot, and supplies enforcement machinery.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires analysis of the DIFFERENT SECURITY PROFILE of each route.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three methods used by the US civil rights movement.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Litigation — the NAACP Legal Defense Fund pursued carefully chosen test cases culminating in Brown (1954). Analysis: highly effective at establishing legal principle, delivering a nationwide ruling no legislature of the period would have produced given southern Democratic power in Congress; but it could not compel implementation.\n• Non-violent direct action — the Montgomery bus boycott, sit-ins, freedom rides, the 1963 March on Washington. Analysis: generated the political pressure litigation could not; televised images of peaceful protesters met with violence shifted northern public opinion decisively.\n• Legislative lobbying — securing the Civil Rights Act 1964 and Voting Rights Act 1965, which supplied enforcement machinery and reached private discrimination.\n\nAnalysis expected: NEITHER method would have succeeded alone. Each addressed a different obstacle, and the movement's effectiveness lay in combining them.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which voting rights have been restricted in the USA.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Historically — literacy tests, poll taxes, grandfather clauses and violence used to suppress black voting under Jim Crow, despite the Fifteenth Amendment.\n• Shelby County v Holder (2013) — struck down the VRA coverage formula, rendering pre-clearance inoperative. Analysis: several formerly covered states subsequently introduced stricter voter ID, reduced early voting, closed polling places and purged rolls; these now take effect immediately and can only be challenged afterwards, placing cost and burden on the challenger.\n• Partisan gerrymandering — Rucho v Common Cause (2019) held such claims non-justiciable in federal courts, leaving remedy to state constitutions and ballot initiatives.\n• Felon disenfranchisement, which disproportionately affects minority communities.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires the Shelby County mechanism, not just the case name.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three arguments against affirmative action.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• The constitutional objection — treating individuals differently by race is itself racial discrimination and sits uneasily with the equal protection clause's guarantee to every person. Analysis: this reasoning prevailed in Students for Fair Admissions v Harvard (2023), ending race-conscious university admissions.\n• Stigma — preferences may lead others, and beneficiaries themselves, to doubt that success was earned on merit. Analysis: the policy can undermine the very equality of esteem it aims to create.\n• Harm to other groups — it can disadvantage applicants from other minority groups, such as the Asian American applicants whose case was central in SFFA. Analysis: race-conscious policy creates new losers rather than simply correcting past wrongs.\n• The wrong target — race is a poor proxy for disadvantage, so policies based on income or class would reach the disadvantaged more accurately. Analysis: many critics favour class-based alternatives rather than no remedy at all.\n\nGuidance: credit accurate case knowledge (Bakke 1978, Grutter 2003, SFFA 2023). The balanced debate belongs in 25-mark answers; here three developed arguments against are required.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which federalism affects civil rights in the USA.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Division of authority — the Tenth Amendment reserves elections, education, policing, criminal justice and (since Dobbs) abortion regulation to the states, so many decisions affecting rights are taken at state level.\n• A historical shield for discrimination — states' rights arguments were used to resist federal intervention throughout the Jim Crow era; it required the Fourteenth Amendment, Supreme Court rulings and eventually federal legislation and troops to overcome state resistance.\n• Contemporary variation — after Dobbs, abortion access differs radically across state lines; voting procedures, LGBT protections and policing standards likewise differ, so a citizen's practical entitlements depend substantially on residence.\n• Federalism can also ADVANCE rights — several states recognised same-sex marriage before Obergefell, and states have legislated protections exceeding the federal floor.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires the point that federalism cuts both ways.` },

      { q: '"Civil rights are adequately protected in the USA." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `ADEQUATELY PROTECTED:\n• Rights are constitutionally entrenched, beyond legislative repeal, and judicially enforceable against both federal and state government.\n• Landmark statutes — the Civil Rights Act 1964 and Voting Rights Act 1965 — reach private discrimination with federal enforcement.\n• The Court has at times EXTENDED protections: Obergefell (2015) on same-sex marriage, Bostock (2020) on employment discrimination — though more recent rulings have run the other way.\n• Federal courts remain available to challenge state action.\n• Pressure groups can litigate effectively, as the NAACP did.\n\nNOT ADEQUATELY PROTECTED:\n• Shelby County (2013) disabled pre-clearance, after which several states introduced restrictions; Congress has not restored it.\n• Rucho (2019) made partisan gerrymandering non-justiciable federally.\n• Dobbs (2022) removed a federal right recognised for fifty years, and the concurring reasoning invited reconsideration of others founded on similar grounds.\n• SFFA (2023) removed a principal tool for addressing accumulated disadvantage.\n• United States v Skrmetti (2025) upheld a state ban on gender-affirming care for minors; in January 2025 Trump revoked EO 11246 (federal-contractor affirmative action) and ordered federal DEI programmes ended.\n• Federalism means rights vary substantially by state.\n• Formal equality has not delivered substantive equality in wealth, incarceration, policing outcomes or health.\n\nGuidance for L5: must engage the structural point that rights depending on INTERPRETATION depend on nine appointments — so their security tracks Senate control and vacancy timing. The strongest conclusions also distinguish formal from substantive equality.` },

      { q: '"The Supreme Court has been the most important driver of civil rights in the USA." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `THE COURT MOST IMPORTANT:\n• Brown (1954) delivered nationwide change no legislature of the period would have produced, given southern Democratic power in Congress.\n• Litigation bypasses hostile majorities — it needs only a plaintiff with standing and a strong argument.\n• Court rulings apply immediately and nationwide, and are reversible only by the Court or an unattainable amendment.\n• Obergefell (2015) and Bostock (2020) show the Court still extending rights where Congress would not.\n• Incorporation subjected state action to federal constitutional standards.\n\nOTHER DRIVERS MORE IMPORTANT:\n• Mass mobilisation created the political conditions for change — televised violence against peaceful protesters shifted opinion decisively; Brown alone produced a decade of resistance requiring federal troops at Little Rock.\n• Legislation reaches PRIVATE discrimination and supplies enforcement and funding, which constitutional rulings cannot.\n• The Court has also OBSTRUCTED rights — Plessy (1896) upheld segregation for nearly sixty years; Shelby County and Dobbs removed protections.\n• Constitutional amendment provided the foundation on which everything else rested.\n• Executive action — Truman desegregating the armed forces; Eisenhower at Little Rock.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest answers argue the routes were INTERDEPENDENT — litigation established principle, mobilisation created political conditions, legislation supplied enforcement — and that isolating one misunderstands how the change occurred.` },

      { q: '"Voting rights are under threat in the USA." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `UNDER THREAT:\n• Shelby County (2013) disabled VRA pre-clearance; several formerly covered states then introduced stricter voter ID, reduced early voting, closed polling places and purged rolls.\n• The shift from prior approval to after-the-fact litigation places cost and burden on challengers, and restrictions operate through at least one election cycle meanwhile.\n• Rucho (2019) removed federal remedy for partisan gerrymandering.\n• Louisiana v Callais (2026) held that race-based districting to satisfy section 2 of the VRA was an unconstitutional racial gerrymander; section 2 claims now effectively require proof of intentional discrimination.\n• Felon disenfranchisement disproportionately affects minority communities.\n• Congress has failed to pass replacement voting rights legislation.\n• Increasing contestation of election results and administration.\n\nNOT UNDER THREAT / overstated:\n• Turnout among affected groups has in some cases RISEN since 2013, so the practical effect is contested even where the legal weakening is not.\n• Voter ID is common in other democracies and commands broad public support.\n• Courts have struck down some egregious practices under section 2 of the VRA (Allen v Milligan, 2023) — though Louisiana v Callais (2026) has since sharply narrowed that route.\n• Some states have EXPANDED access — automatic registration, mail voting, early voting.\n• The 2020 election saw record turnout.\n\nGuidance for L5: must distinguish LEGAL weakening (clear) from EMPIRICAL effect on participation (contested), and reach a reasoned position on which matters. Noting that turnout data cannot settle the question — because it cannot show who was deterred — is an L5 observation.` },

      { q: '"Affirmative action was necessary in the USA." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `NECESSARY:\n• Formal legal equality cannot dissolve compounding disadvantages in wealth, education and networks produced by centuries of slavery, segregation and discrimination.\n• A rule treating unequally situated people identically perpetuates the inequality rather than correcting it.\n• Diversity has recognised educational value (accepted in Bakke and Grutter) and improves professional effectiveness and public trust.\n• It produced measurable increases in minority representation in higher education and the professions.\n• Without it, institutions reproduced existing advantage automatically.\n\nNOT NECESSARY / objectionable:\n• Treating individuals differently by race is itself racial discrimination, in tension with the equal protection clause's guarantee to EVERY person — the reasoning that prevailed in SFFA (2023).\n• May stigmatise beneficiaries by casting doubt on their achievement.\n• Disadvantages other minority groups, notably Asian American applicants.\n• Targets race rather than ECONOMIC disadvantage, which may be the more accurate measure of need.\n• Class-based alternatives could achieve similar ends without the constitutional objection.\n• Quotas were held unconstitutional as early as Bakke (1978).\n\nGuidance for L5: must recognise this is not a factual disagreement but a dispute about WHAT EQUALITY REQUIRES — equal treatment of individuals versus equal outcomes between groups — and is therefore unlikely to be settled by evidence. That meta-point reaches L5.` },
    ]
  },

  '2.9': {
    name: 'Comparative — Constitutional Arrangements',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three differences between the UK and US constitutions.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three, each with EXPLICIT comparison:\n• Codification — the US constitution is a single codified document (1787); the UK's is uncodified, drawn from statute, common law, convention and works of authority. Analysis (structural): determines whether there is a higher law for courts to enforce.\n• Entrenchment — US amendment requires a double supermajority (two-thirds of Congress, three-quarters of states); UK constitutional law changes by ordinary Act. Analysis: the FTPA was passed in 2011 and repealed in 2022; only 27 US amendments since 1789.\n• Sovereignty — parliamentary sovereignty in the UK; popular sovereignty expressed through an entrenched constitution in the USA, with power divided federally.\n• Judicial power — the US Court may strike down legislation; the UK Court may only interpret or declare incompatibility.\n• Separation vs fusion of powers.\n\nGuidance: comparative questions require EXPLICIT comparison throughout, not two separate descriptions.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three similarities between the UK and US constitutions.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Both provide for an independent judiciary, protected by security of tenure and salaries insulated from political manipulation, and both have seen courts become markedly more assertive in reviewing executive action since the late twentieth century.\n• Both are liberal democracies operating a common-law tradition with binding precedent, regular free elections, the rule of law and accountable government.\n• Both disperse power territorially — federalism in the USA, devolution in the UK — and in both the centre–region boundary is genuinely contested.\n• Both have become more rights-focused — the Bill of Rights and the Human Rights Act respectively — and in both, governments have responded to adverse rulings by proposing to curb judicial power.\n• Both give the legislature control over public finance.\n\nGuidance: answers listing only differences cannot access the top band on a similarities question.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which the structural approach explains differences between the UK and US constitutions.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Entrenchment explains judicial power — a codified, entrenched US constitution provides a higher law against which statute can be measured, so the Court can strike legislation down; the UK has no such standard, so its Court can only interpret or declare incompatibility. Given the same constitution, any court would acquire the same power.\n• Veto points explain the rate of change — the US double supermajority plus separation of powers, bicameralism, federalism and the filibuster means a proposal must clear numerous independent institutions. The UK has almost none, so devolution, the HRA and the Supreme Court were all delivered within single parliamentary terms.\n• Fusion vs separation explains executive–legislative relations — a UK PM commands a majority by definition; a US President has no guaranteed majority and faces divided government.\n\nGuidance: the structural approach must be NAMED and APPLIED, not merely implied.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which the cultural approach explains differences between the UK and US constitutions.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Founding experience — the USA was created through revolution against arbitrary and distant rule, producing enduring distrust of concentrated power, expressed in a written text limiting government and guaranteeing rights. The UK constitution evolved gradually through negotiated settlements with no single founding moment at which a comprehensive text could have been written.\n• Rights discourse — American political argument is framed as individual rights asserted against government, so moral questions become constitutional claims litigated in court. The British tradition is one of RESIDUAL LIBERTIES with remedy sought through Parliament.\n• Pragmatism versus principle — British constitutional development is incremental and empirical (Burke, Oakeshott); American constitutionalism appeals to founding principle and original meaning.\n\nLimitation worth crediting: culture and structure are hard to separate, because the institutions were CHOSEN because of these assumptions and then reinforced them. Culture explains the ORIGIN of institutions; structure explains their consequences.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which the rational approach explains constitutional differences between the UK and the USA.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Why UK governments resist codification — it would bind the governing party's own future freedom of action and transfer power to judges. A party in office has no incentive to surrender flexibility, which explains why every party promises constitutional reform in opposition and delivers less in power.\n• Why US amendments fail — small states would have to consent to their own disadvantage to reform the Electoral College or Senate apportionment, so rational self-interest explains the persistence of provisions with majority public opposition.\n• Why UK reform has been piecemeal — reforms delivered since 1997 were those that suited the governing party at the time (devolution, Lords hereditary removal, the HRA), while the Westminster electoral system, which advantages both main parties, was untouched.\n• Why US appointments are politicised — where one appointment can determine policy for decades, heavy investment and the abandonment of norms are rational.\n\nGuidance: the rational approach must be NAMED and APPLIED.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which the UK constitution has become more like the US constitution.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Territorial dispersal of power — devolution created legislatures with primary law-making powers producing genuine divergence, resembling US federalism in practice; the Scotland Act 2016 declared the Scottish Parliament permanent. Analysis: the difference that remains is decisive — devolved powers are delegated and not constitutionally entrenched.\n• A separate, more assertive judiciary — the CRA 2005 created a Supreme Court physically and institutionally separate from the legislature, removed the Law Lords from Parliament and established an independent appointments commission, sharpening the separation of powers along US lines.\n• A rights framework — the HRA performs some of the functions of a bill of rights, and has made the courts a significant rights actor.\n• Greater use of referendums, introducing a source of popular authority alongside parliamentary sovereignty.\n\nAnalysis expected: parliamentary sovereignty survives all of these, so the UK has moved in a US direction WITHOUT adopting the defining feature.` },

      { q: '"The US constitution is superior to the UK constitution." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `US SUPERIOR:\n• Entrenchment protects rights and institutions from temporary majorities; UK rights rest on repealable statute.\n• Clarity — citizens can read the rules; disputes turn on a text rather than contested convention, as the 2019 prorogation dispute showed.\n• Genuine limits on the executive — the "elective dictatorship" problem does not arise where the legislature is separately elected.\n• The federal bargain is constitutionally protected; devolved nations were overridden by the UK Internal Market Act 2020.\n• Judicial review provides a real remedy against unconstitutional legislation.\n\nUK SUPERIOR:\n• Flexibility — adapts rapidly without constitutional crisis; devolution, the HRA and the Supreme Court delivered within single parliamentary terms, whereas the US has 27 amendments in 240 years.\n• Democratic authorship — elected politicians rather than appointed judges make constitutional choices.\n• Avoids gridlock — a government with a majority can actually govern.\n• No obsolete provisions survive against majority wishes, unlike the Electoral College.\n• Avoids the politicisation of judicial appointments that Dobbs and the Garland/Barrett episode illustrate.\n\nGuidance for L5: must refuse the framing — flexibility and insecurity are the SAME property; entrenchment and rigidity are the SAME property. "Which is superior" therefore reduces to which risk one would rather run: a government that can do too much, or one that can do too little. That reframing is what reaches L5.` },

      { q: '"Structural factors best explain the differences between the UK and US constitutions." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `STRUCTURAL BEST:\n• Entrenchment alone explains most observable differences — judicial strike-down power, the finality of rulings, and the rate of constitutional change.\n• Veto point counting explains why the US changes so rarely and the UK so readily.\n• Fusion vs separation explains executive–legislative relations, gridlock and party discipline.\n• The causal chain is direct, observable and testable, unlike cultural claims.\n• Where the UK uses proportional systems at devolved level, multi-party systems emerge from the same electorate — strong evidence that institutions, not culture, drive outcomes.\n\nOTHER APPROACHES NEEDED:\n• Structure treats institutions as GIVEN and cannot explain why each country adopted the institutions it has — the cultural approach fills exactly that gap, tracing US entrenchment to a revolutionary founding and UK flexibility to evolutionary development.\n• The rational approach explains variation over TIME and between individuals, which structure (being static) handles poorly — why UK governments resist codification, why US amendments fail, why appointments are politicised.\n• Cultural factors explain why American rights politics is more absolute and less amenable to compromise.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest conclusion is that the three are COMPLEMENTARY rather than competing — culture explains why the institutions exist, structure explains the constraints they impose, rational choice explains how actors behave within them. An answer defending one approach exclusively caps at L4.` },

      { q: '"The UK is converging on the US constitutional model." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `CONVERGING:\n• Devolution disperses power territorially, resembling federalism in practice; the Scotland Act 2016 declared the Scottish Parliament permanent.\n• The CRA 2005 created a separate Supreme Court, removed the Law Lords from Parliament and established independent judicial appointments — sharpening the separation of powers.\n• The HRA created a rights framework performing some functions of a bill of rights, and made courts significant rights actors.\n• Greater use of referendums introduced popular authority alongside parliamentary sovereignty.\n• UK pressure groups increasingly litigate, as US groups do.\n\nNOT CONVERGING:\n• Parliamentary sovereignty survives ALL of these — courts still cannot strike down statute.\n• Rights are not entrenched; the HRA is repealable by simple majority and repeatedly threatened.\n• Devolved powers can legally be reclaimed — Miller I held Sewel unenforceable and the UK Internal Market Act 2020 overrode withheld consent.\n• The UK retains fusion of powers; the executive is drawn from and sits in the legislature.\n• Parliament reversed judicial expansion in 2022, demonstrating the hierarchy is unchanged.\n• The UK has moved AWAY from the US model in retaining FPTP and an appointed second chamber.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest conclusion is that the changes are significant but NOT transformative, because the UK has adopted US-style institutions without the feature that defines the US model — entrenchment. Naming that single variable reaches L5.` },

      { q: '"Entrenchment is the most significant difference between the UK and US constitutions." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `ENTRENCHMENT MOST SIGNIFICANT:\n• It determines judicial power — a higher law gives US courts something to measure statute against and makes their rulings effectively final; without it UK courts can only interpret or declare incompatibility.\n• It determines the rate and mechanism of constitutional change — the US changes through judicial interpretation because amendment is unattainable; the UK changes through ordinary legislation.\n• It determines the security of rights — US rights are beyond legislative repeal but vulnerable to reinterpretation; UK rights are the reverse.\n• It determines the politics of appointments — the return on capturing a US appointment is decades of policy influence, which is why norms have collapsed there and not in the UK.\n• Most other differences can be derived from it.\n\nOTHER DIFFERENCES AS SIGNIFICANT:\n• Fusion vs separation of powers explains executive dominance in the UK and gridlock in the USA — a difference entrenchment does not explain.\n• Federalism vs devolution determines whether regional powers can be overridden.\n• Codification itself affects clarity and public understanding independently of entrenchment.\n• The electoral systems and party discipline differences flow from separation, not entrenchment.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest answers distinguish differences entrenchment EXPLAINS (judicial power, rights security, appointment politics) from those it does NOT (executive–legislative relations, party discipline), and conclude accordingly.` },
    ]
  },

  '2.10': {
    name: 'Comparative — The Executives',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three differences between the UK Prime Minister and the US President.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three, each with EXPLICIT comparison:\n• Selection and relationship to the legislature — the President is separately elected via the Electoral College and does not sit in Congress; the PM is the leader of the party commanding a Commons majority and sits in Parliament. Analysis (structural): fusion means a PM by definition has a legislative majority; separation means a President may not.\n• Tenure — the President serves a fixed four-year term, limited to two, removable only by impeachment and conviction (never achieved); the PM has no fixed term and can be removed by their own party at any time. Thatcher, May, Johnson and Truss all left without losing a general election.\n• Powers — the President holds a veto and is head of state as well as head of government; the PM has neither, but commands the legislature through the whips.\n• The cabinet — collective and capable of ending a premiership in the UK; individually appointed advisers in the USA.\n\nGuidance: comparative questions require explicit comparison, not two separate accounts.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three similarities between the UK and US executives.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Both are head of government, directing national policy, representing the country abroad and commanding the armed forces.\n• Both have grown substantially more powerful over the twentieth century — the expansion of the state, the demands of war and crisis, and media focus on a single national leader.\n• Both can act UNILATERALLY without new legislation — executive orders in the USA and the royal prerogative in the UK are functionally comparable, and both have been constrained by the courts (Miller I and II; Youngstown; Biden v Nebraska).\n• Both depend on large personal staff organisations that have grown substantially — EXOP and the PM's Office/Cabinet Office.\n• Both face their WEAKEST checks in foreign and security policy.\n\nGuidance: a similarities question answered with differences cannot access the top band.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three reasons why a US President may find it harder to pass legislation than a UK Prime Minister.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Separation of powers — the President is elected separately from Congress and has no guaranteed majority; divided government has been the norm for much of the post-war period. A PM by definition commands a Commons majority.\n• Multiple veto points — a bill must pass the House, pass the Senate, survive the filibuster's effective 60-vote threshold and survive judicial review. A UK government faces a Lords that can delay only one year and no court able to strike down statute.\n• Weak party discipline — even under unified government, US legislators nominated in primaries and funding their own campaigns may defy the President without career penalty; Obama's difficulties passing the ACA illustrate this. UK MPs depend on the party for selection, the label and promotion.\n\nAnalysis expected: the difference is INSTITUTIONAL, not personal — the same individual would face entirely different constraints in the other system.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which the rational approach explains differences between the two executives.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Why US legislators defy a President of their own party — they win nomination in primaries, raise their own money and cannot be deselected by the national party. The career risk is a primary challenge from the ideological flank, so supporting an unpopular President can cost them their seat while defying one costs nothing.\n• Why UK MPs rarely rebel — they depend on the party for selection, for the label delivering most of their vote, and for promotion via the whips. Rebellion risks deselection and ends ministerial prospects, and fusion means a major rebellion can trigger an election that costs them their seat.\n• Why PMs appoint rivals to cabinet — dangerous figures are safer inside the government, bound by collective responsibility, than outside it. The constraint operates BEFORE any appointment is made.\n• Why presidents govern by executive order — it is the only route with a positive expected return when legislation cannot pass.\n\nGuidance: the rational approach must be named and applied.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which the two executives conduct foreign and security policy.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Deployment of armed forces — the President as Commander-in-Chief has acted without a declaration of war since 1942, with the War Powers Resolution 1973 largely unenforced because Congress will not cut funding for deployed troops. The PM deploys under the royal prerogative, though a convention of Commons consultation has developed since 2003 and the 2013 Syria vote blocked action.\n• Treaties — US treaties require two-thirds Senate ratification, a threshold that has defeated major agreements, so presidents increasingly use EXECUTIVE AGREEMENTS that a successor can abandon (the Iran deal, the Paris accord). UK treaty-making is a prerogative power with limited parliamentary involvement.\n• Institutional support — the National Security Council within EXOP centralises US foreign policy in the White House; the UK equivalent is far smaller.\n\nAnalysis expected: BOTH executives face their weakest legislative checks here, which is why the "imperial" charge fits foreign policy better than domestic policy in both systems.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three differences between the UK and US cabinets.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Constitutional status — the UK cabinet is a COLLECTIVE decision-making body bound by collective responsibility; US secretaries are INDIVIDUALLY appointed advisers to a President in whom all executive power is constitutionally vested.\n• Relationship to the legislature — UK ministers are drawn from and sit in Parliament; US secretaries do not sit in Congress and require Senate confirmation.\n• Power over the chief executive — the UK cabinet's withdrawal of support can end a premiership (Thatcher 1990, Johnson 2022); no US cabinet has ever removed a President.\n• Independent standing — UK ministers often have their own followings in the party (Blair–Brown); US secretaries depend entirely on presidential favour.\n• Rival institutions — in the USA, power has shifted markedly from the cabinet to EXOP, whose staff require no confirmation.\n\nGuidance: the collective/individual distinction is the essential structural point.` },

      { q: '"The UK Prime Minister is more powerful than the US President." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `PM MORE POWERFUL:\n• Fusion of powers — a PM with a secure majority controls the executive AND the legislative outcome, faces no veto, a second chamber able only to delay one year, and no court able to strike down the resulting statute.\n• No entrenched constitution, no federal division of powers, no entrenched bill of rights.\n• Patronage over ministers, control of the cabinet agenda and of cabinet committees.\n• Prerogative powers over deployment, treaties, appointments and (since 2022) election timing.\n• A President cannot legislate at all and routinely fails to pass central priorities.\n\nPRESIDENT MORE POWERFUL:\n• Security of tenure — a fixed four-year term, removable only by impeachment and conviction, never achieved. A PM can be removed by their own party within days; three of the last five — May (2019), Johnson (2022) and Truss (2022) — were forced out by their own party.\n• A separate personal national mandate independent of the legislature.\n• The veto, executive orders, executive agreements, the pardon power, command of the world's largest military.\n• Judicial appointments extending influence for decades.\n• Head of state as well as head of government.\n\nGuidance for L5: each is more powerful in DIFFERENT DIMENSIONS, and the dimensions run in opposite directions — the PM over legislation when commanding a majority, the President in tenure security and unilateral action. The strongest answers also note the PM's power is entirely CONDITIONAL, so a small-majority PM may be weaker than any president.` },

      { q: '"Both the UK Prime Minister and the US President have become too powerful." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `BOTH TOO POWERFUL:\n• Both have expanded unilateral action — executive orders and the royal prerogative; both have acted militarily without prior legislative authorisation.\n• Both have grown central staff capacity — EXOP and the PM's Office — insulating decision-making within circles of loyalists.\n• Both dominate media attention, personalising politics around a single leader.\n• The PM controls a sovereign Parliament through the whips (Hailsham's "elective dictatorship"); the President governs by executive order where Congress is gridlocked.\n• Both face their weakest checks in foreign and security policy.\n\nNOT TOO POWERFUL:\n• The PM holds office only while their party permits — three of the last five (May, Johnson and Truss) were forced out by their own party without losing an election. No comparable constraint exists on a President, but it is a severe one.\n• Presidents routinely fail to pass central priorities, lose midterms, see orders reversed by successors and struck down by courts, and have been impeached.\n• Miller I and II constrained prerogative power; Youngstown and United States v Nixon constrained presidential claims.\n• A small-majority PM (May after 2017) may be weaker than almost any president.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest analysis notes the constraints differ IN KIND — the President is constrained by OTHER INSTITUTIONS, the PM by THEIR OWN PARTY — and that both appear imperial chiefly in the domain where legislative checks are weakest in each system.` },

      { q: '"The UK cabinet is more significant than the US cabinet." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `UK CABINET MORE SIGNIFICANT:\n• It is a COLLECTIVE body whose consent legitimises policy and whose withdrawal ends a premiership — Thatcher 1990, Johnson 2022. No US cabinet has ever removed a President.\n• Members sit in Parliament with their own political bases and followings; some have independent power (Blair–Brown).\n• Collective responsibility binds the whole government to decisions, giving ministers a legitimate claim to be consulted.\n• Cabinet committees do substantive policy work under ministerial direction.\n\nUS CABINET MORE SIGNIFICANT THAN ASSUMED / UK LESS:\n• US secretaries run vast departments with enormous budgets and Senate-confirmed independent standing.\n• UK cabinet significance has declined with "sofa government" — the Butler Review criticised informal decision-making before Iraq; meetings are shorter and less deliberative.\n• In both systems power has shifted to central staff — EXOP and the PM's Office/special advisers.\n• A UK PM with a large majority can dominate cabinet almost completely, so its constraint is conditional.\n\nGuidance for L5: the structural point is decisive — the UK cabinet is COLLECTIVE and can remove the chief executive; the US cabinet is a set of INDIVIDUAL advisers to a President in whom all executive power is vested. That difference is constitutional, not cultural, and explains everything else.` },

      { q: '"Structural factors best explain differences between the UK and US executives." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `STRUCTURAL BEST:\n• Fusion vs separation of powers explains legislative success — a PM commands a majority by definition, a President may face divided government.\n• Fixed vs conditional tenure explains why PMs are removed abruptly and failing presidencies limp on.\n• The absence of a UK veto is explained structurally — a PM who opposes a bill simply prevents its introduction, so no veto is needed.\n• Entrenchment explains why US executive action is more vulnerable to judicial invalidation.\n• The causal chains are direct and observable.\n\nOTHER APPROACHES NEEDED:\n• Rational choice explains VARIATION that structure cannot — why US legislators defy their own President (primaries, own funding, no deselection) and UK MPs rarely rebel (party controls nomination, label and promotion). Structure is static; these explain behaviour within it.\n• Rational choice also explains why PMs appoint rivals, and why presidents govern by executive order.\n• Cultural factors explain the personalisation of the presidency — combining head of state and head of government gives it a symbolic national role drawing on patriotic tradition that no PM commands.\n• Culture explains American individualism and British party-government traditions.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest conclusion is complementarity — structure sets the constraints, rational choice explains behaviour within them, culture explains why those institutions were adopted. Defending one approach exclusively caps at L4.` },
    ]
  },

  '2.11': {
    name: 'Comparative — The Judiciaries',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three differences between the UK and US Supreme Courts.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three, with explicit comparison:\n• Power over legislation — the US Court may declare Acts of Congress and state laws unconstitutional and void; the UK Court may only interpret statute or issue a declaration of incompatibility leaving it valid. Analysis (structural): follows entirely from entrenchment.\n• Appointment — US justices are nominated by the President and confirmed by a Senate voting on near-party lines; UK justices are selected on merit by an independent commission.\n• Tenure — US justices serve for life; UK justices are subject to a mandatory retirement age.\n• Finality — a US constitutional ruling can realistically be reversed only by the Court itself; any UK ruling can be reversed by ordinary statute, as the Judicial Review and Courts Act 2022 showed.\n• Size and age — 9 justices since 1789; 12 justices since 2009.\n\nGuidance: explicit comparison required throughout.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three similarities between the UK and US judiciaries.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Review of executive action — both examine whether government has acted lawfully, and both have rejected claims that executive authority lies beyond judicial reach: Miller I and II in the UK; United States v Nixon and Youngstown in the USA.\n• Rights protection — both have become significant rights actors, through an entrenched Bill of Rights and through the Human Rights Act 1998 respectively, and in both, pressure groups now litigate to pursue policy aims.\n• Independence — both protect it through security of tenure and salaries insulated from political manipulation, and both operate a common-law tradition of binding precedent.\n• Political backlash — in both countries, rulings against government have prompted accusations of judicial overreach and government proposals to curb judicial power.\n• Neither can initiate cases; both depend on litigants.\n\nGuidance: answers listing only differences cannot reach the top band here.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three reasons why the US Supreme Court is more powerful than the UK Supreme Court.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Entrenchment provides a higher law — the US Constitution sits above ordinary legislation, giving the Court a standard against which statute can be measured and, since Marbury v Madison (1803), the power to declare legislation void. The UK has no such higher law.\n• Finality of rulings — because US constitutional amendment requires a double supermajority that is practically unattainable, a constitutional ruling is effectively final; a UK ruling can be overturned by an ordinary Act, as the Judicial Review and Courts Act 2022 demonstrated.\n• Federalism creates additional jurisdiction — the US Court arbitrates between federal and state authority, determining the constitutional balance of the whole system. The UK Court's devolution role is smaller and ultimately resolvable politically.\n\nAnalysis expected: the difference is wholly STRUCTURAL rather than a matter of judicial character or ambition. Given an entrenched constitution, any court would acquire comparable power.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which the rational approach explains differences between the two judiciaries.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• The value of the prize explains politicised appointments — because US justices can strike down legislation and serve for life, one appointment can determine policy for thirty years or more, making heavy investment of political capital entirely rational. A UK justice cannot strike down statute and faces a retirement age, so the return is too low to justify comparable investment.\n• Predicted behaviour is observed — presidents nominate young candidates to maximise duration; justices time retirements for a sympathetic President; interest groups fund confirmation campaigns; senators abandon procedural norms when the payoff is large enough (Garland 2016, Barrett 2020); the filibuster was removed for Supreme Court nominations in 2017.\n• Pressure groups litigate more in the USA because a single ruling delivers permanent nationwide change, whereas a UK ruling can be reversed by Parliament.\n\nGuidance: the rational approach must be named and applied to explain the DIFFERENCE, not merely described.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which the two judiciaries have become more politically significant.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Expanding rights frameworks — the HRA 1998 gave UK courts an extensive new jurisdiction; US rights jurisprudence expanded substantially from the Warren Court onwards. Analysis: creating enforceable rights necessarily transfers power to whoever interprets them.\n• Growth of executive power — both executives increasingly use unilateral action (executive orders, prerogative powers), particularly where legislatures are gridlocked, which generates litigation. Analysis: judicial prominence and legislative dysfunction rise together, so judicial expansion is a SYMPTOM of institutional imbalance.\n• Judicialisation of political conflict — contested moral and constitutional questions are increasingly framed as legal claims, from abortion and marriage in the USA to prorogation and Brexit in the UK.\n• Pressure groups adopting litigation as a deliberate political strategy in both systems.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires the causal analysis, particularly the link between legislative gridlock and judicial prominence.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which UK judicial influence exceeds its formal powers.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• The section 3 interpretive duty — the HRA requires courts to read legislation compatibly with Convention rights "so far as it is possible to do so", applied strongly enough to produce readings departing considerably from the natural meaning of the words Parliament enacted. Interpretation of this kind achieves much of what a strike-down power would, without challenging sovereignty.\n• The political force of declarations of incompatibility — although a declaration leaves the statute valid, governments have almost always amended the law in response, because an authoritative public finding that the state is violating rights is politically costly to ignore, particularly with Strasbourg as a backstop (Belmarsh 2004).\n• Judicial review of executive action is unqualified — most government activity is executive rather than legislative, so this reaches a large proportion of what government does, and Miller II showed even long-assumed non-justiciable prerogative powers can be reviewed.\n\nGuidance: credit the counter-point that these are INFLUENCE rather than POWER, and depend on government choosing to respond.` },

      { q: '"The US Supreme Court is more powerful than the UK Supreme Court." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `US COURT MORE POWERFUL:\n• It can strike down federal and state legislation as unconstitutional; the UK Court cannot touch an Act of Parliament.\n• Because amendment is practically unattainable, its constitutional rulings are effectively FINAL; any UK ruling can be reversed by ordinary statute, as the 2022 Act showed.\n• Life tenure extends a justice's influence for decades.\n• Federalism gives it authority over the constitutional balance of the entire system.\n• It determines the rules of political competition — Citizens United, Shelby County, Rucho.\n\nTHE GAP IS NARROWER THAN IT APPEARS:\n• HRA s.3 lets UK courts shape statutory meaning substantially, achieving much of what strike-down would.\n• Declarations of incompatibility are almost always acted upon — political force exceeding legal force (Belmarsh).\n• UK judicial review of executive action is unqualified, and most government activity is executive.\n• Miller I and II show UK courts constraining the executive on the most contested political questions of the era.\n• The US Court has no enforcement power and depends on accepted legitimacy.\n\nGuidance for L5: ENTRENCHMENT is the single structural variable explaining most of the difference, and should be named explicitly. The strongest answers also test the obvious conclusion by examining where UK influence exceeds formal power.` },

      { q: '"Judicial appointments in the USA are more politicised than in the UK." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `MORE POLITICISED IN THE USA:\n• Nominees selected for judicial philosophy; confirmation votes near-party-line.\n• Norms have collapsed — the same election-year argument applied to Garland in 2016 and abandoned for Barrett in 2020; the filibuster removed in 2017.\n• Interest groups campaign for and against nominees, spending heavily.\n• Life tenure makes each appointment a decades-long prize; presidents nominate young candidates and justices time retirements strategically.\n• Dobbs reversed a fifty-year precedent after three appointments, reinforcing the perception that outcomes follow appointments.\n\nUK NOT ENTIRELY APOLITICAL:\n• The Lord Chancellor retains a formal role, and the government sets the framework within which the JAC operates.\n• Concerns about the narrow social and educational background of appointees (Griffith's critique) raise questions about whose assumptions inform interpretation.\n• Ministers and the press have attacked judges after Miller I and II, and governments have proposed curbing judicial power.\n• Politicians have periodically criticised the JAC and proposed changes to it.\n\nGuidance for L5: the RATIONAL explanation is decisive and should be named — where an appointment can determine policy for decades, heavy investment is rational; where a court cannot invalidate statute, the return is too low. The difference is institutional incentive, not national temperament.` },

      { q: '"Entrenchment explains the difference in power between the UK and US judiciaries." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `ENTRENCHMENT EXPLAINS IT:\n• It provides a HIGHER LAW against which statute can be measured — without one, UK courts have nothing to measure against, which is why they cannot strike legislation down.\n• It makes US rulings effectively final, since amendment is unattainable; UK rulings are reversible by ordinary Act.\n• It explains politicised appointments — the return on capturing a US appointment is decades of policy control; the return on a UK appointment is far lower.\n• It explains why US pressure groups litigate so heavily and UK groups lobby ministers instead.\n• Given the same constitution, any court would acquire the same power — the difference is not judicial character.\n\nOTHER FACTORS MATTER:\n• Federalism gives the US Court a whole additional jurisdiction over centre–state disputes that entrenchment alone does not create.\n• Life tenure versus a retirement age independently affects the stakes of each appointment.\n• Cultural factors — a rights-based American political tradition channels moral disputes into constitutional litigation, which is why the Court is the arena for national moral conflict.\n• The UK Court only opened in 2009; institutional confidence may still be developing.\n• HRA s.3 shows UK influence can exceed formal powers, so entrenchment does not determine everything.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest conclusion is that entrenchment explains MOST but not ALL — it is the dominant variable, with federalism, tenure and culture as genuine supplements.` },

      { q: '"Both the UK and US judiciaries have become too political." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `BOTH TOO POLITICAL:\n• Both now rule on the most contested political questions of their era — Miller I and II; Dobbs, Citizens United, Shelby County.\n• Both have expanded rights jurisdiction that requires evaluative judgment — HRA s.3; substantive due process.\n• Both attract accusations of overreach and government proposals to curb judicial power.\n• Pressure groups in both systems now use litigation as a deliberate political strategy.\n• Expanding executive unilateralism draws courts into political disputes in both.\n\nNOT EQUALLY, OR NOT AT ALL:\n• The charge has FAR more force in the USA, where appointments are openly partisan and where precedent has been reversed following changes in composition — nobody claims UK judges were appointed to produce particular outcomes.\n• UK appointments are made by an independent commission on merit.\n• In Miller I and II the UK Court ruled on the LIMITS OF EXECUTIVE POWER under existing principle, not on the merits of Brexit — it constrained the government on Parliament's behalf.\n• UK rulings can be reversed by ordinary statute, so they never remove a question from democratic revision.\n• Neither court chooses the disputes brought to it.\n\nGuidance for L5: the comparative distinction is essential — the UK criticism concerns RULINGS on executive power; the US criticism concerns the PROCESS that produces the judges and the reversibility of precedent. An answer treating the two as equivalent caps at L3.` },
    ]
  },

  '2.12': {
    name: 'Comparative — Electoral and Party Systems',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which the electoral systems of the UK and the USA differ.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three, with explicit comparison:\n• Primaries versus party selection — US candidates are chosen by registered voters in primaries, stripping the national party of gatekeeping power; UK candidates are selected by local constituency parties under national rules and approved lists. Analysis (rational): US politicians answer to primary electorates, incentivising ideological positioning over appealing to the median voter — the principal institutional driver of weak discipline and polarisation.\n• Fixed versus flexible timetables — US elections run on a constitutionally fixed calendar; UK general election timing returned to prime ministerial discretion after the 2022 FTPA repeal. Analysis (structural): the codified US constitution removes a power the UK executive retains.\n• Uniform versus varied systems — the UK uses FPTP for Westminster but AMS in Scotland (and in Wales until 2026) and STV in Northern Ireland, producing multi-party systems at devolved level; the USA uses plurality almost throughout. Analysis: strong evidence that electoral system design SUSTAINS party systems rather than merely reflecting them.\n\nGuidance: explicit comparison required throughout.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three similarities between the UK and US electoral and party systems.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Both use SINGLE-MEMBER PLURALITY for their main legislative chamber, and both are dominated by two major parties that alternate in government — Duverger's law operating in both despite very different political cultures.\n• Both main parties in each country are BROAD COALITIONS rather than narrow ideological groupings, assembled to win national majorities.\n• Both have experienced partisan DEALIGNMENT — the weakening of habitual class-based or traditional loyalties — and insurgent challenges to established parties.\n• Both have opened LEADERSHIP or CANDIDATE SELECTION to wider participation, with similar consequences: leaders or nominees chosen by committed members or primary voters who may not command broader support (Corbyn 2015, Truss 2022; Trump 2016).\n• Both face concerns about money in politics, differing in form rather than kind.\n\nGuidance: a similarities question answered with differences cannot access the top band.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three reasons why party discipline is stronger in the UK than the USA.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three, using the rational approach:\n• Route to nomination — UK MPs are selected by local party members under national rules and CAN be deselected; US legislators win primaries and cannot be removed by the national party, so the leadership holds no effective sanction.\n• Campaign funding — UK candidates depend on the party label and a centrally run campaign within statutory spending limits; US candidates raise their own money from donors, PACs and Super PACs, and are financially independent.\n• Career incentives — UK whips influence promotion to ministerial office, the prize that structures an MP's career; US legislators advance through seniority and committee assignment, with no ministerial prize.\n• Fusion of powers — a UK government falls if it loses Commons confidence, so a major rebellion is a constitutional act with potentially terminal consequences; a US President's survival is entirely independent of congressional votes.\n\nAnalysis expected: discipline is not a cultural disposition but an outcome of WHO CONTROLS the resources a politician needs — nomination, money and promotion.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three differences in campaign finance between the UK and the USA.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Spending limits — UK law imposes statutory limits on national and constituency campaign spending; in the USA, Buckley v Valeo (1976) held spending limits restrict protected speech, and Citizens United (2010) permitted unlimited independent expenditure.\n• Advertising — paid political advertising on television is PROHIBITED in the UK; in the USA it is the dominant campaign medium.\n• Campaign length and structure — UK general election campaigns are formally a matter of weeks; US presidential campaigns run for over a year, beginning with an invisible primary, and candidates must fight both a primary and a general election.\n• Transparency — both require disclosure above thresholds, but US 501(c)(4) "dark money" groups need not disclose donors at all.\n\nAnalysis expected: the crucial structural point is that US reform is CONSTITUTIONALLY FORECLOSED — Congress cannot legislate limits unless the Court reverses itself — whereas the UK regulates by ordinary statute whenever it chooses.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three reasons why third parties are more successful in the UK than in the USA.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Geographic concentration of support — under the same plurality rules, concentrated support succeeds and dispersed support fails. The SNP converted ~1.5m votes into 56 seats in 2015; UKIP's 3.9m produced one. US third-party support is dispersed nationally, so Perot's ~19% in 1992 produced ZERO electoral votes.\n• Territorial identity — UK nationalist parties have a national base and a distinct political community; the USA has no comparable territorial cleavage.\n• Proportional systems at sub-national level — AMS and STV give UK smaller parties a genuine platform, elected representatives and governing experience, from which Westminster credibility follows. US states use plurality almost throughout.\n• Lower institutional barriers — UK ballot access is simpler than the state-by-state US signature and legal requirements, and UK broadcasting rules guarantee some coverage.\n\nGuidance: the concentration/dispersal point is the essential analytical move.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three reasons why polarisation is more pronounced in the USA than the UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Ideological sorting — until the 1960s both US parties contained conservative southern Democrats and liberal northern Republicans, making cross-party coalitions routine; the civil rights realignment eliminated that overlap. UK parties have always been more internally coherent but also more centrally disciplined.\n• Safe districts and primary dominance — where the US general election is a formality, the only threat is a primary challenge from the flank, so compromise is electorally dangerous. The UK has no primaries, and central candidate approval dampens the same pressure.\n• Institutional amplification — US separation of powers translates polarisation directly into gridlock via the filibuster; UK fusion means a government with a majority can still govern.\n• Media regulation — a deregulated partisan US media has no UK equivalent given broadcasting impartiality rules.\n\nAnalysis expected: UK polarisation over Brexit was real but cut ACROSS parties rather than aligning with them, producing internal party crises rather than inter-party deadlock.` },

      { q: '"Electoral systems explain the party systems of the UK and the USA." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `ELECTORAL SYSTEMS EXPLAIN:\n• Both countries use single-member plurality and both have two dominant parties, despite very different political cultures — Duverger's law.\n• Strategic incentives follow directly: voters, donors and candidates converge on the two viable parties to avoid the spoiler effect.\n• Where the UK uses PROPORTIONAL systems at devolved level, genuinely multi-party systems emerge from the SAME electorate — the strongest available natural experiment.\n• Perot's ~19% and zero electoral votes in 1992 shows the mechanism starkly.\n• Institutional barriers (ballot access, debate thresholds) reinforce the effect.\n\nOTHER FACTORS NEEDED:\n• Identical rules produce DIFFERENT outcomes — the UK sustains significant nationalist and third parties at Westminster while the USA does not, so the GEOGRAPHY of support matters independently.\n• Territorial identity and the salience of national cleavages explain the SNP's position; the USA has no comparable cleavage.\n• Cultural factors — American individualism supporting candidate-centred politics; British collective party traditions.\n• Primaries (an institution, not an electoral system) explain much of the discipline and polarisation difference.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest conclusion is that electoral systems explain the NUMBER of major parties and the geography of support explains the survival of minor ones — a distinction that resolves the apparent contradiction.` },

      { q: '"Party discipline differences between the UK and the USA are best explained by rational choice." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `RATIONAL CHOICE BEST:\n• It identifies WHO CONTROLS what a politician needs — nomination, money and promotion — and predicts discipline accordingly. UK parties control all three; US parties control none.\n• The prediction matches observation precisely: strong UK discipline, weak US discipline.\n• It explains VARIATION over time — UK discipline weakened over Brexit because the party's own selectorate was divided, which a static structural account cannot capture.\n• It explains individual behaviour — why a US legislator in a safe district fears a primary challenge more than a general election defeat.\n\nOTHER APPROACHES:\n• STRUCTURAL — fusion of powers means a UK government falls if it loses confidence, so rebellion is a constitutional act with terminal consequences; a US President's survival is independent of congressional votes. This raises the stakes of UK dissent enormously and is not reducible to individual incentives.\n• Separation of powers also explains why US legislators have no ministerial prize to pursue.\n• CULTURAL — American individualism and suspicion of centralised authority; British parliamentary traditions of collective party government. These explain why primaries were adopted in the Progressive era specifically to weaken party machines.\n\nGuidance for L5: rational choice is the strongest SINGLE approach here, but structure supplies the constitutional stakes and culture explains why those institutions were chosen. The best answers rank the approaches and justify the ranking rather than asserting complementarity vaguely.` },

      { q: '"Money is more important in US than UK elections." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `MORE IMPORTANT IN THE USA:\n• Effectively unlimited independent expenditure after Citizens United; UK spending is capped by statute.\n• Paid political TV advertising is the dominant US medium and is PROHIBITED in the UK.\n• Candidate-centred campaigns mean US legislators must raise their own money continuously; UK candidates rely on a centrally run campaign.\n• Primaries plus a general election means US candidates effectively fund two campaigns.\n• Dark money through 501(c)(4) groups has no UK equivalent.\n• The invisible primary screens out candidates who cannot raise money before a vote is cast.\n\nNOT SO DIFFERENT / qualified:\n• UK parties also depend heavily on a small number of very large donors, with recurring honours controversies.\n• Money does not determine US outcomes — Trump was outspent in 2016; heavily outspent candidates win.\n• Partisanship now predicts vote choice more reliably than campaign spending in both countries.\n• UK spending limits are not comprehensive, and non-party campaigning is regulated only loosely.\n• Both have concerns about access purchased by donors.\n\nGuidance for L5: the decisive structural point is that US reform is CONSTITUTIONALLY FORECLOSED while the UK regulates by ordinary statute. The same problem exists in both; only one can legislate a remedy. That reaches L5.` },

      { q: '"The UK and US party systems are converging." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `CONVERGING:\n• Both show partisan DEALIGNMENT and the weakening of class-based or traditional loyalties.\n• Both have seen rising polarisation and insurgent challenges to established parties (UKIP/Reform; the Tea Party/Trump).\n• Both have opened leadership or candidate selection to wider participation, with similar results — Corbyn 2015 and Truss 2022 parallel Trump 2016 in producing leaders the parliamentary party or establishment opposed.\n• Both have personalised, leader-focused and media-driven campaigns.\n• UK party membership has become more ideologically committed, resembling US primary electorates.\n\nNOT CONVERGING:\n• Selection remains fundamentally different — the UK has NO primaries, so parties retain gatekeeping power US parties lack.\n• UK discipline remains strong because the party controls nomination, funding and promotion.\n• UK campaign spending is capped by statute; US independent expenditure is unlimited and constitutionally protected.\n• The UK sustains significant third parties at Westminster; the USA does not.\n• The UK uses proportional systems sub-nationally; the USA does not.\n• UK fusion of powers ties government survival to party discipline in a way separation does not.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest formulation is that the TRENDS converge while the INSTITUTIONS do not — and since institutions shape behaviour, convergence is likely to remain partial. That distinction reaches L5.` },
    ]
  },

  '2.13': {
    name: 'Comparative — Pressure Groups',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three differences between UK and US pressure group activity.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three, with explicit comparison:\n• Electioneering — US groups fund candidates through PACs, spend without limit through Super PACs, publish scorecards and threaten renomination through primary funding; UK groups face statutory spending caps and cannot fund candidates comparably. Analysis (rational): the primaries lever gives US groups direct power over a legislator's career that no UK group can have.\n• Litigation — US groups can have LEGISLATION struck down as unconstitutional, delivering permanent nationwide change; UK groups can challenge EXECUTIVE action and secondary or devolved legislation, or obtain a declaration of incompatibility, but cannot have an Act of Parliament struck down, and Parliament can reverse any ruling.\n• Target — UK groups concentrate on ministers and civil servants because the executive controls the legislative agenda; US groups target individual legislators whose votes are genuinely independent.\n• Access points — US separation of powers and federalism create far more openings than the UK's fused executive and legislature.\n\nGuidance: explicit comparison required throughout.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three similarities in pressure group activity in the UK and USA.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Both rely heavily on supplying SPECIALIST EXPERTISE to government, and in both, groups whose knowledge government needs secure privileged consultation.\n• In both, success depends substantially on ALIGNMENT WITH THE GOVERNING AGENDA — the most reliable predictor in either system.\n• Both have lobbying registers widely criticised as inadequate — the UK's 2014 Act covers only consultant lobbyists; US federal rules are broader but still criticised.\n• Both face the REVOLVING DOOR problem, with officials moving into lobbying and industry roles.\n• Both use media campaigning, demonstrations and direct action.\n• ELITIST critiques apply to both — business enjoys structural leverage in each because governments depend on investment and employment decisions.\n\nGuidance: a similarities question answered with differences cannot access the top band.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three structural reasons why pressure groups are more powerful in the USA.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Multiple access points — separation of powers means a bill must pass the House, pass the Senate, survive the filibuster, be signed and survive judicial review; federalism adds fifty state governments. A group needs to succeed at only ONE veto point to block. The UK's fusion concentrates power in an executive commanding a disciplined majority, so essentially one access point matters.\n• Independent legislators — because US legislators are nominated in primaries and fund their own campaigns, they vote independently, so persuading or funding one member can change an outcome. A whipped UK MP's vote is largely predetermined.\n• Entrenched, judicially enforceable rights — US groups can convert a political defeat into a permanent legal victory by having legislation struck down; UK groups have no equivalent route.\n\nAnalysis expected: the crucial qualification is that many access points are also many VETO points — US groups are better at BLOCKING, UK groups better at OBTAINING, since a UK group that persuades a government with a secure majority sees its aim legislated in full.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which UK pressure group activity has become more like that in the USA.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• More access points through devolution — a group defeated at Westminster may succeed in Edinburgh or Cardiff, replicating in miniature the US pattern of multi-level lobbying; minimum unit alcohol pricing in Scotland is a concrete result.\n• Growth of litigation — the Human Rights Act 1998 and expanded judicial review have made the courts a serious arena, with groups such as Liberty and environmental organisations bringing test cases. The government's response in the Judicial Review and Courts Act 2022 is itself evidence of how significant the route had become.\n• Professionalisation of lobbying — a substantial commercial lobbying industry and a revolving door between government and consultancy.\n• Greater use of media and celebrity campaigning to shift opinion directly rather than through insider negotiation.\n\nAnalysis expected: the limits of convergence — no primaries, statutory spending limits, and parliamentary sovereignty meaning courts cannot strike down statute.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three differences in the use of litigation by UK and US pressure groups.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• What can be achieved — US groups can have LEGISLATION declared unconstitutional, delivering permanent nationwide change reversible only by the Court or an unattainable amendment (the NAACP and Brown). UK groups can only challenge the legality of executive action; courts cannot invalidate an Act of Parliament.\n• Durability — a US ruling is effectively final; a UK ruling can be reversed by ordinary statute, as the Judicial Review and Courts Act 2022 demonstrated by reversing the Cart (2011) ruling on judicial review of Upper Tribunal decisions.\n• Purpose — in the USA litigation is a strategy for winning the substantive argument permanently; in the UK it forces a minister to retake a decision lawfully, which a determined government can usually do.\n• Methods — US groups file amicus curiae briefs and run long-term test-case strategies; UK groups more often seek judicial review of specific decisions.\n\nAnalysis expected: the asymmetry follows entirely from ENTRENCHMENT, which explains why US groups invest so heavily in litigation.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which the cultural approach explains differences in pressure group activity.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• American associational tradition — de Tocqueville observed in the 1830s that Americans formed voluntary associations for every purpose. Combined with deep suspicion of government, this encourages citizens to organise independently rather than rely on the state.\n• Rights-based discourse — framing claims as constitutional rights makes litigation a NORMAL form of political action rather than an unusual one, and the First Amendment right "to petition the Government" gives group activity explicit constitutional standing.\n• British insider consultation tradition — a culture of negotiation between government and recognised representative bodies, with professional associations incorporated into policy-making. The expectation is that legitimate interests will be CONSULTED rather than that they must campaign publicly.\n\nAssessment expected: the cultural approach explains why American associational density and litigious style PREDATE the modern institutional arrangements — but the fact that UK behaviour has shifted towards the US pattern as devolution and judicial review created new access points suggests institutions do more of the explanatory work.` },

      { q: '"Pressure groups are more powerful in the USA than the UK." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `MORE POWERFUL IN THE USA:\n• Many access points — separation of powers, bicameralism, the filibuster, federalism and the courts; a group needs to win at only one to block.\n• Weak party discipline makes individual legislators genuinely persuadable and individually dependent on outside money.\n• Primaries allow groups to threaten a legislator's renomination — a lever no UK group possesses.\n• Unlimited independent expenditure after Citizens United.\n• Litigation can strike down legislation permanently.\n\nQUALIFICATIONS:\n• Many access points are also many VETO points, so US groups are better at BLOCKING than achieving. A UK group that persuades a government with a secure majority sees its aim legislated quickly and in full.\n• UK insider groups enjoy embedded, routine consultation at the drafting stage that is highly effective.\n• Success in BOTH systems depends primarily on alignment with the governing agenda.\n• UK devolution and judicial review have multiplied access points since 1999.\n\nGuidance for L5: "more powerful" depends on whether the group's aim is CHANGE or its PREVENTION. The strongest answers make that distinction explicitly rather than simply accumulating US advantages.` },

      { q: '"Institutional factors rather than culture explain differences in UK and US pressure group activity." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `INSTITUTIONS EXPLAIN:\n• Access points follow directly from separation of powers versus fusion, and from federalism versus devolution.\n• Primaries explain why US groups target individual legislators and UK groups target ministers — the return differs because the lever differs.\n• Entrenchment explains the litigation asymmetry: US courts can strike down statute, UK courts cannot.\n• Campaign finance rules explain the electioneering difference — one system caps spending by statute, the other cannot.\n• DECISIVE EVIDENCE: UK group behaviour has SHIFTED towards the US pattern precisely as devolution and expanded judicial review created new access points — behaviour tracking institutions, not culture.\n\nCULTURE ALSO EXPLAINS:\n• American associational density and litigiousness PREDATE the modern institutional arrangements, which institutions alone cannot explain.\n• A rights-based political tradition makes constitutional litigation a natural form of political action.\n• The British insider-consultation tradition shaped how the executive engages groups.\n• Institutions were themselves CHOSEN because of cultural assumptions, so the two cannot be cleanly separated.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest conclusion is that institutions explain CURRENT BEHAVIOUR most precisely, while culture explains the ORIGIN of the institutions — and that the UK's post-1999 shift is the best available evidence for the primacy of institutions in explaining behaviour.` },

      { q: '"Both UK and US pressure group politics are elitist." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `BOTH ELITIST:\n• In both, influence tracks resources, organisation and access rather than the number of people affected.\n• Business enjoys STRUCTURAL power in both — governments depend on investment and employment decisions, so the anticipated reaction of business constrains policy without any lobbying.\n• The revolving door operates in both; both have lobbying registers criticised as inadequate.\n• In both, well-resourced groups dominate the technical consultation stages where policy is actually shaped.\n• US dark money and UK party dependence on a small number of large donors are different forms of the same concentration.\n\nBOTH ALSO PLURALIST:\n• Group competition is genuine in each, and outcomes frequently reflect compromise.\n• Both offer multiple access points — more in the USA, but devolution and judicial review have increased them in the UK.\n• Poorly resourced groups do win — the 2017 ACA repeal defeat; the Snowdrop campaign; Marcus Rashford.\n• Litigation gives minorities routes to victory without majorities.\n\nGuidance for L5: the defensible conclusion is that both systems are PLURALIST IN FORM and ELITIST IN EFFECT — competition is real but pluralism's assumption of rough parity of access fails in each. They differ in DEGREE (sharper in the USA post-Citizens United) rather than in KIND.` },

      { q: '"UK and US pressure group politics are converging." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `CONVERGING:\n• Devolution created additional access points, replicating multi-level lobbying.\n• The HRA and expanded judicial review made the courts a serious UK arena, with groups bringing test cases as US groups do — and the Judicial Review and Courts Act 2022 shows how significant that route became.\n• Professionalised commercial lobbying and a revolving door have developed in the UK.\n• Greater use of media and celebrity campaigning in both.\n• Both face declining party membership, increasing the relative weight of groups.\n\nNOT CONVERGING:\n• NO PRIMARIES in the UK — so no group can threaten a legislator's renomination, removing the single most powerful US lever.\n• Statutory spending limits cap UK campaign activity; US independent expenditure is unlimited and constitutionally protected.\n• Parliamentary sovereignty means UK courts cannot strike down statute, so litigation can never deliver what it delivers in the USA.\n• UK fusion of powers keeps the executive as the decisive target; US fragmentation keeps individual legislators central.\n• Iron triangles have no real UK equivalent.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest formulation is convergence in BEHAVIOUR, divergence in STRUCTURE — and since structure shapes behaviour, convergence is bounded. Naming the three structural barriers (primaries, spending limits, sovereignty) reaches L5.` },
    ]
  },

  '2.14': {
    name: 'Comparative — Civil Rights',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three differences in rights protection between the UK and the USA.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three, with explicit comparison:\n• Entrenchment — US rights sit in a constitution beyond legislative reach; UK rights rest on ordinary statute (the HRA 1998, Equality Act 2010) repealable by simple Commons majority.\n• Judicial remedy — US courts may strike down rights-violating legislation; UK courts may only issue a declaration of incompatibility leaving the statute valid and in force.\n• Uniformity — the HRA applies UK-wide and the Equality Act 2010 covers all of Great Britain (Northern Ireland has its own equality law); US federalism means rights vary substantially by state, with abortion access, voting procedures and policing standards all differing.\n• External supervision — the UK remains subject to the European Court of Human Rights independently of Brexit; the USA has no comparable external jurisdiction.\n• Main engine of change — legislation in the UK, judicial interpretation in the USA.\n\nGuidance: explicit comparison throughout.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three similarities in how civil rights have been advanced in the UK and the USA.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Both relied on a COMBINATION of litigation, legislation and social movement campaigning; no single route sufficed in either country.\n• In both, the judiciary became a significantly more important rights actor from the late twentieth century — through expanding US rights jurisprudence and through the Human Rights Act 1998.\n• Both have comprehensive ANTI-DISCRIMINATION STATUTES reaching private actors — the Equality Act 2010 and the Civil Rights Act 1964 — which constitutional rulings alone cannot do.\n• In both, rights have been RESTRICTED by counter-terrorism legislation permitting extended detention and expanded surveillance.\n• In both, governments have responded to adverse rulings by proposing to curb judicial power.\n• In both, pressure groups pursue rights goals through the courts.\n\nGuidance: a similarities question answered with differences caps at L2.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three reasons why the US judiciary has played a larger role than the UK\'s in advancing civil rights.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Entrenched, judicially enforceable rights — the Fourteenth Amendment's equal protection clause provides a constitutional standard against which any federal or state law can be measured, and courts can strike down legislation failing it. This made Brown possible: a single ruling invalidated school segregation nationwide.\n• Bypassing hostile majorities — a constitutional claim requires only a plaintiff with standing and a strong legal argument, so groups can win without electoral or legislative majorities. This was decisive when southern Democratic power in Congress made federal legislation impossible for decades.\n• Durability — because amendment requires a double supermajority, a constitutional ruling is effectively final, so a court victory is far more secure than a legislative one.\n\nUK contrast: with no entrenched rights and no power to invalidate statute, UK courts cannot deliver comparable change, so advances have come principally through LEGISLATION — the Race Relations Acts, the Equality Act 2010.\n\nAnalysis expected: each country's rights movements adopted the strategy their institutions made VIABLE.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which federalism and devolution affect rights protection.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• US federalism reserves elections, education, policing and (since Dobbs) abortion regulation to the states, so a citizen's practical entitlements depend substantially on residence — abortion access differs radically across state lines.\n• UK devolution transferred health, education and some policing, but the HRA applies UK-wide and the Equality Act 2010 covers Great Britain (Northern Ireland has its own equality law), maintaining a broadly common floor of protection.\n• Federalism historically SHIELDED discrimination — states' rights arguments were used to resist federal intervention throughout the Jim Crow era, and overcoming it required the Fourteenth Amendment, Supreme Court rulings, federal legislation and ultimately federal troops.\n• Federalism can also ADVANCE rights — several US states recognised same-sex marriage before Obergefell, and states have legislated protections exceeding the federal floor.\n\nAnalysis expected: US federalism produces both the strongest and the weakest rights regimes in the same country; UK devolution does not, because rights law is reserved.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three reasons why civil rights are more politically contested in the USA than the UK.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Federalism keeps questions open — because states control elections, policing, education and now abortion regulation, each contested right is fought fifty times over rather than settled once. UK equality law is reserved and uniform across Great Britain (Northern Ireland has its own), largely removing this dimension.\n• Rights are settled by an appointed COURT rather than a legislature — because the Supreme Court determines constitutional meaning and its composition can shift with a small number of appointments, no ruling is truly final. Dobbs demonstrated that a right recognised for fifty years could be reversed, giving opponents of any ruling a permanent incentive to fight over appointments rather than accept the outcome.\n• Cultural framing — American discourse asserts rights ABSOLUTELY, whereas a policy can be traded; the British tradition of parliamentary remedy encourages incremental legislative settlement.\n\nAnalysis expected: the US system protects rights more strongly against legislative majorities but leaves them more politically contested — the two are connected, not coincidental.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which UK rights may be less secure in law but more stable in practice than US rights.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• The legal vulnerability is real — the HRA and Equality Act are ordinary statutes repealable by simple Commons majority with no entrenchment, and replacement of the HRA has been repeatedly proposed.\n• But no government has ACTUALLY repealed either, despite decades of proposals — the political cost is high, the UK remains bound by the ECHR independently of the HRA so repeal would not remove the underlying obligations, and Convention rights are written into the devolution settlements, complicating repeal further.\n• US rights cannot be repealed by legislation, which appears far more secure — but because their CONTENT is determined by judicial interpretation, they can be narrowed or withdrawn by a Court whose composition has changed. Dobbs removed a right recognised for fifty years with no change to the constitutional text, and the concurring reasoning invited reconsideration of others.\n\nAnalysis expected: the two systems RELOCATE the vulnerability rather than eliminating it — UK rights are exposed to electoral majorities that have proved reluctant; US rights to judicial appointments that have proved willing.` },

      { q: '"Civil rights are better protected in the USA than the UK." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `USA BETTER:\n• Entrenchment places rights beyond ordinary legislative repeal; no Commons-style majority could remove them.\n• Judicially enforceable against BOTH federal and state government.\n• Courts have delivered change elected branches would not — Brown, Obergefell, Bostock.\n• The Fourteenth Amendment provides a standard against which any law can be measured.\n• Strong First Amendment protection of speech, assembly and religion.\n\nUK BETTER:\n• Rights are broadly UNIFORM — the HRA applies UK-wide and the Equality Act 2010 across Great Britain (Northern Ireland has its own equality law) — whereas US rights vary radically by state, particularly after Dobbs.\n• Continuing ECHR obligations provide an external backstop independent of the HRA.\n• Legislated rights carry democratic legitimacy and reach PRIVATE discrimination in employment, housing and services.\n• UK rights do not depend on the composition of a court; despite theoretical fragility, no government has repealed them.\n• The UK has avoided the politicisation of judicial appointments that Dobbs and the Garland/Barrett episode illustrate.\n\nGuidance for L5: the decisive formulation is that the USA protects rights better against LEGISLATURES and the UK protects them better against COURTS — and which matters more depends on which institution is currently more hostile to the right in question. Dobbs and Shelby County show the US answer changed within a decade. That reaches L5.` },

      { q: '"Entrenchment is the best protection for civil rights." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `ENTRENCHMENT IS BEST:\n• It places rights beyond the reach of ordinary majorities, protecting minorities against hostile legislatures — exactly what rights are for.\n• It makes rights judicially enforceable against both levels of government.\n• It enabled Brown, Obergefell and Bostock, delivering change elected branches would not.\n• UK rights are theoretically repealable by simple majority, and replacement of the HRA has been repeatedly proposed.\n• Without it, rights depend entirely on political restraint.\n\nENTRENCHMENT IS NOT ENOUGH / NOT BEST:\n• Entrenched rights depend on INTERPRETATION, so they can be withdrawn by a changed Court — Dobbs reversed a fifty-year precedent with no change to the text.\n• It transfers the definition of rights from elected legislatures to nine appointments, making the Senate and vacancy timing the real determinants.\n• The Reconstruction amendments lay dormant for nearly a century — text without institutional will achieves nothing.\n• Legislated rights reach PRIVATE discrimination that constitutional rulings cannot, and carry democratic legitimacy.\n• UK rights have proved stable in practice despite theoretical fragility, backed by continuing ECHR obligations.\n• Entrenchment makes rights harder to EXTEND as well as harder to remove.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest conclusion is that entrenchment RELOCATES vulnerability rather than removing it — from electoral majorities to judicial appointments — and that the choice is therefore about which risk is preferable, not about which is safe.` },

      { q: '"Federalism undermines civil rights in the USA." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `UNDERMINES:\n• States control elections, policing, education and — since Dobbs — abortion regulation, so entitlements vary radically by residence, which sits uncomfortably with national citizenship.\n• Federalism historically SHIELDED segregation; states' rights arguments resisted federal intervention throughout the Jim Crow era, requiring federal troops at Little Rock.\n• Shelby County restored state control over voting rules, after which several states introduced restrictions.\n• Poorer states with weaker rights regimes disadvantage their most vulnerable residents.\n• It fragments enforcement and creates a patchwork of protection.\n\nDOES NOT UNDERMINE / can advance rights:\n• Federalism permits ADVANCE as well as retreat — several states recognised same-sex marriage before Obergefell, and many legislate protections exceeding the federal floor.\n• States function as policy laboratories, and successful state reforms spread.\n• Federal constitutional rights and the incorporation doctrine set a floor no state may fall below.\n• The Civil Rights Act 1964 and Voting Rights Act 1965 show federal power overriding state resistance when Congress acts.\n• Local control can be more responsive to genuinely different local circumstances.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest analysis notes that federalism produces BOTH the strongest and the weakest rights regimes in the same country, so the question is whether a floor plus variation is preferable to uniformity — and the UK comparison, where rights law is reserved, provides the counterfactual.` },

      { q: '"The UK should adopt an entrenched bill of rights." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `SHOULD ADOPT:\n• Current rights rest on ordinary statute repealable by a simple Commons majority, with replacement of the HRA repeatedly proposed — protection depends on political restraint rather than legal guarantee.\n• Entrenchment would protect minorities against hostile majorities, which is precisely what rights are for.\n• It would give courts a real remedy rather than a declaration of incompatibility that leaves the offending statute in force.\n• Rights have been curtailed by counter-terrorism legislation and restrictions on protest with no effective legal check.\n• It would bring the UK into line with almost all comparable democracies.\n\nSHOULD NOT ADOPT:\n• Entrenchment is INCOMPATIBLE with parliamentary sovereignty as traditionally understood — no Parliament can bind its successors, so it requires a prior constitutional transformation, not a reform.\n• It transfers the final word on contested questions from elected politicians to appointed judges; the US experience shows this politicises appointments severely.\n• Dobbs shows entrenched rights can still be withdrawn by reinterpretation, so the security gained is less than it appears.\n• UK rights have proved STABLE in practice despite theoretical fragility, and ECHR membership provides an external backstop.\n• Legislated rights reach private discrimination and carry democratic legitimacy.\n\nGuidance for L5: must engage the SOVEREIGNTY OBJECTION directly — this is not merely a policy choice but a constitutional impossibility within the current framework. An answer that ignores it caps at L4.` },
    ]
  },

  /* ══════════════════════ PAPER 3 — POLITICAL IDEAS ══════════════════════ */

  '3.1': {
    name: 'Liberalism',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three core ideas of liberalism.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Individualism — the individual is the central unit of moral and political value, and society exists to serve individuals rather than the reverse. Analysis: distinguish EGOISTICAL individualism (classical, self-reliant) from DEVELOPMENTAL individualism (Mill, Green — potential to be realised).\n• Freedom — the supreme political value, but contested between NEGATIVE freedom (absence of constraint) and POSITIVE freedom (capacity to act and develop). Analysis: this ambiguity is the fault line between classical and modern liberalism.\n• Rationalism — human beings are capable of reason and can identify their own interests better than any authority. Analysis: the premise from which consent, rights, toleration and democracy all follow.\n• Equality — foundational and legal equality, and equality of OPPORTUNITY, but not of outcome.\n• The limited, constitutional state — government by consent, constrained by law and dispersed through checks and balances.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires the internal contestation, not just definitions.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three liberal views on the state.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Necessary but dangerous — Locke argued that in the state of nature rights exist but are insecure for want of an impartial judge, so a state is needed; but because it holds coercive power it is the chief threat to liberty.\n• Government by consent and fiduciary power — Locke held that government holds authority IN TRUST for the people, who may withdraw it if the trust is broken.\n• Constitutionalism — because power corrupts, liberals insist on limited government, the rule of law, separation of powers, checks and balances and entrenched rights.\n• The MINIMAL state (classical) — state action is inherently a restriction of liberty, so the state should be confined to protecting person, property and contract.\n• The ENABLING state (modern) — Green argued that poverty, ignorance and ill health are themselves constraints, so state provision EXPANDS freedom.\n\nAnalysis expected: both strands want the state LIMITED, accountable and constitutionally constrained; they disagree about its SIZE.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three differences between classical and modern liberalism.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Concept of freedom — classical liberals hold to NEGATIVE freedom (absence of interference), so freedom is maximised by minimising state action; modern liberals, following T.H. Green, argue that poverty, ignorance and ill health are themselves constraints, so genuine freedom requires the CAPACITY to act.\n• Role of the state — a minimal "night-watchman" state versus an ENABLING state providing education, healthcare and welfare.\n• The economy — laissez-faire and free markets as expressions of individual choice, versus managed capitalism, Keynesian demand management and regulation on the view that unregulated markets produce insecurity that itself restricts freedom.\n• Equality — formal and legal equality versus a substantive equality of opportunity requiring redistribution, most systematically in Rawls's difference principle.\n\nAnalysis expected: the CONTINUITY matters — both treat individual freedom as the supreme value; they disagree about means, not ends. Mill is the bridge.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three liberal thinkers\' views on freedom.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• LOCKE — freedom consists in the secure enjoyment of natural rights to life, liberty and property, which exist prior to government. Analysis: establishes liberalism's foundational move, placing the individual before the state; but says little about the social conditions in which rights can actually be exercised.\n• MILL — the harm principle: liberty may be restricted only to prevent harm to others, never for a person's own good. Analysis: the most influential liberal formulation, ruling out state paternalism and the legal enforcement of morality; but "harm" is contested, and Mill qualified the principle for children and those he regarded as not yet self-governing.\n• GREEN — positive freedom: freedom is the actual capacity for self-development, so poverty and ignorance are constraints as real as legal prohibition. Analysis: transformed liberalism by making an active state an instrument of freedom; but Berlin warned that positive freedom permits others to define a person's "real" interests and coerce them accordingly.\n• RAWLS — equal basic liberties plus the difference principle.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires the critical assessment of each, not just exposition.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three liberal views on human nature.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• RATIONALITY — human beings are capable of reason and can therefore identify their own interests better than any authority. Analysis: the premise from which consent, rights, toleration, free expression and democracy all follow, and the sharpest contrast with conservative human imperfection. It is also liberalism's most vulnerable claim, given evidence on systematic irrationality.\n• Self-interest and self-reliance (egoistical individualism) — classical liberals treat individuals as motivated primarily by their own interests, which the market channels productively.\n• Developmental potential — Mill regarded human beings as PROGRESSIVE beings capable of growth, and Green built the enabling state on the claim that people require certain conditions to develop. Analysis: this shift within liberalism is what permits an active state.\n• Foundational equality — all individuals are of equal moral worth. Analysis: Wollstonecraft showed the radical potential of this premise, since if rights derive from rationality and women are equally rational, excluding them is inconsistent with liberalism's own foundations.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires analysis of CONSEQUENCES.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three liberal views on the economy.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Classical laissez-faire — the free market is the economic expression of individual liberty, allowing people to dispose of their property and labour as they choose; competition allocates resources efficiently without central direction, and state intervention distorts incentives. Private property is both a natural right and a guarantee of independence from government.\n• Modern managed capitalism — modern liberals accept the market's efficiency but reject laissez-faire, arguing that unregulated markets produce booms, slumps, monopolies and insecurity that themselves restrict freedom. Drawing on Keynes, they support demand management, regulation and welfare funded through progressive taxation.\n• Rawls's difference principle — market inequalities are legitimate only where they work to the greatest benefit of the least advantaged.\n• Neo-liberalism — a twentieth-century revival of classical liberal economics: deregulation, privatisation and a minimal state. Note it belongs to the CLASSICAL lineage.\n\nAnalysis expected: all liberals accept private property and the market; the disagreement is about how far the market can be left to itself.` },

      { q: '"Liberals disagree more than they agree about the role of the state." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `DISAGREE:\n• Classical liberals favour a MINIMAL "night-watchman" state confined to protecting person, property and contract, on the view that state action is inherently a restriction of liberty.\n• Modern liberals favour an ENABLING state providing education, healthcare and welfare, on Green's view that poverty and ignorance are themselves constraints, so provision EXPANDS freedom.\n• The economic disagreement is fundamental — laissez-faire versus Keynesian management and redistribution.\n• Rawls's difference principle requires substantial redistribution; Nozick's entitlement theory (from a liberal premise) treats it as a violation of self-ownership.\n• Neo-liberalism revived the classical position in direct opposition to the post-war settlement.\n\nAGREE:\n• ALL liberals hold the state is NECESSARY but DANGEROUS — Locke's state of nature is inconvenient, not intolerable.\n• All insist on LIMITED, CONSTITUTIONAL government — the rule of law, separation of powers, checks and balances, government by consent.\n• All accept private property and a market economy; none advocates common ownership.\n• All prioritise individual freedom as the supreme value and reject the organic conception of society.\n• All support liberal democracy, with reservations about unconstrained majority rule.\n\nGuidance for L5: the decisive point is that liberals share the same ULTIMATE VALUE and reach opposite conclusions because FREEDOM ITSELF is ambiguous — the disagreement follows logically from a genuine conceptual ambiguity rather than from differing goals. That reframing reaches L5.` },

      { q: '"Liberalism is a coherent ideology." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `COHERENT:\n• All liberals share individualism, rationalism, foundational equality, the primacy of freedom, constitutional and limited government, and toleration.\n• The classical/modern disagreement is about MEANS, not ends — both seek to maximise individual freedom.\n• The disagreement follows logically from a genuine ambiguity in the concept of freedom (negative versus positive), so it is a principled division rather than incoherence.\n• Mill functions as a bridge, showing continuity rather than rupture between the strands.\n• Compared with conservatism — which contains both Burke's inherited obligation and Rand's rational egoism — liberalism is the MORE internally consistent tradition.\n\nNOT COHERENT:\n• Classical and modern liberals disagree fundamentally about the state, the economy and equality — Nozick and Rawls reach opposite conclusions from liberal premises.\n• Negative and positive freedom are not merely different emphases but arguably incompatible concepts; Berlin warned that positive freedom licenses coercion in the name of liberty.\n• Neo-liberalism and social liberalism operate as opposing political forces in practice.\n• The commitment to democracy is qualified — Mill's fear of majority tyranny sits awkwardly with popular sovereignty.\n\nGuidance for L5: must distinguish disagreement about MEANS (compatible with coherence) from disagreement about FOUNDATIONAL PREMISES (not). The strongest conclusions test liberalism against a comparator ideology.` },

      { q: '"Liberals agree on the importance of equality." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `AGREE:\n• All liberals accept FOUNDATIONAL equality — all individuals are of equal moral worth and entitled to equal rights.\n• All accept FORMAL (legal) equality — equality before the law, no legal privilege, which underpinned the extension of rights to previously excluded groups.\n• All accept EQUALITY OF OPPORTUNITY — a fair starting point, and all reject inherited privilege and ascribed status.\n• All reject equality of OUTCOME as incompatible with individual freedom and merit.\n• Wollstonecraft and Friedan show the tradition's capacity to extend its own principles to excluded groups.\n\nDISAGREE:\n• What equality of opportunity REQUIRES is fundamentally contested — classical liberals hold that formal equality suffices; modern liberals that it is meaningless without education, healthcare and welfare to make it real.\n• Rawls's difference principle demands that inequalities benefit the least advantaged, requiring substantial redistribution; Nozick holds that a distribution is just if holdings were justly acquired and transferred, whatever the pattern.\n• Neo-liberals regard redistributive taxation as a violation of property rights.\n• Some liberals accept positive discrimination; others regard it as a breach of formal equality.\n\nGuidance for L5: the agreement is at the level of PRINCIPLE and the disagreement at the level of IMPLICATION — foundational equality underdetermines the economic conclusions, which is why liberalism contains both the welfare state and its fiercest critics.` },

      { q: '"Liberals have always supported democracy." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `SUPPORTED:\n• Democracy follows from CONSENT — Locke's fiduciary power means government holds authority in trust for the people, implying popular control.\n• It follows from RATIONALISM and foundational equality — if individuals are rational and of equal worth, they are entitled to a say in their own government.\n• Liberals led the historic extension of the franchise; Wollstonecraft and Mill both argued for women's political rights.\n• Democracy provides accountability and the peaceful removal of government.\n• Modern liberalism is essentially inseparable from liberal democracy.\n\nQUALIFIED SUPPORT / NOT ALWAYS:\n• Mill feared the TYRANNY OF THE MAJORITY, arguing that majorities and prevailing social opinion could oppress dissenters as effectively as any despot — hence the liberal insistence on constitutional constraints and entrenched rights.\n• Mill himself proposed PLURAL VOTING weighted by education, which is incompatible with democratic equality.\n• Early liberals often supported property qualifications restricting the franchise.\n• Liberals support LIBERAL democracy — constrained by rights, courts and constitutions — not simple majoritarianism, and will accept counter-majoritarian institutions to protect individuals.\n• Rawls's principles are derived from a hypothetical original position, not from a vote.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest conclusion is that liberals support democracy INSTRUMENTALLY, as the system most compatible with consent and accountability, rather than as an end in itself — which is precisely why they are willing to constrain it.` },
    ]
  },

  '3.2': {
    name: 'Conservatism',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three core ideas of conservatism.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Human imperfection — people are psychologically limited (needing security and order), morally flawed (capable of selfishness and cruelty) and intellectually limited (reason cannot grasp a world too complex to redesign). Analysis: this last limb generates conservative caution about radical reform.\n• Tradition — established customs and institutions embody ACCUMULATED PRACTICAL WISDOM tested by generations, more reliable than the reasoning of any single generation; Burke's partnership between the living, the dead and those yet to be born.\n• Organic society — a living whole that has evolved, whose parts are interdependent, naturally hierarchical but with hierarchy carrying OBLIGATION. Burke's "little platoons".\n• Pragmatism — judging policies by what works rather than by conformity to doctrine.\n• Change to conserve — cautious reform is the CONDITION of preservation, not its opposite.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires analysis of how the ideas connect.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three conservative views on human nature.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• PSYCHOLOGICAL imperfection — human beings are needy, restless and fearful of isolation and instability, so they require security and order. Hobbes's state of nature is the extreme statement: without a common power, life would be one of continual insecurity.\n• MORAL imperfection — people are capable of selfishness, cruelty and violence, which is why law, authority and punishment are necessary rather than optional.\n• INTELLECTUAL imperfection — reason is limited and the social world too complex to be fully understood, let alone redesigned. Analysis: this is the philosophical basis of conservative scepticism about grand schemes, and of Oakeshott's anti-rationalism.\n• The New Right exception — Rand's rational, self-creating egoist is close to the opposite view, which is why her conservative credentials are contested.\n\nAnalysis expected: human imperfection is the SHARPEST contrast with liberalism's rational individual and socialism's malleable, cooperative human nature.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three conservative views on society.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• ORGANIC rather than mechanical — society is a living whole that has evolved naturally, not a machine that can be dismantled and redesigned. Its parts are interdependent, so change to one institution has unpredictable effects on others. Analysis: the principal argument against radical reform.\n• HIERARCHICAL — inequality of position, wealth and authority is natural and inevitable, arising from differences in ability and function; attempts to abolish hierarchy are futile and destructive of order.\n• Bound by OBLIGATION — hierarchy carries duty. Those with wealth and position owe an obligation to those below them, which is the basis of paternalism and of Disraeli's One Nation, and which distinguishes conservative hierarchy from mere privilege.\n• Rooted in SMALL INSTITUTIONS — Burke's "little platoons" (family, parish, locality, voluntary association) are where loyalty and moral habit are acquired.\n\nAnalysis expected: contrast with liberal society as a collection of individuals and socialist society as structured by class conflict.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three differences between One Nation conservatism and the New Right.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• The state and welfare — One Nation accepts intervention and the welfare state out of paternalistic obligation and to prevent social division becoming dangerous (Disraeli's "two nations"); the New Right's neo-liberal strand regards welfare dependency as undermining self-reliance and would roll the state back economically.\n• The economy — mixed economy and managed capitalism versus free markets, privatisation, deregulation and lower direct taxation.\n• Philosophical basis — One Nation rests on organic society, obligation and Burkean pragmatism; the neo-liberal strand rests on abstract rational individualism (Rand, Nozick), which Burke and Oakeshott would identify as precisely the rationalism they warned against.\n• Attitude to change — One Nation is cautious and evolutionary; Thatcherism was radical and disruptive of established institutions and communities.\n\nAnalysis expected: the New Right's conservative credentials are genuinely contested, and the internal tension between its neo-liberal and neo-conservative strands is examinable.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three conservative thinkers\' views on the state.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• HOBBES — because human beings are needy, restless and in competition, individuals rationally contract to submit to an ABSOLUTE SOVEREIGN whose authority must be undivided; divided sovereignty invites civil war. Analysis: establishes that order precedes liberty, but reasons from an abstract social contract — the very method Burke and Oakeshott reject.\n• BURKE — authority rests on inherited tradition and an intergenerational partnership that individuals do not choose; the state should reform cautiously in order to conserve. Analysis: a wholly different foundation from Hobbes, reaching similar conclusions about the need for authority.\n• NOZICK — a MINIMAL or night-watchman state confined to protection against force, theft and fraud and the enforcement of contracts; redistributive taxation violates self-ownership by treating a person's labour as a resource for others. Analysis: the most radical position, and the hardest to reconcile with organic society and paternalism.\n• One Nation paternalism — the state should relieve distress to maintain cohesion.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires the tensions between them.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three conservative views on tradition and change.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Tradition as ACCUMULATED WISDOM — institutions that have survived have been tested by generations of experience, which is more reliable than the abstract reasoning of any single generation. Burke described society as a partnership between the living, the dead and those yet to be born, so the present generation are trustees rather than owners.\n• CHANGE TO CONSERVE — Burke's principle that a state without the means of some change is without the means of its conservation. Reform is the CONDITION of preservation, not its opposite, but should grow out of existing practice rather than from a blueprint.\n• OAKESHOTT'S ANTI-RATIONALISM — abstract theory cannot capture the complexity of social practice; rationalists recognise only technical knowledge and discard the practical knowledge acquired through experience that makes governing possible. Politics is navigating a boundless sea with no appointed destination.\n• The American/French contrast — Burke supported the American colonists because they defended established rights, and opposed the French because they destroyed an inheritance to build from theory.\n\nGuidance: the Burke test — whether change GROWS FROM existing practice — is the essential analytical point.` },

      { q: '"Conservatives disagree more than they agree." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `DISAGREE:\n• Human nature — Burke and Oakeshott regard people as imperfect, dependent and formed by social inheritance; Rand treats the rational, self-interested individual as the highest value and altruism as a denial of individual worth. These are close to opposites.\n• The basis of authority — Hobbes grounds it in a rational contract between individuals; Burke in inherited tradition nobody chooses. Fundamentally different METHODS.\n• The economy and the state — One Nation accepts a mixed economy, welfare and intervention; neo-liberals would dismantle them.\n• Obligation — paternalism and noblesse oblige versus Rand's rejection of altruism as duty.\n• Method — Oakeshott's anti-rationalism versus Rand and Nozick reasoning from abstract first principles about rights.\n\nAGREE:\n• Most accept human imperfection and the limits of reason.\n• Most value order, authority and property.\n• All are sceptical of utopian schemes and of remaking society from theory.\n• All defend private property as a basis of social order and independence.\n• All prefer the tried to the untried, and accept that some hierarchy is inevitable.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest answers argue that the disagreements are FOUNDATIONAL (human nature, the basis of authority, obligation) rather than merely about means — which is a stronger claim than can be made about liberalism or socialism — and that this is why the New Right's conservative credentials remain genuinely contested.` },

      { q: '"Conservatism is not an ideology but a defence of privilege." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `A DEFENCE OF PRIVILEGE:\n• It defends hierarchy, inherited wealth and established institutions that happen to advantage the already-advantaged.\n• Its pragmatism allows it to abandon positions when they cease to serve those interests, suggesting no fixed principle.\n• Burke opposed the French Revolution while defending the aristocratic order.\n• Paternalism can be read as welfare motivated by FEAR of revolution rather than by principle — Disraeli's "two nations" is explicitly about the danger of social division.\n• Oakeshott presents conservatism as a disposition rather than a creed, arguably conceding it has no doctrine.\n\nA COHERENT IDEOLOGY:\n• It rests on identifiable philosophical claims — human imperfection, the limits of reason, the organic nature of society, the value of accumulated experience — from which its scepticism about radical change follows LOGICALLY.\n• Pragmatism is a CONSEQUENCE of the epistemology (reason is limited, so judge by experience), not the absence of one.\n• It has clear positions on human nature, society, the state and the economy — the standard tests for an ideology.\n• Named thinkers with developed philosophical arguments: Hobbes, Burke, Oakeshott, Rand, Nozick.\n• It has supported reform that damaged established interests when the argument required it.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest evidence for the charge is NOT pragmatism but the INCOMPATIBILITY between its strands — a tradition containing both Burke's inherited obligation and Rand's rational egoism holds positions that cannot both be true. That is a sharper argument than the privilege claim.` },

      { q: '"The New Right is not genuinely conservative." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `NOT GENUINELY CONSERVATIVE:\n• Its neo-liberal strand grounds politics in ABSTRACT RATIONAL INDIVIDUALISM (Rand's objectivism, Nozick's self-ownership) — precisely the rationalism Burke and Oakeshott identify as the error that produced the French Revolution.\n• It is RADICAL and disruptive: privatisation, deregulation and market liberalisation dissolved settled communities and established institutions rather than conserving them.\n• Rand rejects altruism as a duty, which is irreconcilable with the paternalism and social obligation running from Burke through Disraeli.\n• It treats society as atomistic, not organic.\n• Its programme was ideological rather than pragmatic — applying doctrine consistently, which conservatism traditionally distrusts.\n\nGENUINELY CONSERVATIVE:\n• Its NEO-CONSERVATIVE strand is recognisably conservative — social authority, traditional morality, law and order, national sovereignty, the traditional family.\n• Defence of private property and suspicion of state power have long conservative lineage.\n• It responded PRAGMATICALLY to the perceived failure of the post-war settlement in the 1970s.\n• Conservatism has always adapted to circumstances; the post-war acceptance of the welfare state was itself an adaptation.\n• Hobbes also reasons from abstract premises, yet is a named conservative thinker.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest analysis notes the INTERNAL TENSION — the New Right wants the state rolled back economically and strengthened socially, and free markets dissolve the very communities neo-conservatives wish to defend. Whether that is incoherence or a workable synthesis is the real question.` },

      { q: '"Conservatives agree about human nature." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `AGREE:\n• Most conservatives accept HUMAN IMPERFECTION in three respects — psychological (needing security and order), moral (capable of selfishness and cruelty) and intellectual (reason is limited).\n• Hobbes, Burke and Oakeshott all treat people as limited and dependent, requiring authority, tradition or established practice to guide them.\n• All reject the liberal rational individual and the socialist malleable, perfectible human nature.\n• All conclude that radical reform based on abstract theory is dangerous because people cannot understand society well enough to redesign it.\n• All accept that some hierarchy is a natural consequence of unequal ability.\n\nDISAGREE:\n• RAND's rational, self-creating egoist is close to the opposite of Burkean imperfection — she treats the individual as the highest value and reason as the only means of knowledge.\n• NOZICK's self-owning individual reasons from abstract rights, presupposing a rational agent capable of exactly the reasoning Oakeshott distrusts.\n• Hobbes's people are calculating and rational enough to construct a social contract; Burke's are formed by inheritance and could not.\n• One Nation paternalism assumes the privileged are capable of recognising obligation; Rand denies altruism is a duty at all.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest conclusion is that TRADITIONAL conservatives agree closely while the NEW RIGHT introduces a fundamentally different anthropology — which is the deepest evidence that conservatism may contain two incompatible traditions rather than one.` },
    ]
  },

  '3.3': {
    name: 'Socialism',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three core ideas of socialism.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Collectivism — human beings are social beings who achieve more through collective action than individual effort, and cannot be understood as the isolated pre-social individuals of liberal theory.\n• Common humanity and fraternity — people are bound by a shared humanity, so solidarity and cooperation are more authentic expressions of human nature than competition.\n• Equality — the defining socialist value. Socialists regard equality of OPPORTUNITY as inadequate, since a fair race is meaningless when competitors start from radically different positions; they seek equality of OUTCOME on the grounds that needs are broadly similar and inequality is socially produced.\n• Social class — a person's relationship to the means of production shapes life chances, consciousness and political interests.\n• Common ownership and workers' control — private ownership is the source of exploitation.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires analysis of how the ideas connect and where socialists divide.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three socialist views on human nature.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• MALLEABILITY — human nature is shaped by social and economic conditions rather than fixed. Competitive, selfish and acquisitive behaviour is a PRODUCT of capitalist social relations, not an inherent characteristic. Analysis: this is the foundation of socialist optimism, since changing structures can change behaviour.\n• NATURAL COOPERATION — human beings have a capacity for fraternity and solidarity that different institutions would allow to flourish.\n• SOCIAL BEINGS — individuals are formed by the communities and classes they belong to, so the liberal atomised individual is an abstraction.\n• Marx's ALIENATION — under capitalism workers are estranged from the product of their labour, from the act of production, from their own human potential and from one another, so capitalism prevents the expression of authentic human nature.\n\nAnalysis expected: the sharpest contrast with conservative human imperfection and with liberal rational self-interest. The standard objection — that socialism underestimates self-interest — is met by the reply that observed behaviour is produced by existing institutions.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three socialist views on equality.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Rejection of equality of OPPORTUNITY as sufficient — a fair race is meaningless when competitors start from radically different positions, and formal equality of opportunity within a grossly unequal society merely LEGITIMISES the resulting inequality.\n• Equality of OUTCOME — sought on three grounds: needs are broadly similar; inequality is produced by unequal social structures rather than differences in merit; and material equality underpins genuine social solidarity.\n• Revolutionary socialists hold that outcome equality requires ABOLISHING private ownership of the means of production, since exploitation is structural.\n• Social democrats (Crosland) sought not absolute equality but "democratic equality" — a substantial narrowing of outcome inequality — through progressive taxation, welfare and comprehensive education while retaining a market economy.\n• Third Way socialists (Giddens) moved BACK towards equality of opportunity, emphasising social investment and employability.\n\nAnalysis expected: the Giddens shift is why the Third Way's socialist credentials are contested — Crosland kept the goal of reducing outcome inequality and changed the instrument; Giddens narrowed the goal to equality of opportunity.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three differences between revolutionary socialism and social democracy.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• View of capitalism — revolutionary socialists hold it cannot be reformed because exploitation is intrinsic to the ownership of the means of production; social democrats hold it can be humanised through redistribution, welfare and regulation.\n• View of the STATE — this is the fundamental disagreement. Marx and Luxemburg regarded the state as an instrument of class rule whose structure serves capital and will resist fundamental change; Webb regarded it as broadly neutral, capturable through elections and directable towards socialist ends.\n• Method — revolution and mass action versus incremental parliamentary reform ("the inevitability of gradualness").\n• Ownership — common ownership versus a mixed economy with selective nationalisation, later abandoned altogether.\n• Agency — the organised working class versus persuasion of elites and permeation of institutions.\n\nAnalysis expected: the disagreement turns on an EMPIRICAL question about the nature of the state, and the evidence is mixed — social democrats did win power and build welfare states, but did not abolish capitalism.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three socialist thinkers\' views on achieving socialism.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• MARX and ENGELS — capitalism's internal contradictions produce recurring crises and immiseration; the proletariat develops class consciousness and overthrows the system, passing through a dictatorship of the proletariat to a classless society in which the state withers away. The parliamentary road is rejected because the state is not neutral.\n• LUXEMBURG — revolution through the MASS STRIKE and the spontaneous action of workers, whose consciousness develops through the struggle itself. She attacked Bernstein's revisionism (reforms cannot abolish exploitation) AND Lenin's vanguardism (a party substituting itself for the class would end in the dictatorship of a few).\n• WEBB — Fabian gradualism and "the inevitability of gradualness" (Sidney Webb's 1923 phrase); socialism arrives through incremental reform as the electorate recognises its merits, with great faith in expert administration and permeation of institutions.\n• CROSLAND — revisionism; managerial capitalism separated ownership from control, so socialist ends could be achieved by taxing growth rather than nationalising.\n\nGuidance: credit the tensions between them.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three socialist views on the economy.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Common ownership (Marx, Luxemburg) — the means of production must pass into collective ownership, because private ownership is itself the mechanism of exploitation through the appropriation of SURPLUS VALUE. Analysis: identifies a structural cause rather than a distributional symptom, which is why reform is held insufficient.\n• Social democratic managed capitalism (Crosland) — managerial capitalism had separated ownership from control, so nationalisation was unnecessary; socialist ends could be achieved by taxing the proceeds of GROWTH and spending them on welfare, public services and comprehensive education.\n• Third Way market socialism (Giddens) — accept the market as the most effective generator of wealth while directing it to social ends through a social investment state emphasising skills and employability.\n• Webb's co-operative movement and the national minimum.\n\nAnalysis expected: Crosland's strategy depends entirely on sustained growth, which is why stagflation and later globalisation left it without a mechanism.` },

      { q: '"Socialists disagree more than they agree." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `DISAGREE:\n• The nature of the STATE — Marx and Luxemburg regard it as an instrument of class rule that will resist fundamental change; Webb and Crosland as a neutral instrument capturable through elections. This determines everything else.\n• METHOD — revolution versus gradual parliamentary reform; Luxemburg argued reforms merely stabilise capitalism.\n• COMMON OWNERSHIP — essential for Marx and Luxemburg; abandoned by Crosland and Giddens.\n• The GOAL itself — Crosland kept the goal of substantially reducing outcome inequality and changed the instrument; Giddens narrowed the goal to equality of opportunity.\n• The role of the PARTY — Luxemburg's spontaneous mass action versus Lenin's vanguard.\n\nAGREE:\n• All hold human nature is MALLEABLE and shaped by social conditions, with a natural capacity for cooperation.\n• All are COLLECTIVIST, rejecting the liberal atomised individual.\n• All prioritise EQUALITY and regard inequality as socially produced rather than merited.\n• All are hostile to the inequalities capitalism produces and believe society can be improved by changing its structures.\n• All accept that class shapes life chances.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest analysis notes that socialism's disagreements concern MEANS and the nature of the state, while its agreements concern ENDS and the analysis of human nature — and that the Third Way is the genuinely hard case, because it may have changed the end rather than the means.` },

      { q: '"The Third Way represents the abandonment of socialism." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `ABANDONMENT:\n• Accepts MARKET ALLOCATION as the most effective generator of wealth — the central premise of liberalism, not socialism.\n• Dropped COMMON OWNERSHIP entirely; Clause IV was revised in 1995 to endorse a dynamic market economy.\n• Replaced the aim of reducing inequality of OUTCOME with equality of OPPORTUNITY — a liberal rather than a socialist standard.\n• Made no challenge to the ownership of capital or to the structure producing inequality.\n• Retained privatisation and trade union legislation, and accepted Conservative spending limits.\n• Emphasised rights balanced by RESPONSIBILITIES in welfare, importing a conditionality socialists traditionally resisted.\n\nREVISIONISM, NOT ABANDONMENT:\n• It retained the commitment to REDUCING INEQUALITY and to collective provision, pursued through the means available in a globalised economy.\n• It stands in Crosland's revisionist tradition, which socialism has always accommodated.\n• It delivered the minimum wage, tax credits, substantial public investment and reductions in child and pensioner poverty.\n• A purer position would not have won power to deliver anything.\n• Socialism has always adapted its means to circumstances; Webb and Crosland both did so.\n\nGuidance for L5: the decisive distinction is between CROSLAND (changed the instrument, kept the goal of substantially reducing outcome inequality) and GIDDENS (changed the goal to opportunity). That distinction is what determines the answer, and an essay that does not make it caps at L3.` },

      { q: '"Socialists agree about the role of the state." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `AGREE:\n• All socialists see a role for COLLECTIVE ACTION through public institutions to reduce inequality and meet need.\n• All reject the minimal state of classical liberalism and the market's capacity to deliver just outcomes unaided.\n• All accept some form of public provision — welfare, education, healthcare.\n• All regard the state as an instrument for pursuing equality, whether by capturing it or replacing it.\n\nDISAGREE FUNDAMENTALLY:\n• Marx and Luxemburg regard the existing state as an INSTRUMENT OF CLASS RULE whose structure serves capital; it must be overthrown, and after a transitional dictatorship of the proletariat it will WITHER AWAY.\n• Webb regarded it as broadly NEUTRAL — capturable through elections and directable towards socialist ends by expert administration.\n• Crosland saw it as the vehicle for taxing growth and funding welfare, without needing to own the means of production.\n• Giddens recast it as a SOCIAL INVESTMENT STATE — an enabler investing in skills and employability rather than a redistributor.\n• Anarchist-influenced socialists reject the state entirely.\n\nGuidance for L5: this is arguably socialism's DEEPEST division, because the view of the state determines the view of method, and the view of method determines whether capitalism can be reformed. An answer recognising that the state question is PRIOR to the others reaches L5.` },

      { q: '"Socialism is fundamentally about equality." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `FUNDAMENTALLY ABOUT EQUALITY:\n• Equality is the value that most sharply distinguishes socialism from liberalism (freedom) and conservatism (order).\n• All socialists reject equality of opportunity as sufficient and regard inequality as socially produced rather than merited.\n• Crosland explicitly REDEFINED socialism around equality rather than common ownership, and that redefinition was widely accepted within the tradition.\n• The commitment to common ownership, workers' control and progressive taxation are all INSTRUMENTS for achieving equality.\n• Even the Third Way retained a commitment to reducing inequality, if by different means.\n\nNOT ONLY / NOT FUNDAMENTALLY:\n• COLLECTIVISM and COMMON HUMANITY are arguably prior — socialists value equality because they see people as fundamentally social and cooperative.\n• Marx's analysis is about EXPLOITATION and ALIENATION, not primarily about distribution; ending the appropriation of surplus value matters because it is unjust, not because it is unequal.\n• WORKERS' CONTROL concerns power and autonomy, not material distribution.\n• The disagreement about what equality MEANS (outcome versus opportunity) is so deep that "equality" may not identify a shared commitment at all.\n• Giddens's shift to equality of opportunity shows the concept can be retained while its content is emptied.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest analysis notes that "equality" is a CONTESTED concept within socialism, so agreeing it is fundamental does not establish agreement about anything substantive. That move reaches L5.` },
    ]
  },

  '3.4': {
    name: 'Nationalism',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three core ideas of nationalism.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• The NATION as the fundamental unit — humanity is naturally divided into nations, each with its own character, and the nation rather than the individual or the class is the primary object of political loyalty.\n• SELF-DETERMINATION — a nation has the right to govern itself rather than be ruled by another people. Analysis: the core political demand, connecting the cultural claim that a nation exists to the political claim that it should have a state.\n• The NATION-STATE — political and national boundaries should coincide. Analysis: nationalism's practical programme and its central practical difficulty, since populations are rarely neatly distributed; the post-1918 settlement left substantial stranded minorities.\n• CULTURALISM — Herder's Volksgeist, the unique spirit of a people expressed in language, folklore and history.\n• RACIALISM — some nationalisms define the nation by descent and biology, producing exclusion.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires analysis of consequences.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three differences between civic and ethnic nationalism.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• Definition of the nation — civic nationalism defines it by shared political values, institutions and citizenship; ethnic nationalism by common descent, ancestry, language and cultural inheritance.\n• Membership — civic membership is VOLUNTARY and in principle open to anyone who accepts the values; ethnic membership is INHERITED, so those without the ancestry cannot fully belong.\n• Character — civic nationalism is inclusive and compatible with diversity; ethnic nationalism is exclusive and tends towards the expulsion or subordination of outsiders.\n• Associated traditions — civic nationalism with liberal nationalism and the American and French revolutionary traditions; ethnic nationalism with romantic and integral nationalism, and with Maurras.\n\nAnalysis expected: this distinction largely determines whether a given nationalism tends towards liberal self-determination or towards exclusion and conflict — it is the most useful analytical tool in the topic.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three types of nationalism.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• LIBERAL nationalism (Mazzini) — every nation has an EQUAL right to self-determination; nations are like individuals, each entitled to freedom, and a world of free nations would cooperate peacefully, so nationalism and internationalism are compatible. Typically civic.\n• CONSERVATIVE nationalism — emphasises inherited tradition, shared culture and organic unity; defensive in character, used to bind society against internal division or external threat. Often deployed to displace class conflict.\n• ANTI-COLONIAL / postcolonial nationalism — the demand for independence from imperial rule, frequently fused with socialist analysis of economic exploitation (Gandhi, Nkrumah; Garvey's pan-Africanism). Analysis: the strongest case for nationalism as a liberating force.\n• EXPANSIONIST nationalism — chauvinistic, asserting national superiority and justifying domination or conquest; Maurras's integral nationalism.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires analysis of how each relates to the others.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three nationalist thinkers\' views on the nation.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• HERDER — each people possesses a Volksgeist, a unique spirit expressed in language, folklore, customs and history. Nations are organic and PRE-POLITICAL, existing whether or not they have a state. Analysis: Herder celebrated the distinctiveness of ALL cultures, implying their equal worth — he was not a chauvinist, though the cultural definition proved more easily turned to ethnic exclusion than a political one.\n• ROUSSEAU — sovereignty resides in the GENERAL WILL of the people, directed at the common good. Analysis: predates nationalism proper, but the idea that a PEOPLE is the source of authority provided the foundation for the claim that nations should rule themselves.\n• MAZZINI — every nation has an EQUAL right to self-determination; a world of free self-governing nations would cooperate peacefully.\n• MAURRAS — integral nationalism: the nation is an organic whole to which the individual is entirely subordinate, defined by descent and hostile to liberalism, democracy and outsiders.\n• GARVEY — black nationalism and pan-Africanism; racial pride and self-reliance in response to slavery and colonialism.\n\nGuidance: credit the tensions between them.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which nationalism has been a force for liberation.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• National unification and independence in nineteenth-century Europe — Mazzini's Italian Risorgimento and the 1848 national revolutions challenged foreign or autocratic rule in the name of self-government.\n• The dissolution of empires — the Austro-Hungarian and Ottoman empires broke up after 1918 on self-determination principles, and twentieth-century anti-colonial movements ended European rule across Asia and Africa.\n• Anti-colonial nationalism — combined the demand for independence with opposition to imperial economic exploitation, often drawing on socialist ideas (Gandhi, Nkrumah).\n• Garvey's black nationalism — asserted dignity, racial pride and self-reliance in response to slavery, colonialism and racial oppression, for a dispersed people denied a homeland.\n• Contemporary self-determination movements pursued democratically — Scotland, Catalonia.\n\nAnalysis expected: in each case nationalism extended self-government to people previously denied it, which is a recognisably progressive achievement.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three criticisms of nationalism.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• It necessarily creates OUTSIDERS — defining a nation defines those outside it, and where the nation is conceived ethnically and organically this has repeatedly justified exclusion, expulsion and genocide. Maurras's integral nationalism and the ethnic cleansings during the break-up of Yugoslavia illustrate this.\n• It can be EXPANSIONIST and chauvinistic — asserting national superiority rather than national equality, justifying conquest. Analysis: unlike Mazzini's principle, this cannot be universalised without contradiction.\n• Nations may be CONSTRUCTED rather than natural — the modernist account holds that nations were produced by print capitalism, mass education and industrialisation, and are "imagined communities" whose members never meet. If so, the claim that nations are a natural basis for political organisation is undermined.\n• It is practically unworkable — populations are rarely neatly distributed, so the nation-state ideal leaves stranded minorities, as the post-1918 settlement showed.\n• It can displace class and economic questions, serving conservative interests.\n\nGuidance: one-sided by design.` },

      { q: '"Nationalism is inherently dangerous." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `INHERENTLY DANGEROUS:\n• Defining a nation NECESSARILY defines outsiders, so exclusion is structural rather than incidental.\n• Where the nation is conceived ethnically and organically, this has justified expulsion and genocide — Maurras's integral nationalism; the ethnic cleansings during the break-up of Yugoslavia.\n• Expansionist nationalism asserts superiority and justifies conquest, and cannot be universalised without contradiction.\n• It can displace class and economic questions, serving established interests.\n• The nation-state ideal is practically unworkable, leaving stranded minorities and irredentist grievance, as after 1918.\n\nNOT INHERENTLY DANGEROUS:\n• CIVIC nationalism defines the nation by shared political values rather than descent, so membership is open and compatible with diversity.\n• Mazzini's liberal nationalism universalises its own claim — every nation has an EQUAL right to self-determination — and holds that free nations would cooperate peacefully.\n• Anti-colonial nationalism delivered self-government to peoples under imperial rule; Garvey's black nationalism asserted dignity against racial oppression.\n• Herder celebrated the distinctiveness of ALL cultures, implying equal worth.\n• National identity can underpin social solidarity and the willingness to fund collective provision.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest conclusion is that nationalism is CROSS-CUTTING — the premise that peoples should govern themselves does not by itself determine WHO COUNTS as the people, and that question is answered by whichever ideology nationalism fuses with. The danger therefore lies in particular variants, not in the core premise — but the civic/ethnic boundary is easier to cross than liberal nationalists assume.` },

      { q: '"Nationalists disagree more than they agree." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `DISAGREE:\n• Are nations EQUAL? Mazzini treats them as moral equals each entitled to freedom; Maurras asserts particular superiority. This is not a difference of degree but of LOGICAL FORM — one principle can be granted to everyone, the other cannot.\n• Cultural or political definition? Herder locates the nation in a Volksgeist existing prior to any state; Rousseau and Mazzini in a political community exercising sovereignty.\n• Inclusive or exclusive membership? Civic nationalism by shared values; ethnic by inherited descent.\n• Relationship to the individual — liberal nationalism holds the nation serves its members' freedom; integral nationalism subordinates the individual entirely.\n• Compatibility with internationalism — Mazzini says yes; expansionist nationalism says no.\n\nAGREE:\n• All hold humanity is naturally divided into NATIONS and that the nation is the primary object of political loyalty.\n• All value SELF-DETERMINATION for their own nation, at minimum.\n• All regard national identity and culture as politically significant.\n• All reject a purely universal or cosmopolitan basis for political organisation.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest conclusion is that nationalism is best understood as a CROSS-CUTTING ideology attaching to others rather than standing alone — which is why Mazzini and Maurras share only the vocabulary of the nation. That claim reaches L5.` },

      { q: '"Nationalism and internationalism are compatible." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `COMPATIBLE:\n• Mazzini's liberal nationalism holds that every nation has an EQUAL right to self-determination, so a world of free self-governing nations would cooperate peacefully — the principle is universalisable.\n• Herder celebrated the distinctiveness of all cultures, implying equal worth rather than hierarchy, and therefore cultural pluralism.\n• Self-determination was the founding principle of the post-1918 settlement and of the United Nations system.\n• Anti-colonial nationalists frequently cooperated internationally — pan-Africanism explicitly transcended individual national boundaries.\n• Nation-states are the building blocks of international institutions; there can be no international order without them.\n\nINCOMPATIBLE:\n• Nationalism prioritises the national interest, which by definition may conflict with international obligations.\n• EXPANSIONIST and integral nationalism assert national SUPERIORITY, which cannot be reconciled with the equality international cooperation presupposes.\n• Defining a nation creates outsiders, generating suspicion of foreigners and of supranational bodies.\n• Nationalism has repeatedly been mobilised AGAINST international institutions — Brexit and the EU being a contemporary case.\n• The post-1918 application of self-determination produced stranded minorities, irredentism and eventually war.\n\nGuidance for L5: the answer depends entirely on WHICH nationalism — the compatibility question cannot be answered for nationalism as a whole, which is itself evidence that it is not a single ideology. That move reaches L5.` },

      { q: '"Nations are constructed rather than natural." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `CONSTRUCTED (modernism):\n• Nations were produced by print capitalism, mass education, industrialisation and state-building, all of which are modern developments.\n• They are "imagined communities" — members never meet the overwhelming majority of their compatriots yet feel a shared bond, which must therefore be culturally produced.\n• National languages, histories and traditions were frequently STANDARDISED or invented by states in the nineteenth century.\n• National boundaries are historically contingent, and many nations postdate the states that created them.\n• If nations are recent constructions, the claim that they represent a NATURAL basis for political organisation is undermined.\n\nNATURAL (primordialism — what nationalists themselves assume):\n• Herder's Volksgeist treats nations as organic and pre-political, rooted in language and culture that long predate modern states.\n• Some national identities demonstrably predate print capitalism and industrialisation.\n• Shared language, history and territory are real, not invented.\n• The persistence and emotional force of national identity across centuries and through attempts at suppression suggests something deeper than construction.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest reply to modernism is that CONSTRUCTED IDENTITIES ARE ENTIRELY REAL IN THEIR EFFECTS — people demonstrably feel national loyalty and act on it, whatever its historical origin, and a political theory must deal with identities as they are rather than as their genealogy suggests they ought to be. That reaches L5.` },
    ]
  },

  '3.5': {
    name: 'Feminism',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three core ideas of feminism.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• The SEX/GENDER distinction — sex refers to biological difference, gender to socially constructed roles and expectations. Analysis: undermines the argument that women's subordinate position is natural and therefore inevitable; de Beauvoir's claim that one is not born but BECOMES a woman expresses this directly.\n• PATRIARCHY — a system of social structures, practices and beliefs through which men as a group dominate women as a group. Analysis: identifies subordination as SYSTEMIC rather than the product of individual prejudice, locating it in the family, the labour market, law, culture and sexuality.\n• THE PERSONAL IS POLITICAL — private-sphere relationships are structured by power and are therefore properly political.\n• EQUALITY and DIFFERENCE — whether to minimise the significance of sex difference or to recognise and value it.\n• INTERSECTIONALITY — race, class and gender oppression interlock rather than adding up.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires analysis of consequences.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three differences between liberal and radical feminism.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• DIAGNOSIS — liberal feminists locate the problem in legal discrimination, unequal opportunity and cultural conditioning (Friedan's "problem that has no name"); radical feminists locate it in PATRIARCHY itself, embedded in the family, sexuality and culture.\n• REMEDY — liberal feminists seek reform within existing structures (equal rights legislation, equal pay, access to education and the professions); radical feminists regard this as insufficient, since formal public-sphere equality leaves patriarchy intact, and call for transformation of personal life.\n• The PUBLIC/PRIVATE DIVIDE — liberal feminism largely ACCEPTS it, campaigning in the public sphere; radical feminism REJECTS it, arguing the divide is the mechanism by which women's subordination is shielded from scrutiny.\n• Relationship to men — liberal feminism seeks integration on equal terms; some radical feminists regard men as a class with an interest in maintaining patriarchy.\n\nAnalysis expected: liberal feminism delivered most of the concrete legal gains; radical feminism explains why legal equality has not produced substantive equality.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three feminist views on the family.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• RADICAL — Millett regards the family as the chief instrument of patriarchal socialisation, conditioning both sexes into accepting male dominance as natural. Patriarchy is reproduced through consent as much as through force, so a cultural revolution rather than legal reform is required.\n• SOCIALIST — Rowbotham and others argue unpaid domestic labour within the family REPRODUCES THE WORKFORCE at no cost to employers, and the nuclear family transmits property and instils discipline. The family therefore serves capitalism as well as patriarchy.\n• GILMAN — women's subordination is rooted in ECONOMIC DEPENDENCE reinforced by confinement to the domestic sphere; her solution was socialising domestic labour through communal kitchens, laundries and childcare.\n• LIBERAL — Friedan focused on the cultural conditioning that persuaded educated women domesticity should fulfil them, seeking access to public life rather than abolition of the family.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires the differences between these positions.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three feminist views on patriarchy.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• RADICAL — patriarchy is the FUNDAMENTAL and original form of oppression, prior to and deeper than class, embedded in the family, sexuality and culture. Millett argues it operates through culture — literature, art, religion — conditioning both sexes into accepting male dominance as natural.\n• SOCIALIST — patriarchy is bound up with CAPITALISM, which benefits from unpaid domestic labour and from women as a cheap reserve army of labour. Rowbotham insisted socialist analysis must address women's specific oppression rather than treating it as derivative of class.\n• DUAL-SYSTEMS — patriarchy and capitalism are distinct but MUTUALLY REINFORCING systems, an attempt to hold both accounts together.\n• INTERSECTIONAL — hooks argues patriarchy cannot be analysed in isolation, since race, class and gender oppression interlock to produce distinct experiences.\n• LIBERAL — some liberal feminists are sceptical of the concept, preferring to analyse discrimination and conditioning without positing a single system.\n\nGuidance: the key disagreement is whether patriarchy is PRIOR to or DERIVATIVE of economic structures.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three feminist thinkers\' contributions to the ideology.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• DE BEAUVOIR — "one is not born, but rather becomes, a woman": femininity is a socially constructed identity imposed through culture, not a consequence of biology. Woman is defined as "the OTHER", in relation to man as the norm, and therefore as deviation rather than as an autonomous subject.\n• MILLETT — Sexual Politics: relations between the sexes are POLITICAL, and patriarchy operates through culture and personal life as well as institutions, with the family as its chief instrument.\n• GILMAN — women's subordination is rooted in ECONOMIC DEPENDENCE; the solution is socialising domestic labour so women can enter paid employment on equal terms.\n• ROWBOTHAM — socialist feminism; unpaid domestic labour and the reserve army of labour serve capitalism, and women have been "hidden from history".\n• hooks — INTERSECTIONALITY; mainstream feminism presented white middle-class experience as universal.\n\nGuidance: credit the tensions between them.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which feminists disagree about how to achieve equality.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• REFORM versus TRANSFORMATION — liberal feminists pursue legal equality and equal opportunity within existing structures; radical and socialist feminists regard this as inadequate because the structures themselves produce the inequality. Analysis: the evidence supports both partially — legal reform delivered the vote and equal pay legislation, but substantive inequality persists alongside formal legal equality.\n• EQUALITY versus DIFFERENCE — equality feminists seek to minimise the political significance of sex difference and secure identical treatment; difference feminists argue a standard modelled on men's lives disadvantages women. Analysis: a genuine dilemma, since identical treatment ignores pregnancy and caring, while recognising difference risks essentialism.\n• Whether feminism can speak for ALL women — hooks and intersectional theorists argue a feminism built on white middle-class experience cannot represent women whose oppression is shaped equally by race and class.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires the analysis of why each disagreement is genuine rather than resolvable.` },

      { q: '"Feminists disagree more than they agree." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `DISAGREE:\n• Incompatible FOUNDATIONS — liberal feminism rests on liberal individualism, socialist feminism on a Marxist analysis of capitalism, radical feminism on the primacy of patriarchy. These are not variations but different theoretical systems.\n• Different DIAGNOSES — legal discrimination, capitalism, or patriarchy as the root cause — and therefore different remedies.\n• The PUBLIC/PRIVATE divide — liberal feminism accepts it, radical feminism rejects it as the mechanism of concealment.\n• EQUALITY versus DIFFERENCE — whether to minimise or value sex difference.\n• Whether "WOMAN" is even a stable category — postmodern feminists and intersectional theorists question the shared subject on which the movement depends.\n\nAGREE:\n• All hold women are SYSTEMATICALLY DISADVANTAGED because of their sex.\n• All hold this disadvantage is SOCIALLY PRODUCED rather than natural — all accept the sex/gender distinction and reject biological determinism.\n• All hold it SHOULD BE ENDED.\n• All reject the idea that existing arrangements are just or inevitable.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest conclusion is that solidarity can rest on a shared POLITICAL PROJECT rather than an assumed identity of experience — arguably a firmer foundation than the false universalism hooks exposed. An answer that treats the disagreements as fatal, without addressing what the strands share, caps at L3.` },

      { q: '"The personal is political." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `THE CLAIM IS CORRECT:\n• Private-sphere relationships are structured by POWER — who does the housework, how childcare is distributed, the dynamics of marriage, sexuality and reproduction are instances of a wider system of male dominance.\n• Confining political concern to the public realm EXEMPTED from scrutiny the sphere in which most women's lives were lived and most of their subordination occurred.\n• It made domestic violence, marital rape, unpaid domestic labour, reproductive rights and sexual harassment into political questions requiring public remedy — a transformation in what counts as political.\n• Consciousness-raising rested on the insight that individual experiences shared across many women revealed a STRUCTURAL pattern.\n• It explains why legal equality in the public sphere has not produced substantive equality.\n\nOBJECTIONS:\n• LIBERALS value the private sphere as a zone of freedom protected from state intrusion, and worry that politicising it licenses surveillance of personal life.\n• The distinction between public and private protects individual autonomy, including women's.\n• Taken to its limit, no aspect of life remains free of political scrutiny.\n• Liberal feminists such as Friedan achieved substantial gains focusing on the PUBLIC sphere.\n• It risks treating all personal relationships as sites of oppression.\n\nGuidance for L5: the feminist reply to the liberal objection is decisive and should appear — the private sphere was never genuinely free FOR WOMEN, and a neutrality that leaves existing domestic power untouched is not neutrality but endorsement of the status quo.` },

      { q: '"Feminism is a coherent ideology." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `COHERENT:\n• All strands share three core claims — women are systematically disadvantaged because of their sex; this is socially produced rather than natural; and it should be ended.\n• All accept the SEX/GENDER distinction and reject biological determinism, which is a substantive shared theoretical commitment.\n• All identify structures rather than individual prejudice as the cause.\n• Shared concepts — patriarchy, gender, the critique of the public/private divide — provide a common analytical vocabulary.\n• Ideologies are generally internally contested; liberalism and socialism contain comparable divisions.\n\nNOT COHERENT:\n• The strands rest on INCOMPATIBLE FOUNDATIONS — liberal individualism, Marxist class analysis, and the primacy of patriarchy cannot all be true.\n• They diagnose different causes and prescribe incompatible remedies — reform within structures versus their abolition.\n• Intersectional and postmodern feminism question whether "WOMAN" is a stable category at all, which threatens the collective subject on whose behalf feminism claims to act.\n• Difference and equality feminism give opposite answers to what equal treatment requires.\n• Some strands regard men as structural opponents; others seek integration.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest resolution is that solidarity rests on a shared POLITICAL PROJECT rather than a shared theory or a common experience — which is a weaker but more defensible basis for coherence than the false universalism hooks exposed.` },

      { q: '"Sex and gender are separate concepts." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `SEPARATE:\n• Sex refers to BIOLOGICAL difference; gender to the SOCIAL roles, behaviours and expectations attached to those categories.\n• Gender roles vary enormously across societies and over time, which biological difference cannot explain.\n• De Beauvoir: one is not born but BECOMES a woman — femininity is imposed through culture, not a consequence of biology.\n• The distinction is politically essential: it undermines the argument that women's subordinate position is natural and therefore inevitable. If roles are constructed, they can be changed.\n• It is the analytical foundation of most feminist thought.\n\nNOT ENTIRELY SEPARATE / contested:\n• Some DIFFERENCE feminists argue genuine differences exist — particularly around reproduction and caring — that should be recognised and valued rather than erased.\n• Biology does impose some real constraints, notably pregnancy and childbirth, which policy must accommodate (maternity provision treats women differently precisely to enable equal participation).\n• POSTMODERN feminists question whether "sex" is itself an unmediated biological category rather than one already shaped by social interpretation, which collapses the distinction from the other direction.\n• Essentialist accounts hold that gender differences have some biological basis.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest analysis notes that the distinction is doing POLITICAL work as well as analytical work — it was developed precisely to defeat naturalistic justifications of inequality — and that both difference feminism and postmodern feminism destabilise it, though from opposite directions.` },
    ]
  },

  '3.6': {
    name: 'Multiculturalism',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three core ideas of multiculturalism.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• The POLITICS OF RECOGNITION — Taylor argues identity is dialogical, formed through interaction with others, so misrecognition inflicts real harm by imposing a demeaning picture. Public institutions should therefore positively acknowledge minority identities rather than treating culture as a private matter.\n• CULTURAL EMBEDDEDNESS — human beings are formed within particular cultures, which shape values, meanings and the sense of self; culture is not an optional accessory to an otherwise complete individual, which is why its erosion is a genuine harm.\n• MINORITY RIGHTS — specific entitlements such as language rights, exemptions from general laws, self-government provisions and representation guarantees.\n• DIVERSITY AND VALUE PLURALISM — Berlin's claim that there are many genuine human goods that cannot be ranked on a single scale, so no one culture has a monopoly on the good life.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires analysis of consequences, particularly the tension with liberal individualism.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three types of multiculturalism.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• LIBERAL multiculturalism (Kymlicka) — endorses diversity WITHIN LIBERAL LIMITS; practices must remain compatible with individual autonomy and equality. Culture is valuable as the CONTEXT OF CHOICE within which individuals exercise autonomy, so protecting minority cultures serves a liberal value. Distinguishes EXTERNAL PROTECTIONS (defended) from INTERNAL RESTRICTIONS (rejected).\n• PLURALIST multiculturalism (Parekh, drawing on Berlin) — accords value to non-liberal cultures IN THEIR OWN TERMS, on the view that liberalism is itself a particular tradition rather than a universal standard, so judging all cultures by liberal criteria is not neutrality but imposition.\n• COSMOPOLITAN multiculturalism — identity is FLUID; individuals draw on many cultures and construct hybrid identities, so diversity is a resource for self-creation rather than a set of communities with claims.\n\nAnalysis expected: the liberal version resolves the individual/group tension within liberalism; the pluralist version does not, which is why it is better described as post-liberal.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three thinkers\' contributions to multiculturalism.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• BERLIN — value pluralism: there are many genuine human goods that conflict irreducibly and cannot be ranked on a single scale. Analysis: if no culture has a monopoly on the good, diversity reflects the plural character of human value itself rather than being a regrettable fact to be tolerated.\n• KYMLICKA — a LIBERAL defence of minority rights, beginning from individual autonomy rather than the value of groups. Culture supplies the CONTEXT OF CHOICE, so its erosion diminishes autonomy; members of a majority culture enjoy this security automatically, so minority rights correct an inequality. His EXTERNAL PROTECTIONS / INTERNAL RESTRICTIONS distinction answers the standard liberal objection.\n• TAYLOR — the POLITICS OF RECOGNITION; liberal neutrality is illusory because a supposedly neutral public sphere reflects the majority culture.\n• PAREKH — a "COMMUNITY OF COMMUNITIES", with shared values negotiated through intercultural dialogue rather than inherited from the majority.\n• MODOOD — religious identity must be accommodated, since a secularism excluding religion is not neutral but disadvantages particular groups.\n\nGuidance: credit the tensions between them.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three criticisms of multiculturalism.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• The CONSERVATIVE COHESION critique — a stable society requires shared culture, common values and mutual obligation; endorsing separate identities produces "parallel lives", weakens the common national culture underpinning trust, and may erode the shared identity sustaining willingness to fund collective provision.\n• The LIBERAL INDIVIDUALIST critique — rights properly attach to individuals, not groups; conferring them on communities empowers self-appointed leaders, entrenches conservative elements and leaves dissenting members unprotected.\n• The GENDER critique (Okin) — the sharpest version: practices subordinating women may be shielded from scrutiny in the name of respecting culture, so protecting the group comes at the expense of its most vulnerable members.\n• ESSENTIALISM — multicultural policy treats cultures as bounded, homogeneous and internally uncontested when they are in fact diverse, contested and changing; requiring identifiable "community representatives" privileges particular voices as authentic.\n\nAnalysis expected: Kymlicka's internal-restrictions distinction answers much of the liberal critique; Parekh's dialogical model addresses cohesion.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three tensions between multiculturalism and liberalism.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• INDIVIDUAL versus GROUP — liberalism locates rights in individuals; multiculturalism accords significance and sometimes rights to groups. Where a community constrains its own members, liberalism requires protecting the individual AGAINST the group, which can mean overriding the cultural autonomy multiculturalism defends.\n• NEUTRALITY — liberalism aspires to a state neutral between conceptions of the good; Taylor argues this neutrality is illusory, since the supposedly neutral public sphere embodies majority culture in its language, holidays and institutional assumptions, so genuine equality requires POSITIVE RECOGNITION rather than official indifference.\n• AUTONOMY — liberalism values the capacity to revise and reject inherited values; some cultural practices, particularly regarding religious authority, gender roles and the upbringing of children, are premised on the TRANSMISSION of values rather than their critical examination.\n\nAnalysis expected: Kymlicka resolves much of this WITHIN liberalism through the external/internal distinction; Parekh's pluralism does not, which is why his version is post-liberal rather than liberal.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ways in which multiculturalism differs from assimilation.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• DEMAND ON MINORITIES — assimilation requires minorities to abandon distinctive practices and adopt the majority culture's values, language and customs as the condition of full membership; multiculturalism requires no such abandonment.\n• VIEW OF DIVERSITY — assimilation treats diversity as a TEMPORARY PROBLEM to be dissolved; multiculturalism treats it as a PERMANENT AND VALUABLE feature of society to be positively endorsed.\n• PUBLIC RECOGNITION — assimilation confines cultural identity to the private sphere; multiculturalism holds, following Taylor, that identity requires PUBLIC acknowledgement to flourish, and that misrecognition inflicts real harm.\n• Direction of adaptation — assimilation requires minorities to change; Modood's multicultural citizenship requires the NATIONAL IDENTITY ITSELF to be continually remade to include them.\n• Note INTEGRATION as an intermediate position — full participation in common institutions while retaining some private difference.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires the underlying difference in how diversity is VALUED.` },

      { q: '"Multiculturalism is compatible with liberalism." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `COMPATIBLE:\n• Kymlicka's argument is INTERNAL to liberalism — he begins from individual autonomy, the core liberal value, not from the value of groups. Culture supplies the CONTEXT OF CHOICE, so protecting minority cultures is REQUIRED BY liberalism rather than a concession to it.\n• His EXTERNAL PROTECTIONS / INTERNAL RESTRICTIONS distinction answers the standard objection: liberals can defend a minority against the wider society while refusing to let it coerce its own dissenting members.\n• Both value toleration and diversity; multiculturalism developed largely out of liberal concern for equal treatment.\n• Berlin's value pluralism is itself a liberal position.\n• Recognising that minorities lack the secure cultural context majorities enjoy automatically is an EQUALITY argument.\n\nINCOMPATIBLE:\n• Liberalism locates rights in INDIVIDUALS; conferring them on groups empowers community leaders over dissenting members — the gender critique (Okin) is the sharpest form.\n• Taylor argues liberal NEUTRALITY is illusory and must be abandoned for positive recognition, which challenges a foundational liberal commitment.\n• Parekh's PLURALIST version accords value to non-liberal cultures in their own terms, treating liberalism as one tradition among others — which departs from the liberal framework rather than extending it.\n• Liberalism values the capacity to REVISE inherited values; some practices are premised on transmission rather than examination.\n\nGuidance for L5: the answer depends on WHICH multiculturalism — liberal multiculturalism is compatible, pluralist multiculturalism is post-liberal. Making that distinction explicitly is what reaches L5.` },

      { q: '"Multiculturalism undermines social cohesion." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `UNDERMINES:\n• Conservatives argue a stable society requires shared culture, common values and mutual obligation; endorsing separate identities produces "parallel lives" and communities living alongside one another without meaningful interaction.\n• It may erode the shared identity that sustains willingness to fund a welfare state — a material as well as a symbolic consequence.\n• Essentialism — treating cultures as bounded and homogeneous, and requiring "community representatives", entrenches separateness and privileges conservative voices.\n• Several European states retreated from multicultural policy after the 2000s, citing exactly these concerns.\n• Group rights can create resentment among the majority.\n\nDOES NOT UNDERMINE:\n• The causal claim is contested — ALIENATION results from exclusion and discrimination rather than from recognition, and people whose identity is respected are MORE likely to identify with the wider society.\n• Parekh's DIALOGICAL model addresses the objection directly by constructing shared values through negotiation between communities rather than assuming or imposing them.\n• Modood's multicultural citizenship builds an inclusive national identity continually remade to include minorities.\n• Assimilationist policies have their own record of generating resentment and failure.\n• Cosmopolitan multiculturalism produces hybrid identities that connect rather than separate communities.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest analysis notes the counterfactual problem — cohesion difficulties may be caused by exclusion rather than by multicultural policy, and the evidence cannot easily distinguish the two. Naming that difficulty reaches L5.` },

      { q: '"Group rights are incompatible with individual rights." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `INCOMPATIBLE:\n• Rights properly attach to INDIVIDUALS; conferring them on groups empowers self-appointed community leaders over dissenting members.\n• The GENDER critique (Okin) is the sharpest case — practices subordinating women may be shielded in the name of cultural respect, so protecting the group comes at the expense of its most vulnerable members.\n• Children may be denied education or exit options in the name of cultural transmission.\n• Liberalism values the capacity to REVISE inherited values; group rights can entrench practices premised on transmission rather than examination.\n• Group rights presuppose cultures are homogeneous, which suppresses internal dissent.\n\nCOMPATIBLE:\n• KYMLICKA's argument is decisive here — culture supplies the CONTEXT OF CHOICE within which individuals exercise autonomy, so protecting it SERVES individual rights rather than competing with them.\n• His EXTERNAL PROTECTIONS / INTERNAL RESTRICTIONS distinction directly resolves the objection: shield a minority from the wider society, but refuse to let it coerce its own members.\n• Members of majority cultures enjoy a secure cultural context automatically; group rights correct an INEQUALITY between individuals.\n• Many group rights (language rights, exemptions) impose no constraint on group members at all.\n• Taylor argues recognition is a vital HUMAN need, so denying it harms individuals.\n\nGuidance for L5: Kymlicka's distinction is the strongest available material and should be applied, not merely stated — the objection succeeds against PLURALIST multiculturalism, which declines to judge cultures by liberal standards, far more than against the liberal version.` },

      { q: '"Multiculturalism is a coherent ideology." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `COHERENT:\n• All strands REJECT ASSIMILATION and hold that diversity is valuable rather than merely tolerable.\n• All hold that identity has legitimate PUBLIC standing rather than being a purely private matter.\n• All accept CULTURAL EMBEDDEDNESS — that people are formed within cultures that shape values and meaning.\n• Shared concepts — recognition, minority rights, the critique of difference-blind liberalism — provide a common analytical vocabulary.\n• It offers positions on identity, the state, rights and social organisation, which are the usual tests for an ideology.\n\nNOT COHERENT:\n• The strands pull in INCOMPATIBLE directions — liberal multiculturalism tolerates diversity only within liberal limits, which pluralists call disguised assimilation; pluralist multiculturalism accords value to non-liberal cultures but then struggles to object to practices harming members.\n• COSMOPOLITAN multiculturalism treats cultures as optional resources for individuals, which DISSOLVES the group dimension that the politics of recognition existed to address — so it is arguably not multiculturalism at all.\n• It lacks a distinctive account of the economy, human nature or the state comparable to liberalism or socialism.\n• It may be better understood as a corrective WITHIN other ideologies than as a free-standing one.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest conclusion notes the parallel with NATIONALISM and ECOLOGISM — a distinctive set of premises that must combine with a prior political theory to generate a programme. That comparative move reaches L5.` },
    ]
  },

  '3.7': {
    name: 'Anarchism',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three reasons why anarchists reject the state.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• COERCIVE — the state rests ultimately on force, compelling obedience regardless of individual consent, which anarchists regard as an inherent violation of liberty.\n• SOVEREIGN — it claims supreme authority over everyone within its territory, and anarchists deny any such authority can be legitimate.\n• CORRUPTING — power corrupts those who exercise it, so even well-intentioned rulers become oppressive. Analysis: this is why anarchists reject CAPTURING the state as a route to liberation, and is the basis of Bakunin's dispute with Marx.\n• UNNECESSARY — human beings are capable of voluntary cooperation, so social order does not require compulsory authority. Kropotkin's mutual aid provides the empirical support.\n\nAnalysis expected: the corrupting objection distinguishes anarchism from Marxism, since it denies a workers' state would wither away — a prediction substantially vindicated by twentieth-century history.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three differences between collectivist and individualist anarchism.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• INTELLECTUAL ORIGIN — collectivist anarchism derives from SOCIALISM (Bakunin, Kropotkin, Goldman); individualist anarchism from the premise of the sovereign individual — drawing on LIBERALISM in the case of anarcho-capitalism, though Stirner's egoism rejected liberalism as well.\n• HUMAN NATURE — collectivists emphasise sociability, cooperation and fraternity; individualists the self-interested, self-creating individual who owes nothing to others.\n• PROPERTY — collectivists favour common ownership or possession by those who work it; anarcho-capitalists private property and voluntary exchange, while Stirner's egoism recognises no property rights at all, only what the ego can take and hold.\n• ORGANISATION — communes, cooperatives, revolutionary trade unions and federations versus voluntary contract between individuals.\n\nAnalysis expected: they share ONLY opposition to the state; on property, economics and human nature they are close to opposites. This exposes a fundamental ambiguity — does anarchism oppose the STATE specifically, or HIERARCHY AND DOMINATION in general? The two traditions answer differently.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three anarchist views on human nature.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• BROADLY OPTIMISTIC — anarchists reject the conservative view that people are irredeemably flawed and require restraint. If human beings were that bad, entrusting some of them with state power would be the worst possible response.\n• SHAPED BY INSTITUTIONS — the selfishness, competition and violence attributed to human nature are PRODUCED by hierarchical institutions (the state, capitalism, organised religion, private property) rather than being inherent; remove them and cooperative dispositions can be expressed.\n• KROPOTKIN'S MUTUAL AID — voluntary reciprocal cooperation is observable throughout the natural world and human history, so cooperation is at least as natural as competition. Offered as EMPIRICAL support rather than assertion.\n• THE INDIVIDUALIST EXCEPTION — Stirner's egoist recognises no authority above their own will and owes nothing to others, a very different view of human nature reaching the same anti-statist conclusion.\n\nAnalysis expected: the standard criticism — that the optimism is unsupported — is met by the reply that the evidence cited comes from societies already structured by the institutions anarchists would abolish.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three anarchist views on the economy.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• MUTUALISM (Proudhon) — "property is theft" where it permits an owner to live off others' labour, but he DEFENDED possession by those who work the land or tools. An economy of independent producers and small cooperatives exchanging at fair value, with credit supplied at cost through people's banks.\n• ANARCHO-COMMUNISM (Kropotkin) — goes further, rejecting exchange and wages altogether in favour of common ownership with distribution ACCORDING TO NEED, organised through federations of self-governing communes.\n• ANARCHO-SYNDICALISM — revolutionary trade unions as both the means of overthrowing capitalism (the general strike) and the structure of the future society, with industry run by the workers.\n• ANARCHO-CAPITALISM — the outlier: unrestricted markets and private property, with protection and law supplied by competing private agencies. Classical anarchists reject it as not anarchist at all, since it abolishes public authority while preserving concentrated private power.\n\nGuidance: the common thread is that economic arrangements should be voluntary, decentralised and federal.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three anarchist thinkers\' contributions to the ideology.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• PROUDHON — the first to call himself an anarchist; "property is theft" where it permits living off others' labour; mutualism, people's banks and bottom-up federalism.\n• BAKUNIN — collectivist anarchism and the decisive dispute with Marx: ANY state, including a workers' state, would develop its own interests and become a new apparatus of domination rather than withering away. Also "propaganda of the deed" and the argument that MEANS SHAPE ENDS, so a hierarchical movement cannot produce a free society.\n• KROPOTKIN — Mutual Aid: cooperation is a major factor in evolutionary success, providing empirical support for anarchist optimism; anarcho-communism with distribution according to need.\n• GOLDMAN — extended anti-authoritarianism to PERSONAL LIFE: marriage, sexual morality and gender expectations are forms of authority; formal political rights alone cannot emancipate women.\n• STIRNER — egoism; the state, religion and morality are "spooks" constraining the self.\n\nGuidance: credit the tensions between them.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three differences between anarchism and Marxism.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• THE TRANSITIONAL STATE — Marx held that a dictatorship of the proletariat would be needed to suppress counter-revolution, after which the state would wither away. Bakunin rejected this absolutely, arguing any state would develop its own interests and become a NEW apparatus of domination. Analysis: substantially vindicated by twentieth-century communist states, and the single strongest historical support for any anarchist argument.\n• THE NATURE OF THE STATE — Marxists see it as an instrument of a particular CLASS; anarchists as oppressive BY ITS VERY NATURE, so it cannot be captured and used before withering away.\n• REVOLUTIONARY AGENCY — Marx looked to the industrial proletariat disciplined by factory production; Bakunin also to the peasantry and the dispossessed, and was suspicious of the socialist intellectuals who would staff a new state.\n• ORGANISATION — Marxists accept centralised revolutionary organisation; Bakunin held that MEANS SHAPE ENDS, so a hierarchical movement cannot produce a free society.\n\nShared ground: both oppose capitalism and private ownership of the means of production.` },

      { q: '"Anarchism is utopian." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `UTOPIAN:\n• Its view of HUMAN NATURE is unsupported — without enforceable rules, disputes would be settled by whoever is strongest, so abolishing the state risks replacing public authority with private domination rather than harmony.\n• NO MECHANISM — anarchism is vague about dispute resolution, large-scale infrastructure planning, and dealing with a minority that refuses to cooperate. The problems government exists to solve do not disappear when government does.\n• THE RECORD — no anarchist society has been sustained at scale; revolutionary Catalonia and Ukraine were destroyed.\n• Its rejection of hierarchy makes sustained large-scale organisation difficult.\n• The claim that observed selfishness is produced by existing institutions risks being UNFALSIFIABLE, since any evidence of conflict can be attributed to structures not yet abolished.\n\nNOT UTOPIAN:\n• KROPOTKIN'S MUTUAL AID provides genuine empirical evidence that cooperation is widespread in nature and human society.\n• Small-scale cooperative and mutualist organisation demonstrably works.\n• Catalonia and Ukraine were destroyed by STATES, not by internal collapse — which partly proves the anarchist point about state power.\n• Anarchist critiques have been VINDICATED — Bakunin's prediction about the workers' state was borne out by twentieth-century history.\n• Demanding a detailed blueprint imports statist assumptions about how societies must be organised.\n\nGuidance for L5: the most sophisticated version of the charge is STRUCTURAL rather than moral — anarchism's rejection of hierarchy and of capturing state power deprives it of the instruments by which movements consolidate change, so it is systematically disadvantaged against opponents who use both. That is not a refutation of its principles but describes a movement whose means are constrained by its ends.` },

      { q: '"Anarchists disagree more than they agree." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `DISAGREE:\n• PROPERTY — Kropotkin's common ownership with distribution by need versus Stirner's egoism and anarcho-capitalism's private property and unrestricted markets. These are opposites.\n• HUMAN NATURE — Kropotkin's naturally cooperative human being versus Stirner's self-interested, self-creating ego owing nothing to others.\n• INTELLECTUAL ORIGIN — collectivist anarchism derives from socialism, individualist anarchism from liberalism, so they share no theoretical foundation.\n• EXCHANGE — Proudhon's fair exchange between independent producers versus Kropotkin's abolition of exchange and wages altogether.\n• METHOD — Bakunin's revolution and propaganda of the deed versus Proudhon's gradual construction of alternative institutions.\n\nAGREE:\n• ALL reject the state as coercive, sovereign, corrupting and unnecessary.\n• Collectivist anarchists reject all hierarchy, but anarcho-capitalists accept authority arising from property and contract — so agreement beyond opposition to the state is incomplete.\n• All are ANTI-CLERICAL, seeing organised religion as teaching submission and reinforcing hierarchy.\n• All believe order can emerge from VOLUNTARY cooperation without compulsion.\n• Most anarchists reject electoral participation as legitimising the state, holding that it cannot be used to abolish itself (Proudhon's brief spell in the 1848 Constituent Assembly being a notable exception).\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest conclusion is that anarchism contains TWO IRRECONCILABLE TRADITIONS sharing only opposition to the state — and that this exposes a deeper ambiguity about whether anarchism opposes the STATE specifically or DOMINATION in general. The anarcho-capitalism dispute is the sharpest test of that ambiguity.` },

      { q: '"Anarcho-capitalism is not genuinely anarchist." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `NOT GENUINELY ANARCHIST:\n• Classical anarchists argue concentrated PRIVATE economic power is itself a form of domination — Proudhon's "property is theft" where it permits living off others' labour.\n• Private protection agencies holding a monopoly of force in a territory would RESEMBLE STATES in all but name, so it abolishes the state in name only.\n• It preserves precisely the hierarchy — of employer over worker, owner over non-owner — that anarchism exists to oppose.\n• It derives from liberalism rather than from the socialist tradition that produced most anarchist thought.\n• Kropotkin, Bakunin and Goldman all regarded capitalism as a system of domination requiring abolition.\n\nGENUINELY ANARCHIST:\n• It opposes the state outright, which IS the defining anarchist commitment.\n• It is internally consistent — voluntary exchange and contract with no compulsory authority.\n• INDIVIDUALIST anarchism has always been part of the tradition; Stirner's egoism is equally distant from Kropotkin's communism, yet Stirner is a named anarchist thinker.\n• All arrangements would be voluntary, which is the anarchist test of legitimacy.\n• Excluding it requires defining anarchism by its ECONOMICS rather than by its anti-statism, which is contestable.\n\nGuidance for L5: this question forces the underlying definitional issue — is anarchism defined by opposition to the STATE or to HIERARCHY AND DOMINATION generally? An answer that identifies and resolves that question reaches L5; one that simply asserts a conclusion caps at L3.` },

      { q: '"Anarchists agree about human nature." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `AGREE:\n• All are broadly OPTIMISTIC — all reject the conservative view that people are irredeemably flawed and require restraint, since that would make entrusting some with state power the worst possible response.\n• All hold that human beings are capable of ordering their affairs WITHOUT compulsory authority.\n• All hold that observed selfishness and violence are PRODUCED by hierarchical institutions rather than inherent — the state, capitalism, religion and property teach domination and obedience.\n• All believe people can live cooperatively once those institutions are removed.\n\nDISAGREE:\n• KROPOTKIN'S human being is naturally SOCIAL and cooperative, with mutual aid as an evolutionary fact; STIRNER'S is a self-interested, self-creating EGO who recognises no authority and owes nothing to others. These are close to opposite anthropologies.\n• Collectivists treat people as fundamentally communal, deriving fulfilment from association; individualists treat association as instrumental — a "union of egoists" entered for mutual advantage and dissolvable at will.\n• Anarcho-capitalists assume rational self-interested actors of the kind classical economics posits, not Kropotkin's cooperators.\n• Whether human beings need community or merely tolerate it divides the traditions entirely.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest conclusion is that anarchists agree on the NEGATIVE claim (people do not need rulers) and disagree fundamentally on the POSITIVE one (what people are actually like) — and that the shared conclusion is reached from incompatible premises.` },
    ]
  },

  '3.8': {
    name: 'Ecologism',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain and analyse three core ideas of ecologism.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• HOLISM — the whole is more than the sum of its parts, and entities can only be understood through their relationships within a wider system. Analysis: scientifically it reflects ecological interdependence (Carson's Silent Spring); philosophically it challenges liberal individualism and the mechanistic worldview.\n• ECOCENTRISM — the ecosystem as a whole has INTRINSIC value independent of human use, so human interests should not automatically take priority. Analysis: the principal fault line, since it determines whether nature is protected for our sake or its own.\n• SUSTAINABILITY — meeting present needs without compromising future generations. Analysis: makes the interests of people who do not yet exist a political consideration, which no other ideology accommodates naturally.\n• ENVIRONMENTAL ETHICS — extending moral consideration beyond humans, to sentient animals, all living things, or (Leopold) the biotic community as a whole.\n• Critique of MATERIALISM, consumerism and growth.\n\nGuidance: L3 requires analysis of consequences.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three differences between ecologism and environmentalism.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• VALUE OF NATURE — environmentalism is ANTHROPOCENTRIC, valuing nature instrumentally because degradation harms human beings; ecologism is ECOCENTRIC, holding nature has intrinsic value independent of human use.\n• APPROACH — environmentalism is REFORMIST, addressing problems through regulation, technology, conservation and sustainable growth within existing structures; ecologism requires FUNDAMENTAL RESTRUCTURING of society, economy and values.\n• VIEW OF INDUSTRIALISM — environmentalism treats environmental harm as manageable with the right policies; ecologism holds industrialism and the commitment to growth are THEMSELVES the problem.\n• PRACTICAL IMPLICATIONS — shallow ecology can endorse nuclear power, carbon trading and technological solutions permitting continued growth; deep ecology rejects growth as incompatible with a finite planet.\n\nAnalysis expected: critics argue shallow ecology addresses symptoms while leaving the underlying commitment to growth untouched; but deep ecology offers no clear basis for resolving conflicts between human welfare and ecological preservation.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ecologist views on the economy.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• LIMITS TO GROWTH — indefinite exponential growth cannot continue on a finite planet with limited resources and limited capacity to absorb waste.\n• SCHUMACHER'S CRITIQUE — conventional economics treats natural resources as INCOME rather than as irreplaceable CAPITAL, so depleting a forest or fishery registers as profit rather than as liquidating an asset. GDP counts activity regardless of whether it creates or destroys value.\n• HUMAN-SCALE ECONOMICS — smaller, decentralised production units serving human needs; endorsing Gandhi's call for "production by the masses, not mass production"; intermediate or appropriate technology suited to local conditions.\n• ECO-SOCIALISM — growth is not a policy choice but capitalism's STRUCTURAL REQUIREMENT, since firms that do not expand are eliminated by competition, so the problem cannot be solved within capitalism.\n• POST-MATERIALISM — beyond the point at which basic needs are met, further consumption yields little additional wellbeing.\n\nCounter-argument worth crediting: growth funds the technology and environmental protection poorer societies need, and decoupling growth from emissions may be achievable.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three ecologist thinkers\' contributions to the ideology.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• LEOPOLD — the LAND ETHIC: the moral community should be extended to include soils, waters, plants and animals. An action is right when it preserves the integrity, stability and beauty of the biotic community. Analysis: HOLISTIC rather than individualistic — moral standing attaches to the community, which distinguishes ecologism from animal rights philosophy.\n• CARSON — Silent Spring documented how DDT accumulated through food chains, demonstrating INTERCONNECTEDNESS concretely and launching the modern environmental movement.\n• SCHUMACHER — natural resources are irreplaceable CAPITAL, not income; human-scale, decentralised economics and intermediate technology.\n• MERCHANT — ECOFEMINISM: the mechanistic worldview that replaced an organic conception of nature legitimised its exploitation, and the same patriarchal dualisms devalue both women and nature.\n• BOOKCHIN — SOCIAL ECOLOGY: the ecological crisis originates in hierarchical social structures; the domination of nature extends the domination of humans by humans.\n\nGuidance: credit the tensions between them, particularly Bookchin against deep ecology.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three strands of ecologism.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• DEEP ECOLOGY — ecocentric; nature has intrinsic value and humanity is one part of the biotic community. Requires a wholly new ethic and a radical reduction in human impact and consumption.\n• SHALLOW ECOLOGY — anthropocentric; protects the environment for human welfare through regulation, technology and sustainable growth within existing structures.\n• SOCIAL ECOLOGY / ECO-ANARCHISM (Bookchin) — the crisis originates in HIERARCHY and domination; the prescription is decentralised, directly democratic, federated municipalities with production for need.\n• ECO-SOCIALISM — the crisis originates in capitalism's structural requirement for continuous accumulation; replace capitalist production with production for need.\n• ECOFEMINISM (Merchant) — patriarchal dualisms devaluing both women and nature; dismantle them and revalue nurture and interdependence.\n\nAnalysis expected: most strands ATTACH ecological premises to a PRIOR analysis of domination, which is the strongest objection to ecologism's independence as an ideology.` },

      { q: 'Explain and analyse three criticisms of deep ecology.', marks: 9, command: 'Explain and analyse',
        markScheme: `Indicative content — any three:\n• NO BASIS FOR RESOLVING CONFLICTS — by denying that human interests take priority, deep ecology provides no principled way to adjudicate between human welfare and ecosystem preservation.\n• MISANTHROPY — attributing the crisis to humanity in general, and particularly to population, treats humanity as a disease rather than as an agent of solutions.\n• BOOKCHIN'S CRITIQUE — blaming "humanity" obscures the actual cause in hierarchical social structures, and cannot identify WHO BENEFITS from ecological destruction or WHO SUFFERS (the poor suffer most). A movement that blames humanity as such has no political programme.\n• PRACTICAL WEAKNESS — it offers no realistic route from present arrangements to the society it envisages, and the radical reduction in consumption it requires has no evident constituency.\n• Its ethical claims about intrinsic value are philosophically contested and difficult to ground.\n\nGuidance: one-sided by design; require analysis rather than assertion.` },

      { q: '"Ecologism is a distinct ideology." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `DISTINCT:\n• It offers a COMPLETE WORLDVIEW — an account of humanity's place in nature, a critique of industrialism, materialism and growth, and a prescription for restructuring economy and society.\n• HOLISM and ECOCENTRISM genuinely BREAK with the anthropocentric individualism shared by liberalism, conservatism and socialism alike. No other ideology questions whether human beings should be the measure of political value.\n• It generates positions no other tradition reaches — rejecting growth as an objective, and treating the interests of future generations and other species as political considerations.\n• Schumacher's reconception of natural resources as capital rather than income reconceives economics rather than adding a constraint to it.\n\nNOT DISTINCT:\n• MOST of ecologism ATTACHES to other ideologies — eco-socialism to a Marxist critique of capitalism, eco-anarchism to hierarchy, ecofeminism to patriarchy. Each combines ecological premises with a PRIOR analysis of domination.\n• Only DEEP ECOLOGY makes a genuinely free-standing claim, and it is the strand with the LEAST developed political programme.\n• Each strand's politics comes from its prior analysis, not from its ecology.\n• It lacks a distinctive account of human nature comparable to the major ideologies.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest conclusion draws the parallel with NATIONALISM — a distinctive set of premises that must combine with a social analysis to generate politics, rather than a free-standing tradition. That comparative move reaches L5.` },

      { q: '"Ecologists disagree about the causes of environmental crisis." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `DISAGREE:\n• DEEP ECOLOGY blames ANTHROPOCENTRISM itself — the belief that nature exists for human use — and often human population.\n• BOOKCHIN'S SOCIAL ECOLOGY blames HIERARCHY — the domination of nature extends the domination of humans by other humans — and explicitly rejects deep ecology's attribution to humanity in general as politically useless.\n• ECO-SOCIALISM blames CAPITALISM's structural requirement for continuous accumulation, since firms that do not expand are eliminated.\n• ECOFEMINISM (Merchant) blames PATRIARCHAL DUALISMS and the mechanistic worldview that replaced an organic conception of nature.\n• SHALLOW ECOLOGY blames specific policy failures and treats the crisis as manageable.\n\nAGREE:\n• All accept that a crisis EXISTS and is human-caused.\n• All accept HOLISM and ecological interdependence — that intervening in one element has consequences throughout.\n• All reject unlimited consumption and the assumption that more material acquisition means a better life.\n• All accept SUSTAINABILITY as a constraint, and the finitude of the planet.\n• All radical (dark-green) strands identify INDUSTRIALISM and modern economic organisation as central to the problem; shallow ecology treats them as manageable.\n\nGuidance for L5: the disagreement about CAUSES is severe and generates incompatible prescriptions — but it follows from each strand attaching ecological premises to a prior analysis of domination, which is itself evidence about ecologism's status as an ideology. Linking the two questions reaches L5.` },

      { q: '"Economic growth is incompatible with environmental protection." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `INCOMPATIBLE:\n• Indefinite EXPONENTIAL GROWTH cannot continue on a finite planet with limited resources and limited capacity to absorb waste.\n• Schumacher — conventional economics treats natural resources as INCOME rather than irreplaceable CAPITAL, so depletion registers as profit; GDP counts activity regardless of whether it creates or destroys value.\n• ECO-SOCIALISTS argue growth is not a policy choice but capitalism's structural requirement, so it cannot be abandoned within capitalism.\n• Beyond the point at which basic needs are met, further consumption yields little additional wellbeing (post-materialism), so the growth is not even serving its purpose.\n• Historical evidence associates growth with rising emissions and resource depletion.\n\nCOMPATIBLE:\n• Growth funds the TECHNOLOGY and environmental protection that poorer societies need; environmental quality has improved in many wealthy countries.\n• DECOUPLING — some economies have grown while reducing territorial emissions, suggesting the link is not necessary.\n• The target should be the ENVIRONMENTAL IMPACT of growth rather than growth itself; green growth and circular economy models attempt exactly this.\n• Degrowth would impose severe costs on the poorest, both within and between countries, and has no evident political constituency.\n• Innovation driven by growth may deliver the solutions.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest analysis interrogates DECOUPLING — whether it is absolute or relative, and whether it survives accounting for emissions embedded in imports. That empirical scrutiny reaches L5; asserting either position does not.` },

      { q: '"Ecologism requires a fundamental change in human values." Analyse and evaluate this statement.', marks: 25, command: 'Analyse and evaluate',
        markScheme: `REQUIRES VALUE CHANGE:\n• DEEP ECOLOGY holds the crisis originates in ANTHROPOCENTRISM itself, so nothing less than a new ecocentric ethic will suffice — Leopold's land ethic extends the moral community to soils, waters, plants and animals.\n• The critique of MATERIALISM and consumerism is a critique of what people value, not merely of how they produce.\n• HOLISM challenges the individualism underpinning liberal societies.\n• MERCHANT argues the mechanistic worldview that replaced an organic conception of nature removed the ethical restraints on exploitation, so restoring restraint requires reversing that conceptual shift.\n• Sustainability requires valuing the interests of future generations who cannot vote or bargain.\n\nSTRUCTURAL CHANGE MATTERS MORE:\n• BOOKCHIN argues the crisis originates in HIERARCHY, so changing values without dismantling hierarchical structures achieves nothing — and blaming humanity's values obscures who actually profits from destruction.\n• ECO-SOCIALISTS argue capitalism's structural requirement for accumulation operates regardless of individual values; firms that do not expand are eliminated whatever their owners believe.\n• SHALLOW ECOLOGY shows substantial gains are achievable through regulation and technology without any value transformation.\n• Value change is slow, unreliable and hard to engineer, whereas institutional change can be legislated.\n• Individuals with green values are constrained by the systems they live within.\n\nGuidance for L5: the strongest conclusion notes this reproduces ecologism's central internal division — whether the problem lies in VALUES or in INSTITUTIONS — and that the answer determines whether ecologism is a free-standing ideology or a set of premises attaching to a prior social analysis.` },
    ]
  },

};
